\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  Rules  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Clinics Can Challenge Texas Law; State law remains in effect for now; Texas has banned procedure after about six weeks of pregnancy\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brent Kendall and Jess Bravin \par \uc2 1,511 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 December 2021\par 22:28 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14The \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  on Friday gave \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers a narrow legal path to challenge Texas' ban on ending pregnancies after six weeks, but the splintered ruling {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-in-court-what-you-need-to-know-11631730720"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 left the law in effect for now  }}}\uc2  and made any rapid resumption of such abortions in the state unlikely.\par \par \uc2 While most justices found common ground on allowing the providers to challenge the law in a limited way, they split sharply on whether a broad set of Texas officials could be sued\u8212\'20\'14an important procedural question that could affect the future of the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions. A slim conservative majority said they couldn't be, prompting spirited exchanges among the justices about whether the court was retreating from its role as the protector of constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 Eight justices agreed the head of the state medical board and other licensing authorities could be sued before the law was enforced to test its constitutionality, despite Texas' efforts to insulate the law from federal court review by assigning enforcement power to private litigants who can win monetary awards for successful suits.\par \par \uc2 But the conservative majority said other state officials, including the attorney general and Texas court clerks, gatekeepers for the private litigation that enforces the ban, cannot be sued.\par \par \uc2 Taken together, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://s.wsj.net/public/resources/documents/21-4633ebh.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 court's holdings  }}}\uc2  are something of a setback for Texas, but they potentially leave room for the state\u8212\'20\'14or other states\u8212\'20\'14to try to pass new laws that seek to undercut \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights or other types of constitutional protections. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, meanwhile, said the decision doesn't necessarily leave them enough room to win a ruling in federal district court that would effectively block the state's ban, which has been in effect for more than three months.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  separately declined to issue a ruling in a related challenge to the Texas law, which is known as SB 8, brought by the Justice Department, and it denied the department's emergency request to block the law immediately.\par \par \uc2 The law is the toughest in the nation, banning abortions after about six weeks' of pregnancy, far earlier than current \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  precedent allows. It bars doctors from knowingly performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if there is a "detectable fetal heartbeat," which the law defines to include early cardiac activity in the embryo, and doesn't contain exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 The impact of Friday's ruling on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access could be limited because the high court, in another pending case, is broadly revisiting the propriety of constitutional protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-hears-arguments-in-landmark-mississippi-abortion-case-11638354602"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 In that litigation, from Mississippi  }}}\uc2 , the justices are weighing whether to restrict or withdraw the right to end an unwanted pregnancy which the court recognized in the 1973 case, Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 "I think all eyes are upon the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  's decision in the Mississippi case," said Julia Kaye, an attorney with the American Civil Liberties Union Reproductive Freedom Project.\par \par \uc2 John Seago, legislative director for Texas Right to Life and a backer of SB 8, described the high court's decision as a partial victory.\par \par \uc2 "It's going to be very difficult for a broad injunction to be granted from the district court. Now that the case only deals with state agencies, an injunction would just apply to them," Mr. Seago said. "We're very optimistic that the law will continue to be enforced until the future when maybe the case is back up before the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  ."\par \par \uc2 Those on the other side didn't disagree.\par \par \uc2 "It's stunning that the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  has essentially said that federal courts cannot stop this bounty-hunter scheme enacted to blatantly deny Texans their constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2  , president of the Center of \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , an advocacy group representing the providers. "For 100 days now, this six-week ban has been in effect, and today's ruling means there is no end in sight."\par \par \uc2 Friday's decision saw the justices divide into several camps.\par \par \uc2 Conservatives, led by Justice \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  , said no matter the nature of the Texas ban, also known as the Texas Heartbeat Act, there were deep legal problems with allowing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights challengers to sue a host of state government actors.\par \par \uc2 He and Justices \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  , \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  said only a narrow group of officials could be sued. Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  said he would have thrown out the providers' claims entirely.\par \par \uc2 Typically, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers have challenged restrictions when enacted by suing state officials charged with their enforcement. In a bid to neutralize this tactic, Texas assigned enforcement authority to private civil litigants instead, who can win awards of at least $10,000 if they prevail in court.\par \par \uc2 The providers therefore sued several types of officials they argued would nonetheless be involved in enforcement, including state court clerks who would docket private lawsuits under SB 8.\par \par \uc2 But Justice Gorsuch said if "federal judges could enjoin state courts and clerks from entertaining disputes between private parties under this state law, what would stop federal judges from prohibiting state courts and clerks from hearing and docketing disputes between private parties under other state laws?"\par \par \uc2 Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  and the court's three liberal justices said the court erred badly by not allowing such clerks and other officials to be sued, and they warned the limited ruling could inflict long-term damage on the court's institutional role and the protection of constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 "Texas has employed an array of stratagems designed to shield its unconstitutional law from judicial review," the chief justice wrote. If state legislatures can employ such gambits to eviscerate federal rights, "the Constitution itself becomes a solemn mockery," he wrote, quoting from an 1809 ruling.\par \par \uc2 "The nature of the federal right infringed does not matter; it is the role of the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  in our constitutional system that is at stake," he added.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  , writing for the court's three liberals, criticized the majority for permitting the Texas law to stand for months "in open defiance of this Court's precedents."\par \par \uc2 "The Court should have put an end to this madness months ago, before S.B. 8 first went into effect," she wrote, joined by Justices \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  and \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2  . She wrote that the majority's reasoning still left room for creative state lawmakers to deny a host of constitutional rights by using the Gorsuch opinion as a roadmap "to more thoroughly disclaim all enforcement by state officials, including licensing officials."\par \par \uc2 Justice Gorsuch rejected such worries, writing that other avenues remained to assert federal constitutional rights, including pre-enforcement challenges that could be filed in state courts. He noted that on Thursday, a state judge ruled parts of the law's enforcement structure were unconstitutional and shouldn't be enforced in Texas courts.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  's ruling comes more than five weeks after it heard expedited oral arguments in a pair of cases in which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and the Justice Department {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/justice-department-to-ask-supreme-court-to-block-texas-abortion-law-11634321002"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sued to block SB 8  }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 When the law went into effect on Sept. 1, the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  , on a 5-4 vote, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-rejects-emergency-request-to-block-texas-six-week-abortion-ban-11630556283"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 declined to block it at that time  }}}\uc2 , with the court's most conservative justices saying there were procedural complexities that prevented them from intervening. Weeks later, the court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-agrees-to-quick-consideration-of-texas-abortion-case-leaves-state-law-in-place-for-now-11634921299"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 moved with unusual speed  }}}\uc2  to consider the two legal challenges and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-hears-arguments-on-texas-abortion-law-11635765801"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 held fast-track oral arguments  }}}\uc2  on Nov. 1.\par \par \uc2 After the clinics ran into early court roadblocks, the Justice Department stepped in and filed a lawsuit against Texas, arguing the state was engaged in an unlawful scheme to deny citizens their constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 The department said Friday it would "continue our efforts in the lower courts to protect the rights of women and uphold the Constitution."\par \par \uc2 Since the law took effect, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-gives-a-glimpse-of-a-post-roe-world-11635942617"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 number of abortions performed at Texas clinics has dropped  }}}\uc2  and women have traveled across state lines to providers able perform procedures later in pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 State clinics say the law has created an ethical dilemma for them as they turn away patients, some of whom are unable to travel elsewhere. They also warn of the ripple effects of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/illinois-abortion-clinics-are-preparing-for-a-potential-post-roe-world-11638419374"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 patients traveling out of state  }}}\uc2 , creating higher demand at clinics in other states and longer waits for the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Under current high court precedent, women have a constitutional right to terminate their pregnancies before fetal viability, considered to be somewhere around 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 In the Mississippi case, the state is seeking to impose a ban after 15 weeks and argues the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights framework dating back to the 1973 Roe v. Wade decision is incorrect and should be abandoned.\par \par \uc2 The court heard arguments in that litigation last week, with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-hears-arguments-in-landmark-mississippi-abortion-case-11638354602"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 conservative justices sharply questioning  }}}\uc2  the propriety of constitutional protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Calfas contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Brent Kendall at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:brent.kendall@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 brent.kendall@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Jess Bravin at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jess.bravin+1@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jess.bravin+1@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Jess Bravin at\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-rules-abortion-clinics-can-challenge-texas-abortion-law-11639149404"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court Rules \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Clinics Can Challenge Texas Law  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020211210ehca002xl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
