\par\par \uc2 Review & Outlook (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The Supreme Court's  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Standing; The merits of the Texas law weren't at issue, and the majority is right to block most pre-enforcement federal lawsuits.\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 558 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 December 2021\par 23:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court ruled Friday in a case involving S.B.8, the Texas law that bans abortions at around six weeks. But despite all the alarms, this opinion had nothing to do with the merits of the law or the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The issue was legal standing. Credit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/21pdf/21-463_3ebh.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the majority opinion}}}\uc2  by Justice \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  for refusing to rip up old doctrines to create some for-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -only exception.\par \par \uc2 The key question: Who can sue whom? S.B.8 was written to be difficult to block before its enforcement, because state officials were meant to play no role. Instead the law authorizes private parties to bring lawsuits. So until somebody tries to enforce S.B.8, where can \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers go to seek relief? The Court unanimously agreed Friday in Whole Woman's Health v. Jackson that they cannot pre-emptively bring a lawsuit against a citizen who swore he had no plans to try to enforce S.B.8.\par \par \uc2 The Justices also agreed unanimously that state judges are not proper defendants. What about state court clerks? Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , joined by the Supreme Court's three liberals, argues in an opinion both concurring and dissenting that clerks should be sue-able, because if they docketed S.B.8 cases they'd be "unavoidably enlisted" in the law's "scheme." The Chief did not persuade a majority of his colleagues.\par \par \uc2 Federal courts, as Justice Gorsuch says, only resolve "actual controversies" between "adverse litigants." Court clerks are neutral functionaries. If they can be sued to stop them from docketing S.B.8 cases, would clerks "assemble a blacklist of banned claims"? How would that square with due process? Would states need to hire independent counsel for clerks? "Many more questions than answers would present themselves," Justice Gorsuch says, "if the Court journeyed this way."\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court did not block \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers from suing Texas health regulators. Justice Gorsuch's explanation is that these officials are authorized to take action against medical professionals who breach the state's health code, of which S.B.8 is a part. So that piece of the federal lawsuit can proceed, although one possibility is that a lower court will now ask the state judiciary to opine on whether this is a correct reading of Texas law.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 , dissenting in part for the three liberals, is at her usual pitch. "By foreclosing suit against state-court officials and the state attorney general, the Court effectively invites other States to refine S.B.8's model for nullifying federal rights. The Court thus betrays not only the citizens of Texas, but also our constitutional system of government."\par \par \uc2 Yet as Justice Gorsuch explains, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers can also ask for relief in state court, where he says 14 cases "already seek to vindicate both federal and state constitutional claims." Or Congress can give the federal judiciary "more tools" to "combat this type of law." The mistake would have been if the Supreme Court had given into the political pressure and carved up its standing doctrines, with who knows what long-term consequences.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/the-supreme-courts-abortion-standing-texas-law-neil-gorsuch-11639175695"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Supreme Court's \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Standing}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020211210ehca0080x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
