\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood has built a new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic and health center in Flossmoor, Ill., near the Indiana border. PHOTO: Beth Rooney for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 Illinois clinic directors, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates and groups that fund \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related costs say they have been anticipating the possibility of a post-Roe America for several years, as more GOP-led states across the U.S. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/states-are-shifting-farther-apart-on-abortion-11549656745"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have added restrictions }}}\uc2 . More out-of-state patients have been coming to Illinois, with 5,668 nonresidents receiving abortions there in 2018, compared with 2,970 in 2014, according to the most recent data from the state Department of Public Health.\par \par \uc2 Providers have strategically opened new locations, increased staffing and expanded hours in the past several years. They are now coordinating with colleagues across the country to ensure they could accommodate more patients if \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is outlawed elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 "All of our building in the last five years has been with the possibility of a loss of Roe in mind," said Jennifer Welch, president and chief executive of Planned Parenthood of Illinois.\par \par \uc2 The organization built two new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics and health centers in Flossmoor and Waukegan, near the Indiana and Wisconsin borders, respectively. Planned Parenthood of the St. Louis Region and Southwest Missouri opened another in Fairview Heights, Ill., near the Missouri border. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich426\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6390
