\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  assistants talked in the hall during a break at the clinic in Flossmoor on Monday. PHOTO: Beth Rooney for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood of Illinois said it is preparing to serve up to five times as many patients, depending on where patients choose to travel if federal protections are weakened.\par \par \uc2 Antiabortion groups, meanwhile, are working to build more crisis pregnancy resource centers in the Chicago area, said Amy Gehrke, executive director of Illinois Right to Life, an antiabortion organization focused on educational outreach.\par \par \uc2 "Illinois is pretty much an existential threat to the pro-life laws of all of our neighboring states," said Ms. Gehrke.\par \par \uc2 The group has seen increased interest. On Nov. 12, nearly 500 people attended one of its annual fundraising events for Illinois Right to Life Action, the organization's lobbying arm, Ms. Gehrke said. Around 200 to 300 attendees came in previous years. They doubled the amount raised from the event, compared with the last one, held in 2019, she added. She declined to disclose the amount raised.\par \par \uc2 Members of the Coalition for Life St. Louis, a Christian-based organization in Missouri, started traveling to Planned Parenthood's clinic in Flossmoor in November to try to convince women to not have abortions, said Brian Westbrook, the organization's director. He said the group was able to dissuade five women from going through with their appointments at the time.\par \par \uc2 "We see opportunities in the state of Illinois to save even more lives," said Mr. Westbrook. He said the group has been building connections with antiabortion groups in other states for years, in anticipation of the potential weakening of federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections.\par \par \uc2 Should Roe be overturned, it is possible about half of U.S. states could further restrict or ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to recent analysis of state laws from the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 . A dozen other states have laws protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights up until fetal viability, a threshold set by the Supreme Court that is around 24 weeks of pregnancy. Vermont, Oregon and Washington, D.C., have protections for abortions in place throughout pregnancy, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Texas {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-gives-a-glimpse-of-a-post-roe-world-11635942617"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has already provided a glimpse }}}\uc2  into a possible post-Roe America after lawmakers there effectively banned abortions after six weeks of pregnancy. The new law, which remains in place as Supreme Court justices consider a legal challenge, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-prompts-women-to-seek-out-of-state-clinics-11632315616"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has driven some women out of state }}}\uc2  for the procedure, according to clinics, including those in Illinois. The number of abortions performed in Texas dropped by half after the ban took effect, several studies have found.\par \par \uc2 The Chicago \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Fund, which helps finance \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedures and the logistics associated with them, has received 3,300 requests for assistance so far this year, said executive director Megan Jeyifo. While the majority of callers are from the Midwest, Ms. Jeyifo said she is starting to become more familiar with Texas area codes as more callers from there seek grants, which average between $200 and $240. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw426\pich640\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6390\pichgoal9600
