\par \par \uc2 Dr. Erin King says the Granite City clinic, which is near the Missouri border, saw a jump in patient volume when that state put new restrictions in place. PHOTO: Whitney Curtis for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 The fund is adding 14 new case managers and doubling its number of volunteers, because of the heavy caseload and to bolster infrastructure in anticipation of what may come.\par \par \uc2 "We recognize how critical Illinois is and the kind of mandate that we have," Ms. Jeyifo said.\par \par \uc2 The Hope Clinic for Women, an independent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider in Granite City, Ill., about 4 miles from the Missouri border, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/two-abortion-clinics-20-minutes-and-a-legal-universe-apart-11561023000"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 was underprepared }}}\uc2 when patient volume doubled between 2018 and 2019, said Executive Director Erin King. Many of those patients came from Missouri, where the governor had signed new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions into law and the state's only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic was entangled in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/hearing-to-decide-fate-of-missouris-only-abortion-clinic-11572181200"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a licensing dispute }}}\uc2  in 2019.\par \par \uc2 Hope Clinic has since doubled the number of its doctors to eight from four, increased full-time staff by 30% to 40% and opened its doors for more days. It now operates under a flexible schedule, adding hours or shifts when needed to help accommodate more patients.\par \par \uc2 "We are not just protecting our own residents, we're protecting people across the country," Dr. King said. \par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw640\pich426\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal9600\pichgoal6390
