\par\par \uc2 Commentary (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Good Reasons to Go Slow on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Precedents; Overturning them all at once poses risks to legal and political stability. Gradualism is a better way.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Richard M. Re \par \uc2 887 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 December 2021\par 23:28 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Should the Supreme Court eliminate all constitutional \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights? That question dominated oral argument in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization on Wednesday. Opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have urged the court to overrule almost 50 years of precedents, including Roe v. Wade (1973) and Planned Parenthood v. Casey (1992).\par \par \uc2 Such a sweeping decision would be both precipitous and out of character. The justices typically move gradually before issuing disruptive decisions, and that approach is especially warranted in Dobbs.\par \par \uc2 The case concerns a Mississippi law that prohibits \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the 15th week of pregnancy\u8212\'20\'14a deadline several weeks before viability and so impermissible under existing case law. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers have asked that the court reconfirm all existing precedents on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while Mississippi seeks their complete overruling. But while that all-or-nothing choice may be attractive to partisans in search of decisive victory, justices who think the earlier cases were wrongly decided should be attuned to the value of deliberation and the danger of overconfidence.\par \par \uc2 Gradualism has been a hallmark of the court throughout the tenure of Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 . In cases ranging from campaign-finance regulation to same-sex marriage, the justices have often signaled their interest in transformative rulings years before actually issuing them. Sometimes, the court has changed course or thought better of following through on its apparent plans. And even when the court does proceed, one-last-chance rulings give warning to the public and help blunt the force of what would otherwise be an abrupt shift in the law.\par \par \uc2 The court followed that gradualist approach a few months ago in Fulton v. City of Philadelphia, a case concerning religious liberty. Some justices argued that it was past time to overrule a 1990 precedent that, like Roe, had been the focus of sustained criticism. But even though most justices agreed that the precedent had wrongly curtailed a constitutional right, the court declined to overrule it. As Justice Amy Coney Barrett argued in an opinion joined by Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , the problem was deciding what to do once precedent had been set aside. The court wisely opted for caution today rather than risk embarrassment tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Some commentators have argued that gradualism in Dobbs is impossible. Because the Mississippi law is so squarely contrary to precedent, the argument goes, any half-measure would only end up entrenching a new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctrine or leading to an unworkable morass as courts worked out new legal standards. But the justices can, and often do, reinterpret and revise their past decisions while leaving open the possibility of making additional changes in future cases. Whether a new standard would ultimately prove workable can be tested\u8212\'20\'14by trying it and finding out.\par \par \uc2 On Demand: WSJ Opinion Talk\par \par \uc2 A Momentous Term for the Supreme Court WSJ Opinion's Paul Gigot, Allysia Finley and Kyle Peterson with legal scholar Ilya Shapiro discuss the new Court's first full term. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/live-qa/wsj-opinion-a-momentous-term-for-the-supreme-court/301AD303-A28A-48DD-8CC4-9196CA91EA1E"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Watch Now}}}\par \par \uc2 During oral argument, Chief Justice Roberts suggested eliminating the viability rule while developing other aspects of existing case law, such as the looser rule that laws may not impose an "undue burden" on women seeking abortions. Lawyers on both sides resisted that suggestion. But advocates have to worry about undermining their primary arguments by endorsing compromise. That the parties have both adopted a go-for-broke strategy should tell the court that it is being drawn into partisan conflict.\par \par \uc2 At any rate, other forms of gradualism are available, and some would not require creating new legal rules. The justices could, for example, dismiss the case on the grounds that Mississippi has sharply changed its position. When requesting that the court take the case, the state promised that its arguments "do not require the court to overturn Roe or Casey." Yet in later briefing and at argument, the state focused almost exclusively on the broadest possible way to decide the case. Under the normal standards for granting review, Dobbs never merited the court's attention. The court could find surer footing by awaiting a properly presented dispute.\par \par \uc2 The point is not that precedents are absolute or that overruling them is always wrong. A decision can be worthy of overruling on the day that it is decided. And as several justices suggested, Brown v. Board of Education owed no respect to the Jim Crow South's "reliance interests" in the separate-but-equal doctrine. Yet precedent's flexibility is precisely what makes gradual decision-making both possible and attractive. The justices do their best work when they approach legal issues cautiously. A court that leaps to issue a major decision is not resolving a case but implementing an agenda.\par \par \uc2 Dobbs thus offers a chance for the court to avoid a fundamental defect in Roe itself: the pretension to resolve a complex, difficult issue in one fell swoop. Many critics of earlier \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  decisions recognized the folly of deciding too much, too fast. Justices skeptical of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights should not repeat that mistake.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Re is a law professor at the University of Virginia. He served as a clerk for Judge \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and Justice \uc2 Anthony Kennedy\uc2 .\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-go-slow-roe-v-wade-abortion-precedents-gradualism-mississippi-dobbs-11638475763"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Good Reasons to Go Slow on \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Precedents}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020211202ehc2007vd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
