\par\par \uc2 Commentary (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Does  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Promote Equality for Women? No, says Mississippi's Attorney General Lynn Fitch, as her office defends the state's restrictions.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicole Ault \par \uc2 1,047 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 November 2021\par 22:06 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Here's a measure of how far American women have progressed since 1973, when the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  decided Roe v. Wade: Then, no woman had ever been elected attorney general of a U.S. state, and none would be for another dozen years. Now, Mississippi's attorney general is defending the constitutionality of her state's law banning most abortions after 15 weeks. Earlier this year Lynn Fitch appointed Solicitor General Scott G. Stewart, who on Wednesday will make the state's case to the Supreme Court in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization.\par \par \uc2 "Everything has changed in the last 50 years," says Ms. Fitch, 60, a Republican who was elected in 2019, in an interview. "Our laws are much different. The culture is different today than it was 50 years ago. And certainly the workforce and the workplace has changed." That's part of the reason the justices should uphold Mississippi's law, the state argues.\par \par \uc2 Roe, as Mississippi notes in its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/19/19-1392/184703/20210722161332385_19-1392BriefForPetitioners.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 brief to the court}}}\uc2 , "suggested that, without \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , unwanted children could 'force upon' women 'a distressful life and future.' " Planned Parenthood v. Casey (1992) asserted that the "ability of women to participate equally in the economic and social life of the Nation has been facilitated by their ability to control their reproductive lives." Mississippi disagrees: "Modern options regarding and views about childbearing have dulled concerns on which Roe rested."\par \par \uc2 Ms. Fitch elaborates. "Maternity leave is very commonplace, where it wasn't 50 years ago," she says. Paternity leave "was unheard of 50 years ago." A 2020 \uc2 Society for Human Resource Management\uc2  survey {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.shrm.org/about-shrm/press-room/press-releases/pages/new-shrm-research-shows-employers-offering-paid-leave-has-increased.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 found}}}\uc2  that 55% of employers in the U.S. offer paid maternity leave and 45% offer paid paternity leave.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps most meaningful is a shift in cultural expectations. "If you look back at 1973," Ms. Fitch says, "your professional success was really defined by that office and your job, and that's so different now. Today your success is defined by what your goals are."\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/19/19-1392/185366/20210804180314919_19-1392 Brief of 240 Women Scholars et al In Support of Petitioners.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 friend-of-the-court brief}}}\uc2 , signed by 240 female scholars and professionals and pro-life feminist groups, echoes the point: "Ironically, under the guise of women's rights, equality arguments for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  suggest that females are intrinsically blighted by their reproductive capacity to bear children. These arguments tend, unwittingly perhaps, to promote the male childless norm in educational and employment settings."\par \par \uc2 In its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/19/19-1392/192267/20210913143126849_19-1392bs.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 brief}}}\uc2 , Jackson Women's Health Organization calls that argument "false and paternalistic": "Two generations\u8212\'20\'14spanning almost five decades\u8212\'20\'14have come to depend on the availability of legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the right to make this decision has been further cemented as critical to gender equality." Jackson Women's Health cites women who claim that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  allowed them to pursue their careers. But it's impossible to prove what would have happened in the absence of a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the cultural expectations it engendered.\par \par \uc2 The brief from pro-life feminists notes that women's social progress was already under way in 1973. Congress enacted the Equal Pay Act of 1963, the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and the Equal Employment Opportunity Act before the high court decided Roe. The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate in the U.S. has fallen since the 1990s as women's earnings relative to men's increased, the number of women-owned businesses increased, and women attended college in numbers so great that they now substantially outnumber men.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Fitch, who raised three children while working, argues that the pro-life movement should think about "empowering" women as well as protecting the unborn. "We need to first and foremost have the support network; we need to have the conversation about how we can help these mothers to be and how we can talk with them about the different pregnancy centers, about adoption, about options for them. You know, faith-based is always key to this."\par \par \uc2 "If we look back at the time 50 years ago, the concept of raising a child alone was pretty unspeakable, as was the concept of giving your child up for adoption. But things have changed," Ms. Fitch says. "Back in 1999 you had your first safe-haven law and now every state has one." These laws allow a mother to give her baby up to the state, often through a hospital or fire department. In 2019, 27 pro-life pregnancy centers in Mississippi {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://s27589.pcdn.co/wp-content/uploads/2021/06/2019-Mississippi-State-Impact-Report-2.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 provided}}}\uc2  $1.9 million in services and materials to more than 12,000 people, according to the Charlotte Lozier Institute.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights have also diminished societal expectations of fathers, the pro-life feminist groups argue: "While sex and potential motherhood remain unshakably connected a half-century after Roe, the connection between sex and potential fatherhood\u8212\'20\'14that connection irresponsible men have always sought to avoid\u8212\'20\'14has grown far more tenuous." That argument was also made in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.jstor.org/stable/2946680"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 1996 paper}}}\uc2  whose co-authors included Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  and her husband, economist \uc2 George Akerlof\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ms. Fitch recalls that when she was on the platform committee for the Republican National Convention in 2016, she "pushed and had language added that affirmed our moral obligation to assist these pregnant women. To hold the fathers financially responsible too." The platform {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://prod-cdn-static.gop.com/media/documents/DRAFT_12_FINAL[1]-ben_1468872234.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 supported}}}\uc2  "legislation that requires financial responsibility for the child be equally borne by both the mother and father upon conception until the child reaches adulthood."\par \par \uc2 Ms. Fitch is optimistic that the state will prevail. "I'm excited," she says. "I truly believe that the Supreme Court is going to take a hard look at this and give us some good directions to protect life."\par \par \uc2 What about the woman who sacrifices a job she loves to raise a child? That's a choice too, and not necessarily one made in despair. Roe and Casey "pitted women and children against one another, and that's not where we want to be," Ms. Fitch says. "With Roe out of the way, we'll have the opportunity to really have genuine, healthy conversations about how we care for the dignity of women and their children."\par \par \uc2 Ms. Ault is an assistant editorial page writer at the Journal.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/does-abortion-promote-equality-for-women-childbearing-law-11638115972"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Does \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Promote Equality for Women?}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020211128ehbs00234\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
