\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Texas  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law Gives a Glimpse Into a Post-Roe World; State provides window into what antiabortion advocates hope, and  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2 -rights proponents fear, could spread nationwide\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Findell \par \uc2 1,475 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 November 2021\par 12:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 AUSTIN, Texas\u8212\'20\'14Over nine weeks, a new Texas law effectively banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks has transformed the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  climate.\par \par \uc2 The number of abortions performed in clinics has dropped. Some women have carried out their own abortions, such as one woman who took drugs she purchased from overseas. Others, including a 13-year-old assault victim, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-prompts-women-to-seek-out-of-state-clinics-11632315616"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have fled Texas to other states }}}\uc2 , where clinics say they are overwhelmed by Texas demand.\par \par \uc2 "What we've seen in Texas is a glimpse of a post-Roe world, an insight into what an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -free state is going to look like," said John Seago, legislative director for Texas Right to Life, which helped push for the law's passage.\par \par \uc2 The Texas Heartbeat Act, also known as SB8, prohibits abortions after "cardiac activity" can be detected, which for most pregnancies happens at around two weeks after a woman has missed her period. It was designed to avoid being overturned under the precedent of Roe v. Wade, the Supreme Court case that established the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to the viability of the fetus, by outsourcing its enforcement to private individuals. The law, which legislatures in other states have said they would emulate if it survives legal challenges, allows anyone to sue someone suspected of aiding in an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for at least $10,000.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  heard arguments over whether the federal government and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers can challenge the law, with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-hears-arguments-on-texas-abortion-law-11635765801"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 several justices voicing skepticism }}}\uc2  over the state's efforts to shield its stringent restrictions from judicial scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 A potentially more far-reaching legal challenge will reach the Court early next month, when {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/in-mississippi-abortion-case-supreme-court-sets-arguments-for-december-11632176939"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the justices will consider a Mississippi law }}}\uc2  that seeks to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks. That case will give the court a direct opportunity to revisit Roe v. Wade and later rulings that established and maintained a woman's constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before fetal viability. Texas and several other states have passed laws that would immediately outlaw nearly all abortions should Roe and later rulings be overturned.\par \par \uc2 The effect of SB8 in Texas provides a window into what antiabortion advocates hope, and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights proponents fear, could spread nationwide if either SB8 or the Mississippi law is upheld.\par \par \uc2 "Patients are already living in a world where Roe v. Wade doesn't exist," said Molly Duane, a senior staff attorney at the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In September, procedures performed at Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics fell by half, according to a University of Texas study released last Friday. Pregnancy centers designed to steer women away from abortions have seen a boom in calls, Mr. Seago said. Clinics have faced increased protests and threats and are facing possible closure due to curtailed business, providers said. One has been housing 13 of its staff members temporarily in New Mexico to perform procedures on Texas patients at a sister clinic there. Patients have sought other options.\par \par \uc2 One 40-year-old woman in North Texas was already past the SB8 deadline when she was surprised to discover she was pregnant in late September. Fearful that neither her body nor her finances could handle a third child, the woman, who is a mother and grandmother, turned to the internet.\par \par \uc2 "I was so desperate that\u8230\'20\'26I was researching the clothes hanger," the woman said. Instead, she found Aid Access, an organization that prescribes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills via a doctor based in Europe. She was unable to pay the full $125 for the medication, so the organization discounted it, she said.\par \par \uc2 Medication prescribed to end a pregnancy is approved by the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  up to 10 weeks of gestation, and a pause during the pandemic on a requirement that it be dispensed in-person by a doctor has allowed it to be prescribed remotely in some states. In Texas, such medication is still subject to the six-week ban, as well as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-aims-to-crack-down-on-abortion-pills-sent-by-mail-11633179600"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 another recent law that bans sending it through the mail }}}\uc2 , although that is hard to track. Several organizations provide unapproved off-brand forms of the medication through pharmacies in Europe and Asia.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What will be the impact on the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the U.S. if the Texas bill is upheld? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 SB8 provides an exception for a medical emergency, which Texas law defines as a condition that places a pregnant woman in danger of death. Doctors in the state are interpreting that differently. In September, call-takers at the \uc2 National \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Federation\uc2 's hotline heard from a woman in the Rio Grande Valley who was denied care for an ectopic pregnancy, a nonviable pregnancy that can quickly rupture and become fatal.\par \par \uc2 "We typically don't have long conversations with people who have ectopic pregnancies, it's just 'You need to go to the hospital right now,' " said Rachel Lachenauer, director of patient experience for the hotline. "In this case, the patient was repeatedly calling us back because the hospital was telling her 'We don't know if we can help you.' "\par \par \uc2 The organization provided financial assistance for the woman to drive more than 12 hours to New Mexico for care, Ms. Lachenauer said.\par \par \uc2 Other doctors in Texas, who are still treating ectopic pregnancies, called the example shocking but not a surprise. Some doctors said SB8, which protects those filing lawsuits from having to pay court costs even if they lose, gives incentive for frivolous lawsuits. Some said they were questioning whether to treat patients with complications from miscarriages, because the procedure can look similar to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Others have sent patients out of state to abort fetuses with abnormalities that meant the child wouldn't survive.\par \par \uc2 John Thoppil, president of the Texas Association of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, said doctors who have never performed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are now limiting care discussions with their patients, afraid they could be misinterpreted or falsely accused of aiding in an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He said he felt that way with a patient who recently came to see him with a rare, high-risk pregnancy, despite having an IUD. "You are scared to discuss, even in the confidentiality of an exam room, her options," Dr. Thoppil said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Seago, who championed the bill, said doctors are misunderstanding the law. Though SB8 is vague, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is defined in other chapters of Texas law to exclude miscarriages and ectopic treatment. In two months of the law being in effect, only three lawsuits have been filed, all against a doctor who publicly stated he had performed an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Mr. Seago said.\par \par \uc2 Overall, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers have complied with SB8, both providers and opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. In some cases, women eligible for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at their first appointment have been turned away 24 hours later\u8212\'20\'14after a mandatory state waiting period\u8212\'20\'14when a second ultrasound has shown cardiac activity, providers said in interviews and court filings.\par \par \uc2 Providers called it emotionally taxing to deny procedures to patients. Dr. Bhavik Kumar, a staff physician at Planned Parenthood Center for Choice in Houston, had to deny an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a 13-year-old who had been assaulted by a family member, he said. She traveled to another state weeks later to terminate the pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 "For me, knowing what happened pre-Roe came from my mentors," he said. "In my lifetime, this the closest we have been to going back to where it was, when there were \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -restricted states and states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was accessible."\par \par \uc2 In New York, Amy Paulin, a Democrat member of the state Assembly, introduced a bill that would shield New Yorkers' assets in the event they are sued under the Texas law.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Paulin said she hoped that if passed, the bill would allow New Yorkers' to feel safe in assisting women in Texas in accessing abortions. If Roe v. Wade were overturned, she said, she envisions a future of networks in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -friendly states helping those in states where it is banned.\par \par \uc2 Among the states with laws already on the books to immediately outlaw most abortions if Roe v. Wade is overturned are those where clinics report being slammed with patients from Texas, such as Oklahoma, Arkansas and Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 Angie Thomas, the associate director of Louisiana Right to Life, said she believes that if stricter \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans go into effect in more states, more people will begin to believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is wrong.\par \par \uc2 "I think the culture will begin to be shaped by that," she said. "Are people going to travel across the country for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? I don't know."\par \par \uc2 Write to Elizabeth Findell at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Elizabeth.Findell@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Elizabeth.Findell@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-gives-a-glimpse-of-a-post-roe-world-11635942617"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Law Gives a Glimpse Into a Post-Roe World }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020211103ehb3002jp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
