FDED708FBBDCE74EF7BADBFDEE78CFBBDEF7CEF7BEFBFDEF800FBCE0074FF8C21BFEF0884FBCE217CF78B40704003B}}
\par\par \uc2 Review & Outlook (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Distortion at the Supreme Court; Will the Justices blow up a crucial and longtime limit on judicial power?\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 777 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 November 2021\par 22:28 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 One bad law often begets another, and so it has gone with the Supreme Court and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Roe v. Wade. Now a bad Texas law banning abortions after six weeks may induce the Court to distort the constitutional doctrine on legal standing, with long-term damage to the judiciary and the separation of powers.\par \par \uc2 On Monday the Court heard arguments in separate lawsuits by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and the Justice Department seeking to block the Texas law. Justices \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and Amy Coney Barrett seemed troubled by how the law is designed to frustrate federal court pre-enforcement review. The law bars state officials from enforcing it while empowering private individuals to sue anyone in Texas who performs or aids an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks. Plaintiffs who prevail are entitled to at least $10,000 in damages and legal fees.\par \par \uc2 There's no doubt the law is an unconstitutional violation of the Court's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  precedents. But the proper place to vindicate the existing right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is when there is a tangible injury as the law is enforced. Under the Constitution, courts get involved only when there is a case or controversy.\par \par \uc2 This is the core constitutional restraint on judicial power. Judges preside over actual disputes. They don't exist to issue advisory opinions or to serve as roving commissions to opine on laws they don't like. Remove that requirement for legal standing, and you give courts power to rampage willy-nilly over decisions reserved for the political branches.\par \par \uc2 The standing problem in the Texas provider case (Whole Woman's Health v. Jackson) is that there is no proper defendant. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers want the Court to enjoin private plaintiffs from bringing lawsuits before they've been brought. They also want to Court to bar state officials, judges and even clerks from enforcing the law.\par \par \uc2 But state officials can't be enjoined from enforcing a law they can't legally enforce. And neither state judges nor clerks are adverse to providers' interest. "That seems to me to raise a real problem under the case or controversy requirement," Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  noted in oral argument. Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  quipped: "There are four billion tort suits in the United States, okay? And probably in three billion of them, somebody thinks something is unconstitutional. So can they all sue the judge?"\par \par \uc2 WSJ Opinion Talk\par \par \uc2 A Momentous Term for the Supreme Court Join WSJ Opinion's Paul Gigot, Allysia Finley and Kyle Peterson with legal scholar Ilya Shapiro to discuss the new Court's first full term Online on Tuesday, November 9 at 7:00 PM ET. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/live-qa/wsj-opinion-a-momentous-term-for-the-supreme-court/301AD303-A28A-48DD-8CC4-9196CA91EA1E"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Register Now}}}\par \par \uc2 State judges were also excluded from the Court's Ex Parte Young (1908) precedent that let federal courts enjoin state officials enforcing unconstitutional laws: "An injunction by a Federal court against a State court would violate the whole scheme of this Government." As for clerks, they are court functionaries.\par \par \uc2 Justices \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  and \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  wondered if private plaintiffs could be deemed state actors or private Attorneys General under the Texas law. So perhaps the Texas AG could be enjoined? Sorry, but many federal and state laws create private rights of action, and those private enforcers aren't considered junior AGs.\par \par \uc2 The proper place to challenge the law is in state court. There are 14 pre-enforcement challenges to the Texas law pending in Travis County state court. Providers note that a ruling in one state court won't set a legal precedent that can be enforced state-wide. But the Texas High Court will ultimately get to weigh in, and its decision will be reviewable by the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The oral argument on Monday suggested that the Justices may already have decided, with a 6-3 majority ready to let the Texas provider suit proceed. Justice Breyer seemed skeptical of the Justice Department suit, which is likely to be dismissed. But he may know he can count on a majority in the provider suit.\par \par \uc2 This would be a bad mistake by the Court. The Justices seemed to be hunting for a way to let the providers' challenge move in federal court but without violating their standing principles. Such a way doesn't exist.\par \par \uc2 If Justices make an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  exception, there's no limiting principle to the kind of suits that federal courts will be asked to entertain. Conservative Justices shouldn't muck up the Court's standing principles merely because a state passed a rotten \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-distortion-at-the-supreme-court-11635892105"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Distortion at the Supreme Court}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020211102ehb2006v9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
