\par\par \uc2 Politics and Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court Questions Texas  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law; Justices voice concern about state's efforts to shield its stringent restrictions on abortions from judicial scrutiny\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brent Kendall and Jess Bravin \par \uc2 1,268 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 November 2021\par 21:21 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14Supreme Court justices asked skeptical questions Monday about a novel Texas ban on many abortions, voicing concern about the state's efforts to shield its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-in-court-what-you-need-to-know-11631730720"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stringent restrictions }}}\uc2  from judicial scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 During nearly three hours of oral arguments, justices across the ideological spectrum explored how they might allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates to challenge the law in federal court\u8212\'20\'14with some expressing concern that other states could follow the model of the Texas law to target religious expression, access to guns or other rights defined by the court.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's focus Monday wasn't directly on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, but rather the anomaly of the law, known as SB 8 or the Texas Heartbeat Act: State officials aren't allowed to enforce it, a feature designed to thwart legal challenges. Under Supreme Court precedents, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights plaintiffs would sue such officials and ask a judge to block them from enforcing the law.\par \par \uc2 Texas instead limited enforcement to private parties, who are offered financial incentives to sue \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and other people who aid abortions. A successful plaintiff is entitled to at least $10,000 in damages. That structure was intended to force providers to break the law and then be sued before they can raise challenges to SB 8's constitutionality.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 Do you think the Texas law banning most abortions is constitutional? Why or why not?\par \par \uc2 The law took effect two months ago and is the toughest in the nation, banning abortions after about six weeks of pregnancy, far earlier than current Supreme Court precedent allows. It bars doctors from knowingly performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if there is a "detectable fetal heartbeat," which the law defines to include early cardiac activity in the embryo, and doesn't contain exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 Justice Elena Kagan said the purpose of Texas's law was to get around Supreme Court precedents on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and evade the "even broader principle that states are not to nullify federal constitutional rights."\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , citing Justice Kagan's comments, questioned whether Texas was exploiting a loophole that the court ought to close. He also cited a brief in support of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics that was filed by Second Amendment advocates who warned that other states could follow Texas and effectively nullify other constitutional rights, such as protections for gun ownership.\par \par \uc2 Lawyer Marc Hearron, representing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, said SB 8 created a chilling effect on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights because it subjects providers to the threat of an unending barrage of lawsuits, which wouldn't recede even if clinics successfully defended themselves in early cases in the state courts.\par \par \uc2 Texas Solicitor General Judd Stone argued the providers' lawsuit was improper and said the plaintiffs have no basis for getting into federal court right now. Texas was entitled to channel challenges to its own state courts, he said, where more than a dozen "pre-enforcement" suits currently are pending.\par \par \uc2 Justice Amy Coney Barrett, the court's newest member and a potentially pivotal vote, made early comments that questioned the propriety of SB 8. The state law, she said, may not allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers to make a full defense in state court if they get sued, for instance by limiting the application of Supreme Court precedents that prevent imposition of "undue burdens" on women's access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before fetal viability.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , another member of the court's conservative wing, voiced resistance to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers' challenge. He suggested that traditional legal rules under the Texas Constitution already could effectively restrict a wave of private lawsuits by individuals under SB 8, no matter that the new law encourages them. That is because plaintiffs may have no legitimate basis to claim they were harmed by an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic performing the procedure, he said.\par \par \uc2 That is because plaintiffs normally must demonstrate they have been harmed to file suit.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  asked what harm any third party could claim from someone else's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "One example could be akin to the injury suffered in the tort of outrage, where an individual becomes aware of a noncompliant \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and they suffer the sort of same extreme emotional harm," Mr. Stone said. That's "where a person witnesses something they essentially find to be so extreme and outrageous it causes them extreme moral or psychological harm."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers had sued to stop the law before it went into effect, but the Fifth U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals said the lawsuit faced procedural problems in suing state officials. Then on Sept. 1, the day the law took force, a 5-4 Supreme Court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-rejects-emergency-request-to-block-texas-six-week-abortion-ban-11630556283"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 declined to intervene }}}\uc2  right away, with the court's most conservative justices forming a majority that cited reasons similar to those of the appeals court.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department then {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-administration-expected-to-file-suit-soon-challenging-texas-abortion-law-11631205764"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed its own lawsuit }}}\uc2  against Texas, arguing that courts could validly block the state\u8212\'20\'14and private citizens acting on its behalf\u8212\'20\'14from enforcing SB 8. A federal {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/federal-judge-blocks-texas-abortion-law-11633568447"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 trial judge in Austin agreed }}}\uc2 , but the Fifth Circuit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-restrictions-can-continue-during-litigation-appeals-court-rules-11634261230"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 put that ruling on hold }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Alongside the Supreme Court's concerns about the Texas law, its members also expressed reservations about the department's lawsuit against the state that sought to block anyone, including state court judges, from taking actions related to SB 8.\par \par \uc2 "Are you aware of a precedent that permits an injunction against all persons in the country, the world, the cosmos?" Justice \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  asked.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Solicitor General \uc2 Elizabeth Prelogar\uc2 , in her first argument since her Senate confirmation last week, responded that the department's proposed injunction "doesn't do that either."\par \par \uc2 "In the history of the United States, no state has done what Texas has done here," she added.\par \par \uc2 The litigation is moving at a speed hardly ever reached at the high court, which prefers a slow and steady pace. Just 10 days ago, the justices said they would step in to address thorny legal questions raised by the law, which has sent Texas women seeking abortions after six weeks of pregnancy scrambling for procedures in nearby states.\par \par \uc2 The Texas battle is one of two significant \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  tests in the next month for a Supreme Court that has grown more conservative since the arrival of three Trump appointees. On Dec. 1, the court will consider a Mississippi law that seeks to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks, a case that provides a direct opportunity to revisit the 1973 Roe v. Wade decision and later rulings that established and maintained a woman's constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before fetal viability.\par \par \uc2 Mississippi and its allies have asked the court to eliminate the federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  altogether and leave the procedure's legality to the discretion of state legislatures. Should that happen, the SB 8 controversy could become little more than a footnote, because Texas and several other states have passed legislation that would immediately outlaw nearly all abortions should Roe be overruled. SB 8 itself observes that Texas never repealed the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that was set aside in Roe, a case that stemmed from a Texas woman's unsuccessful effort to end her unwanted pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Given the Supreme Court's expedited consideration of the Texas law, a ruling could come more quickly than under the court's usual timeline, where a decision would typically be several months away.\par \par \uc2 Write to Brent Kendall at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:brent.kendall@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 brent.kendall@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Jess Bravin at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jess.bravin+1@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jess.bravin+1@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-hears-arguments-on-texas-abortion-law-11635765801"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court Questions Texas \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Law }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020211101ehb100105\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
