\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Conservative Appeals Court Is Prime Venue for Biden-Era Litigation; New Orleans-based federal court already has dealt administration setbacks on immigration,  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brent Kendall \par \uc2 1,171 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 October 2021\par 22:34 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The New Orleans-based Fifth U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals, which last week allowed Texas' restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law to remain in effect in the face of a federal challenge, promises to be a legal battleground for a host of Biden administration initiatives in the years ahead.\par \par \uc2 The Fifth Circuit, one of the nation's leading conservative courts, covers the states of Texas, Louisiana and Mississippi. It has 17 active judges, 12 of whom are Republican appointees, including six placed on the court by former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . An array of hot-button cases are likely to land on the court's docket as Republicans including Texas Attorney General \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2  and conservative groups challenge administration policies.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Paxton already was part of a successful effort to block the administration's planned rollback of a Trump-era immigration policy on U.S. asylum seekers, and he has sued the Biden administration on issues including energy, coronavirus aid, Medicaid and gender-identity policies in the workplace.\par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case, it was the federal government that stepped in to sue Texas, arguing the state's ban on abortions after about six weeks of pregnancy was in open defiance of established constitutional rights. The Justice Department won an initial ruling from a federal judge in Austin, but {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-restrictions-can-continue-during-litigation-appeals-court-rules-11634261230"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Fifth Circuit blocked that ruling }}}\uc2  and issued a brief written order suggesting there were problems with the department's lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court on Friday {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-agrees-to-quick-consideration-of-texas-abortion-case-leaves-state-law-in-place-for-now-11634921299"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 agreed to quickly consider that lawsuit }}}\uc2  and another one challenging the Texas law, while leaving it in place for now.\par \par \uc2 The Fifth Circuit's role as a central venue for Republican litigation in many ways mirrors the role of the Ninth U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals during the Trump administration. California's attorney general, other Democrats and civil-liberties groups bringing legal challenges against Mr. Trump's treatment of immigrants, his travel ban and his plans to build a southern-border wall often filed them in Northern California. They eventually went through the Ninth Circuit, which has a reputation as the nation's most liberal appeals court.\par \par \uc2 "Given that Texas, like California before it, is in a position to be bringing more of these lawsuits, it does mean that the Fifth Circuit will be in the spotlight," said Scott Keller, a former solicitor general of Texas.\par \par \uc2 The Fifth Circuit long has been among the most conservative jurisdictions within the federal judiciary. It has become consistently more conservative, however, with the addition of Mr. Trump's six appointees, who were part of a push by the GOP to make filling court vacancies a priority, allowing the Trump White House to shape {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/trump-appointees-poised-to-influence-legal-outcomes-for-decades-to-come-11612112401"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the direction of the federal courts }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The newest Fifth Circuit judges have made their mark in some of the court's biggest cases and been vocal on issues including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 One of them, Judge James Ho, was part of the 2-1 majority last week that allowed the Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law to remain in place while the Justice Department challenges it. In a previous case from 2018, Judge Ho called \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a "moral tragedy."\par \par \uc2 Two other Trump appointees were part of an earlier Fifth Circuit panel that declined to block the Texas law before it went into effect Sept. 1, citing what it said were procedural problems with a separate lawsuit filed by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers. That case will now go to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 William Jay, an appellate lawyer with \uc2 Goodwin Procter LLP\uc2  in Washington, D.C., said while the Fifth Circuit wasn't the only regional federal court with a large contingent of conservative judges, "There are more thought-leader conservatives on the Fifth Circuit" than on most others. That is because the court has a large number of judges, a docket regularly populated with high-profile cases and a large audience for its work, he said. Given the court's makeup, he added, "Just as a matter of probabilities, you are more likely to draw a conservative-leaning panel."\par \par \uc2 On immigration, a three-judge Fifth Circuit panel, including two Trump appointees, ruled in August that the Biden administration for now has to maintain a Trump-era policy requiring asylum seekers at the southern U.S. border to remain in Mexico while their claims are considered. The court agreed with a trial judge that the Biden administration hadn't offered sufficient reasons for rescinding the policy. The Supreme Court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-reinstates-remain-in-mexico-policy-for-asylum-applicants-11629849547"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 also rejected the administration's emergency bid }}}\uc2  to keep the Trump policy from being reinstated.\par \par \uc2 In a separate case, the court last week declined a Biden administration request to postpone proceedings in a long-running challenge to the Deferred Action for Childhood Arrivals program, or DACA, an Obama-era initiative that provided deportation protections and work permits to some young immigrants. The \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  wanted the case put on hold while it wrote new rules it hoped would pass legal muster.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What role do you see the Fifth Circuit playing throughout the Biden administration? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 The administration, however, did win some relief from the appeals court in another immigration case, where an ideologically mixed three-judge panel partially allowed it to move forward with a policy of not arresting or deporting immigrants unless they have committed serious crimes.\par \par \uc2 The Fifth Circuit previously was home to legal battles challenging Obama-era policies. The court in 2015 blocked the Obama administration's plans to extend deportation protections and benefits to a broader range of immigrants. A short-handed Supreme Court deadlocked 4-4 on the issue, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/u-s-supreme-court-deadlock-blocks-obama-immigration-plan-1466693502"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 which left the Fifth Circuit ruling in place }}}\uc2  and effectively ended the effort.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, the appeals court ruled a central feature of the Affordable Care Act was unconstitutional, but the Supreme Court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-leaves-affordable-care-act-intact-11623938948"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reversed that decision }}}\uc2  in June, ruling Texas and other Republican-led states lacked standing to challenge the 2010 healthcare law.\par \par \uc2 The high court in recent years also has reversed the Fifth Circuit in cases involving \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-strikes-down-louisiana-abortion-law-11593440418"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 striking down restrictions in Louisiana and Texas }}}\uc2  after the appeals court allowed them.\par \par \uc2 Despite the Fifth Circuit's overall conservatism, its Republican appointees don't hold monolithic views on the law. One prominent split has focused on the doctrine of qualified immunity that provides legal protections to police and other public officials accused of misconduct.\par \par \uc2 Judge \uc2 Don Willett\uc2 , a Trump appointee and former justice on the Texas Supreme Court, has argued the current legal approach "shields lawbreaking officials from accountability, even for patently unconstitutional abuses," and should be re-examined.\par \par \uc2 Judge Ho, meanwhile, has argued that the doctrine is appropriately calibrated to provide police with the leeway they need to perform an often dangerous job and prevent judges from "sneering at cops on the beat from the safety of our chambers."\par \par \uc2 Write to Brent Kendall at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:brent.kendall@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 brent.kendall@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/conservative-appeals-court-is-prime-venue-for-biden-era-litigation-11634907602"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Conservative Appeals Court Is Prime Venue for Biden-Era Litigation }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020211022eham0028o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
