\par\par \uc2 Politics and Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 DOJ Asks Supreme Court to Block Texas  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law; Government's emergency appeal forces high court to confront Texas ban on most abortions for second time in a matter of weeks\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brent Kendall \par \uc2 727 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 October 2021\par 22:13 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14The Justice Department on Monday filed an emergency request with the Supreme Court that seeks to block {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-what-to-know-as-supreme-court-allows-it-to-take-effect-11630606238"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas' ban on most abortions  }}}\uc2 , returning the issue to the justices after they previously declined to intervene against the new state law.\par \par \uc2 The department's high-court filing said the Texas measure, which bans abortions after about six weeks of pregnancy, is in open {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/21/21A85/196650/20211018120230336_US v. Texas application final.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 defiance of Supreme Court rulings that have protected \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights  }}}\uc2  for a half-century. "Texas has, in short, successfully nullified this court's decisions within its borders," the department wrote.\par \par \uc2 Texas has engaged in a novel effort to shield its law from judicial review. The department said that if that tactic succeeds, there is nothing to stop other states from mounting similar efforts to write laws that deny other constitutional rights the Supreme Court has previously protected, such as the right to own a handgun in the home.\par \par \uc2 "If Texas is right, states are free to use similar schemes to nullify other precedents or suspend other constitutional rights," the department wrote.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department asked the court to block the law while lower-court litigation continues. It also suggested the justices could go ahead and give full consideration to the case, with detailed written briefing and oral arguments, even though lower courts aren't finished.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court already is scheduled to hear {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/in-mississippi-abortion-case-supreme-court-sets-arguments-for-december-11632176939"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a major \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 -rights case from Mississippi  }}}\uc2  on Dec. 1. It is conceivable the court could add the Texas case to the mix.\par \par \uc2 Hours after the Justice Department filing, the high court said it would expedite consideration of a separate pending request by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers to hear their challenge to the Texas law before a lower-court judgment is final. The move raised the possibility that the Supreme Court could hear both Texas cases during its current term by taking them from lower-court dockets now.\par \par \uc2 The Texas attorney general's office didn't immediately respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 The state law, known as the Texas Heartbeat Act, or SB 8, has been in effect since Sept. 1, and is the most restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law in the nation. Physicians can't perform the procedure if they know there is "a detectable fetal heartbeat," which the law defines as including cardiac activity in the embryo in the early weeks of pregnancy. The law doesn't contain exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 Texas wrote its law in a manner designed to make it difficult to challenge in court. It did so by preventing state officials from having any direct role in enforcing the law. Instead, SB 8 empowers private parties to file civil lawsuits alleging violations. A plaintiff who wins in court can collect at least $10,000 from someone found to have performed\u8212\'20\'14or aided\u8212\'20\'14a banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers filed a lawsuit challenging the law before it went into effect, but were unable to obtain a court order blocking the law, in large part because of difficulties in determining who was a proper defendant, given that the Texas officials in charge of enforcing laws are barred from doing so with SB 8.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court cited that issue on Sept. 1, when it declined an emergency request by the providers to block the law. The Justice Department followed with its own lawsuit and argues its case is different, in part because it says it has authority to sue Texas directly to protect the federal government's sovereign interests.\par \par \uc2 An Austin, Texas, federal judge originally sided with the department on Oct. 6 and issued {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/federal-judge-blocks-texas-abortion-law-11633568447"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a preliminary injunction  }}}\uc2  against the Texas law. But the Fifth U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals last week {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-restrictions-can-continue-during-litigation-appeals-court-rules-11634261230"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 postponed the effect of that ruling  }}}\uc2  and said SB 8 could remain in effect for now.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department's application Monday was directed to Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  , who oversees emergency matters out of the Fifth Circuit. He will almost surely refer the matter to the full court for consideration.\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito ordered Texas to respond to the department's filing by Thursday at noon.\par \par \uc2 Write to Brent Kendall at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:brent.kendall@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 brent.kendall@wsj.com  }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/justice-departments-asks-supreme-court-to-block-texas-abortion-law-11634582218"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 DOJ Asks Supreme Court to Block Texas \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Law  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020211018ehai003ux\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
