\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Texas  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Restrictions Can Continue During Litigation, Appeals Court Rules; Federal appeals court order sets the stage for the issue to return to the Supreme Court\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brent Kendall \par \uc2 553 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 October 2021\par 02:22 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A federal appeals court on Thursday said Texas's restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law can remain in effect during ongoing litigation with the Justice Department, extending a previous temporary order that kept the law in place.\par \par \uc2 The order, from the New Orleans-based Fifth U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals came on a 2-1 vote and postpones the effect of an Oct. 6 ruling from an Austin federal judge. He said Texas had engaged in an unprecedented effort to deprive women of constitutionally protected \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and keep its restrictions shielded from judicial review.\par \par \uc2 U.S. District Judge \uc2 Robert Pitman\uc2  had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/federal-judge-blocks-texas-abortion-law-11633568447"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 issued a preliminary injunction  }}}\uc2  prohibiting enforcement of the law, which bars the procedure after about six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The Fifth Circuit last week paused the judge's ruling temporarily to give itself some time to consider an emergency request from Texas to reinstate the law, known as the Texas Heartbeat Act, or SB 8. The new order Thursday {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-ruling-overcame-a-ban-designed-to-thwart-courts-11633736413"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 could keep Judge Pitman's decision on hold  }}}\uc2  for many months while the case continues, unless the Supreme Court steps in. The Justice Department now has the option to seek emergency intervention from the high court.\par \par \uc2 The state law says physicians can't knowingly perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if there is "a detectable fetal heartbeat," which includes cardiac activity in the embryo that appears after about six weeks into a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The law, which has been in effect since September, delegates enforcement to private parties and authorizes civil damages of $10,000 or more to anyone who successfully sues a defendant accused of performing or aiding in such an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A wider range of abortions were again legal in Texas during a two-day window following Judge Pitman's decision, though some providers didn't rush to offer them amid ongoing legal uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court, in earlier litigation initiated by Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, on Sept. 1 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-rejects-emergency-request-to-block-texas-six-week-abortion-ban-11630556283"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 declined to block the law  }}}\uc2  on the day it went into effect, by a 5-4 vote, with the court's most conservative justices in the majority. The court said there were serious questions about the law's constitutionality but declined to intervene because of procedural complexities created by Texas's unusual command that the law could only be enforced by private parties, not state officials.\par \par \uc2 The Fifth Circuit's order Thursday, which ran just one paragraph, cited the high court's earlier order, as well as a previous order from its own court.\par \par \uc2 The two judges in the Fifth Circuit majority were appointed by Republican presidents. The dissenter was a Democratic appointee.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department stepped in to sue Texas last month after \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates were unable to obtain a court order against the state restrictions, which are in conflict with current Supreme Court precedent.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department has argued that its lawsuit should not face the same procedural hurdles as those facing the private plaintiffs, a contention Texas disputes.\par \par \uc2 Neither the department nor the Texas attorney general's office could immediately be reached for comment.\par \par \uc2 Write to Brent Kendall at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:brent.kendall@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 brent.kendall@wsj.com  }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-restrictions-can-continue-during-litigation-appeals-court-rules-11634261230"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Restrictions Can Continue During Litigation, Appeals Court Rules  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020211015ehaf0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
