\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court Considers Reviving Appeal of Kentucky  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law; Case centers on right of state's Republican attorney general to pursue defense of law limiting  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  after the Democratic governor stood back from further appeals\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jess Bravin \par \uc2 807 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 October 2021\par 19:06 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14The Supreme Court on Tuesday appeared inclined to revive an appeal of lower-court rulings that struck down a Kentucky antiabortion law as unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 The legal question was procedural: Whether Kentucky's Republican attorney general has the right to pursue additional litigation in defense of the statute, which outlaws most second-trimester abortions, following a loss before a federal circuit court that the Democratic governor's administration declined to appeal further.\par \par \uc2 Most justices appeared to think the answer was yes.\par \par \uc2 It would be odd if appellate rules left nobody "there to defend Kentucky's law, even though there are significant parts of Kentucky's government that still want its law defended," Justice \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's term is rife with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases. Last month, the justices cited procedural reasons {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-rejects-emergency-request-to-block-texas-six-week-abortion-ban-11630556283"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to permit a Texas law prohibiting abortions after six weeks to take effect  }}}\uc2  while legal challenges proceed. In a case set for Dec. 1, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/in-mississippi-abortion-case-supreme-court-sets-arguments-for-december-11632176939"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mississippi is asking the court to end federal \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights  }}}\uc2  by overruling the 1973 case that recognized them, Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 There was no discussion of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself at Tuesday's argument, suggesting that the case centered on the peculiarities of Kentucky politics more than the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Democrat \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  was attorney general in April 2018, when Republican Gov. \uc2 Matt Bevin\uc2  signed the measure outlawing the most common second-trimester \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedure, dilation and evacuation.\par \par \uc2 When the state's sole \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic, EMW Women's Surgical Center in Louisville, sued, Mr. Beshear's office sat the case out, leaving the defense of the law to Mr. Bevin's secretary of health and family services.\par \par \uc2 Some 10 states have passed similar bans on the procedure, in which a physician uses suction and instruments to remove fetal material through the patient's dilated cervix. Those laws have been struck down by federal courts for violating Supreme Court precedents that protect women's right to end pregnancy before fetal viability.\par \par \uc2 Kentucky's version was no exception. In May 2019, a federal district judge in Louisville found it unconstitutional. The state appealed to the Sixth U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals, in Cincinnati.\par \par \uc2 While the appeal was pending, Mr. Beshear ousted Mr. Bevin from the governor's office and a Republican, \uc2 Daniel Cameron\uc2  , won the race for attorney general in the November 2019 elections. The Beshear administration continued defending the law before the Sixth Circuit but, after losing before a three-judge panel in June 2020, it declined to pursue further appeals.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cameron then sought to intervene in the case, intending to ask the full Sixth Circuit or the Supreme Court itself to review the panel decision. The Sixth Circuit turned him down, finding that the attorney general's office had waited too long to intervene.\par \par \uc2 "What we as Kentuckians want is a fail-safe, a fail-safe that if a state official who enforces state law says, 'I'm not going to appeal any further,' Kentucky's attorney general can come in for the commonwealth and say, 'No, the commonwealth wants to go further,'" Mr. Cameron's lawyer, Matthew Kuhn, told the justices Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  , more than most justices, was skeptical.\par \par \uc2 If the attorney general "had an opportunity to be in the litigation and chose to get out and chose to bind itself to the decisions of the secretary...how can we say the Sixth Circuit abused its discretion in honoring that commitment?" she said.\par \par \uc2 Alexa Kolbi-Molinas, an \uc2 American Civil Liberties Union\uc2  lawyer representing the clinic, said Mr. Cameron should be held to the legal statements removing the attorney general from the case made when Mr. Beshear held that post.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What clues to the Supreme Court's approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related issues will you look for in this case? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 "A successor in office is bound by the stipulations made by and judgments against their predecessors. It doesn't matter that there's been a political party change," she said.\par \par \uc2 But Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  said the partisan switch was relevant.\par \par \uc2 "First, the Republicans are in [the governor's office], then the Democrats are in, and they have different views on an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  statute," he said. "If there's no prejudice to anybody, and I can't see where there is, why can't he just come in and defend the law?" Justice Breyer said.\par \par \uc2 A decision in the case, Cameron v. EMW Women's Surgical Center, is expected by June.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jess Bravin at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jess.bravin+1@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jess.bravin+1@wsj.com  }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-considers-reviving-appeal-of-kentucky-abortion-law-11634065600"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court Considers Reviving Appeal of Kentucky \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Law  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020211012ehac005sd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
