\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 In Pivotal Virginia Beach, Governor's Race Turns on National Issues; Many voters say their views on  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2 , the pandemic and the state of the two parties are guiding their choice between \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  and Glenn Youngkin\b0\par\par\uc2 By Scott Calvert and Chad Day | Photographs by Carlos Bernate for The Wall Street Journal \par \uc2 1,486 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 October 2021\par 13:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 VIRGINIA BEACH, Va.\u8212\'20\'14With three weeks remaining in Virginia's tightly contested race for governor, both parties are focused on winning this coastal city known for a bustling tourist boardwalk and the roar of jet fighters from the naval air station.\par \par \uc2 The once reliably Republican city has swung toward Democrats the past four years and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/democratic-clashes-hold-up-bidens-agenda-after-rocky-month-11633120486"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 now stands as a key gauge of the mood }}}\uc2  of voters in politically competitive areas heading into next year's midterms. That is particularly the case as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-rights-house-vote-planned-by-pelosi-after-supreme-court-decision-11630603877"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nationally resonant issues such as \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 , vaccine mandates, schools and fallout from the Jan. 6 U.S. Capitol riot have permeated the race.\par \par \uc2 Broadly, the contest between former Democratic \uc2 Gov. Terry McAuliffe\uc2  and Republican Glenn Youngkin, a former private-equity executive new to politics, will provide the latest insight into whether Virginia will continue to be a blue state after years of gains by Democrats or whether the GOP can reverse losses in the suburbs during former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's administration. Mr. Trump has endorsed Mr. Youngkin.\par \par \uc2 In more than 20 interviews last week, many Virginia Beach residents framed their gubernatorial choice at least partly in terms of a national political issue.\par \par \uc2 "I would have previously been leaning toward Republicans. But since Trump, I have to be anti-Republican. I just have to, because he's made a mockery of the Republican Party," said Jeff Willett, a 67-year-old boutique owner who plans to vote for Mr. McAuliffe.\par \par \uc2 Tom Wunderlich, 76, a Youngkin voter who retired from successive careers as a Marine lieutenant colonel and a college administrator, said he thought "McAuliffe essentially would be a rubber stamp of the Democratic national leadership." He also blasted the chaotic exit of U.S. forces from Afghanistan under President Biden as a "disaster."\par \par \uc2 The results in the governor's race here will give some clues as to whether Mr. Biden's flagging approval rating will have consequences down ballot. Mr. Biden won the state last year by 10 points, but the off-year gubernatorial contest has historically favored the opposition party of the sitting president, with Mr. McAuliffe's 2013 victory being a recent exception.\par \par \uc2 Polls for weeks have portrayed a close race in the state, where official elections data collected by the nonprofit Virginia Public Access Project shows already more than 275,000 people have voted early. A \uc2 Christopher Newport University\uc2  voter survey released Friday put Mr. McAuliffe ahead by 4 percentage points among likely voters, a statistical tie, with independent voters swinging to Mr. Youngkin.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, turnout in the blue-trending Northern Virginia suburbs and ruby-red rural areas will play large roles in deciding the state's next governor. But places like Virginia Beach will provide some of the first hard data points on national political trends in swing regions.\par \par \uc2 During the Trump administration, Democrats made gains in the state's largest independent city, which has a population of nearly 460,000. Gov. Ralph Northam, who isn't allowed to run for a consecutive term, won here in 2017, as did Democratic Sen. Tim Kaine in 2018. Last year Mr. Biden became the first Democratic presidential candidate to carry the city since President Lyndon Johnson in 1964.\par \par \uc2 While a large city, Virginia Beach is generally suburban in nature and has seen modest increases in diversity. The city's median household income of $76,610 and its share of residents with at least a bachelor's degree\u8212\'20\'1436%\u8212\'20\'14are both higher than national figures.\par \par \uc2 "We've seen nationally, and certainly in Virginia Beach, a shift in party coalitions, where education is now an important determinant of party choice, especially among whites. So this has helped swing suburbs all across the country and urban areas toward the Democratic Party," said Jesse Richman, an associate professor of political science at Old Dominion University in nearby Norfolk.\par \par \uc2 In Virginia Beach, "I could see it going either way and certainly that could suggest a bellwether case," said Rebecca Bromley-Trujillo, the research director at the Wason Center for Civic Leadership at \uc2 Christopher Newport University\uc2 . Mr. Biden's drop in approval rating is aiding Mr. Youngkin, and recent Democratic inroads in suburban areas are helping Mr. McAuliffe, she said.\par \par \uc2 For close friends Valerie Register and Valerie Ainsworth, who had met for lunch at a restaurant, a key factor in their vote is their stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has emerged as a top campaign issue since {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-ruling-overcame-a-ban-designed-to-thwart-courts-11633736413"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passage of a Texas ban on abortions }}}\uc2  after six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Register, a 53-year-old software developer who described herself as pro-life, said a close friend growing up ended a pregnancy, and "it's wrecked her entire life to this day." She said she would vote for Mr. Youngkin.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Ainsworth, a 60-year-old data analyst, said a family member who is developmentally disabled had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after becoming pregnant by a man who abused her. "The idea that we would choose governmentally what women can do with their bodies, I cannot accept that," said Ms. Ainsworth, who is voting for Mr. McAuliffe.\par \par \uc2 Laurie Miller, a 35-year-old stay-at-home mother who popped into a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , said she had a medically necessary \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 21 weeks and will vote Democratic despite disappointment with Mr. Biden.\par \par \uc2 "I literally cannot imagine someone else in the position I was in to be denied that access," said Ms. Miller, who fears tighter restrictions if the GOP returns to power in Virginia. Republicans could retake the state House; the state Senate, also in Democratic hands, isn't on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Youngkin, who was co-chief executive of the \uc2 Carlyle Group\uc2 , said at a debate last month he wouldn't have signed the Texas law but indicated he would endorse new limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He said he supports exceptions for rape, incest or to save a woman's life. Mr. McAuliffe, governor from 2014 to 2018, says he would defend women's access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Christopher Newport poll found 61% of likely voters back laws to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, and 55% oppose banning the procedure once a fetal heartbeat is detected around six weeks.\par \par \uc2 Last week, voters in Virginia Beach raised other issues motivating their vote.\par \par \uc2 Beach-apparel-shop owner Virginia Slusher, a Republican, said she backs Mr. Youngkin in part because of his sympathy for parents concerned about school instruction. "He believes in parents being involved in what their children are taught in the public school system," Ms. Slusher said.\par \par \uc2 Retired teacher Anne Deary, 64, a self-described liberal, said she has been dismayed by recent contentious school-board meetings where parents have wanted to dictate lessons. "We have curriculum people who I think in Virginia are very levelheaded about what should be taught," she said.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 Who do you think will be the winner in the Virginia governor race, and why? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 Kevin Deary, a 63-year-old retired Army chief warrant officer who is married to Ms. Deary, said he voted early for Mr. McAuliffe, in part because he is disturbed by Republicans' response to the Jan. 6 assault on the \uc2 Capitol\uc2  by a pro-Trump mob.\par \par \uc2 Both candidates have urged people to get Covid-19 vaccinations. Mr. McAuliffe has embraced mandates for some groups, such as schoolchildren who are eligible to be vaccinated. Mr. Youngkin says it should be a personal choice.\par \par \uc2 Gym owner Tom Lenhart, 36, said Mr. Youngkin's position is a big reason he will get his vote. "I do not believe in mandated vaccinations," he said.\par \par \uc2 Retired teacher Della Oliver, 70, a Democrat who supports Mr. McAuliffe, said Covid-19 vaccines shouldn't be optional. "It's not all about you; you're looking out for other people, too," she said.\par \par \uc2 Jim Church, who served in the Army and typically votes Democratic, said he thinks Mr. McAuliffe made good decisions as governor. "I think that McAuliffe is more of a personable-type individual versus Youngkin," he said.\par \par \uc2 Some voters said they are still considering their options. Keara McNeal, a 29-year-old barista, said she has usually voted Democratic but said Mr. Youngkin's approach to vaccinations appeals to her. She said she isn't vaccinated but might get the shot soon.\par \par \uc2 "I've been kind of changing my views as I've been looking stuff up. So I might be leaning more toward the Republican Party, but not 100% sure," she said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Scott Calvert at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:scott.calvert@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 scott.calvert@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Chad Day at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Chad.Day@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Chad.Day@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/in-pivotal-virginia-beach-governors-race-turns-on-national-issues-11633957202"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 In Pivotal Virginia Beach, Governor's Race Turns on National Issues }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020211011ehab001gt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
