\par\par \uc2 Politics and Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Texas  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law Again In Effect After Appeals Court Temporarily Pauses Injunction Against State; A federal judge's earlier ruling overcame a ban designed to thwart courts\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jacob Gershman \par \uc2 1,189 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 October 2021\par 02:26 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Texas' restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law is again in effect, at least temporarily, after a federal appeals court on Friday night paused a recent ruling that blocked the state from banning the procedure after six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The Fifth U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals issued a brief order that put the ruling on hold while it considers an emergency motion by Texas to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions in place during ongoing litigation.\par \par \uc2 For a five-week stretch, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was almost abolished in the country's second-most populous state\u8212\'20\'14the result of a law called the Texas Heartbeat Act, or SB 8, that had defied constitutional protections under Roe v. Wade and eluded judicial review.\par \par \uc2 That changed briefly when U.S. District Judge \uc2 Robert Pitman\uc2  of Austin on Wednesday granted {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/federal-judge-blocks-texas-abortion-law-11633568447"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an injunction that halted the law  }}}\uc2  at the request of the \uc2 U.S. Justice Department\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 The Texas attorney general's office, in a motion filed Friday with the Fifth Circuit, urged the appeals court to intervene and "vindicate Texas's sovereign interest in preventing a single federal district court from superintending every Texas court."\par \par \uc2 Judge Pitman's ruling\u8212\'20\'14filling more than 100 pages\u8212\'20\'14included English doctrine predating the Constitution, obscure exceptions to state sovereign immunity and legal concepts familiar to child-custody cases. It all led to an exercise of federal power over a state-court system: an order forbidding the public from suing violators of SB 8 and prohibiting the Texas judiciary from taking cases under the law.\par \par \uc2 Within hours of the decision, some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in Texas resumed normal operations after closing their doors to most patients since Sept. 1, when SB 8 took effect. Clouding the victory for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights movement was the possibility that Judge Pitman's decision could be undone by the conservative-leaning benches above him: the Fifth Circuit and the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Friday's action from the appeals court underscored that threat, and legal scholars say parts of his opinion are vulnerable to reversal.\par \par \uc2 "It's not so clear this ruling will survive," said Jonathan R. Siegel, a professor at \uc2 George Washington University Law School\uc2  , pointing to what he called "technical barriers" to the Justice Department's lawsuit that the judge found creative ways to overcome.\par \par \uc2 The law's authors have said it was designed to fend off such challenges. Unlike other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans enacted and struck down elsewhere, SB 8 gave the state government no enforcement role. Instead, it deputizes the public, empowering anyone in the country to file lawsuits against clinics and their insurers or anyone helping a patient get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The law's private enforcement scheme choked off the normal avenues that clinics have for challenging pre-viability \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. The threat of damages and protracted litigation in state court proceedings compelled clinics to comply.\par \par \uc2 That was the status quo until Judge Pitman, a former federal prosecutor appointed to the bench by President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  in 2014, granted the Biden administration's request for a preliminary injunction.\par \par \uc2 A small part of Judge Pitman's opinion dealt with Roe or subsequent Supreme Court rulings affirming the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "There can be no question that S.B. 8 operates as a ban on pre-viability abortions in contravention of Roe v. Wade," he wrote.\par \par \uc2 The heart of the decision dealt with the federal government's right to sue, the authority of the court to hear the lawsuit and who can be ordered to do what. Much of the sprawling legal discussion in the ruling centers around the federal government's role as plaintiff.\par \par \uc2 Among the sharpest points of disagreement between Texas and the Justice Department has to do with the concept of standing, the constitutional requirement that, among other things, a plaintiff show that it has\u8212\'20\'14or is about to\u8212\'20\'14suffer some concrete harm caused by the defendant.\par \par \uc2 The judge said the law exposes federal agencies and employees to legal liability for assisting abortions, noting \uc2 Federal Bureau of Prisons\uc2  regulations that authorize prison medics to arrange for elective abortions requested by inmates.\par \par \uc2 The judge took a step further. He wrote that the U.S. position also rests on the legal concept that recognizes the government's role as guardian of the populace. It is the doctrine referred to in law as parens patriae (Latin for "parent of the people"), a term more often invoked in the context of juvenile court when the state assumes custody over a neglected child. The doctrine enables states to sue private defendants accused of harming or violating the rights of the general public.\par \par \uc2 Texas argued that the doctrine is reserved for lawsuits brought by states, not the federal government. Judge Pitman disagreed, writing that the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  is the "ultimate parens patriae of every American citizen."\par \par \uc2 The decision has raised concerns that the federal government could begin suing states with more frequency, but Judge Pitman said this was an exceptional case.\par \par \uc2 "The judge goes out of his way to say that the relief being ordered is not going to open the floodgates," said \uc2 Cornell University\uc2  constitutional law professor Michael Dorf.\par \par \uc2 The judge also tackled the question of what exactly is the cause of action entitling the U.S. to sue, again rejecting Texas' arguments. This part of the court's analysis could draw the most scrutiny in appeals, according to Prof. Siegel.\par \par \uc2 The right to seek redress in court is limited. The usual basis is either a statute or common law, such as negligence suits. From time to time, courts have exercised their powers to grant so-called equitable relief\u8212\'20\'14remedies the law doesn't specifically authorize\u8212\'20\'14to prevent state officials from acting unconstitutionally.\par \par \uc2 The question for the court: Could the judge use that power here because there was no other practical legal pathway to stop SB 8 and restore the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas? Judge Pitman decided he had that power.\par \par \uc2 "Had this Court not acted on its sound authority to provide relief to the United States, any number of states could enact legislation that deprives citizens of their constitutional rights, with no legal remedy to challenge that deprivation, without the concern that a federal court would enter an injunction," he wrote.\par \par \uc2 The only way to stop enforcement of the law is to stop people from bringing suits and to stop judges from hearing them. The Supreme Court has held that judges are immune from suits for damages but may be sued for injunctive relief. Cited in the litigation is a 1948 Supreme Court case that barred Missouri courts from enforcing racially restrictive housing covenants that excluded Blacks from occupying housing they had purchased.\par \par \uc2 Texas, in court papers, argued that the Supreme Court has never permitted "the federal government to generally challenge alleged constitutional violations."\par \par \uc2 Brent Kendall contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jacob Gershman at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jacob.gershman@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jacob.gershman@wsj.com  }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-ruling-overcame-a-ban-designed-to-thwart-courts-11633736413"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Law Again In Effect After Appeals Court Temporarily Pauses Injunction Against State  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020211008eha8007ep\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
