\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Texas  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law in Court: What You Need to Know; Judge grants preliminary injunction supporting Justice Department effort to upend near-ban on procedure\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brent Kendall \par \uc2 903 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 October 2021\par 13:43 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A federal judge has issued a preliminary injunction blocking a Texas law that imposes a near-ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Since the law went into effect on Sept. 1, it has largely shut down \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state by prohibiting the procedure after about the sixth week of pregnancy. The Supreme Court had earlier {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-what-to-know-as-supreme-court-allows-it-to-take-effect-11630606238"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 declined to block }}}\uc2  the Texas Heartbeat Act, or SB 8, from taking effect, rejecting by a 5-4 vote an emergency request from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics. Since then, the Justice Department filed its own lawsuit. Here's what you should know about the status of the law.\par \par \uc2 What is the latest ruling on the law and what does it mean?\par \par \uc2 Acting on a request from the Justice Department, U.S. District Judge Robert Pitman {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/federal-judge-blocks-texas-abortion-law-11633568447"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 issued a preliminary injunction }}}\uc2  blocking SB 8, saying Texas had pursued "an unprecedented and aggressive scheme to deprive its citizens of a significant and well-established constitutional right." His order lifted the strictures on abortions in the state, and at least one \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider said it would resume performing the procedure in pregnancies of up to 18 weeks.\par \par \uc2 What happens next?\par \par \uc2 Shortly after Judge Pitman released his order in Austin, the Texas attorney general's office filed a notice of appeal. The matter now goes to the New Orleans-based Fifth U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals, which is widely considered the most conservative appeals court in the U.S. That court in the past has been sympathetic to arguments by states seeking to impose new curbs on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 How can a federal judge block a law that the Supreme Court declined to block?\par \par \uc2 There were legal proceedings ahead of SB 8's Sept. 1 effective date, with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics seeking to block the six-week ban before it took hold. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-rejects-emergency-request-to-block-texas-six-week-abortion-ban-11630556283"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Supreme Court's order late that evening }}}\uc2  allowed the Texas law to take effect but didn't address its merits or whether future lawsuits could succeed in obtaining judicial review of the ban. Instead, the unsigned majority opinion, one paragraph long, only said the court couldn't intervene because of procedural uncertainties over the proper defendant in the clinics' lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department's lawsuit, the basis for the current injunction, raises a somewhat different set of legal issues.\par \par \uc2 What is the Justice Department's case against the law?\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department alleges that the Texas ban violates the constitutional command that federal law is supreme to state law as well as the 14th Amendment's guarantee of due process of law, the basis for the Supreme Court's recognition of a constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department also says federal law pre-empted the state measure because it prohibited certain abortions "that federal agencies are charged with facilitating, funding, or reimbursing," including through healthcare programs that cover the procedure in cases of rape or incest. The Texas law includes no exceptions for such cases.\par \par \uc2 Why is this litigation so complicated?\par \par \uc2 Texas's law is in clear conflict with current Supreme Court precedent that accords women the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to the stage of pregnancy at which a fetus is viable outside the womb. But the state law was designed to be difficult to challenge in court because state officials don't actually enforce the law. Instead, the Texas measure empowers private citizens to bring civil lawsuits against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics and others who may assist someone in obtaining an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That action has made it tough for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights plaintiffs to contest the ban, effectively requiring them to violate SB 8, get sued and risk damages of at least $10,000.\par \par \uc2 While the Justice Department argues that the government shouldn't face the same difficulties, Texas has argued that the federal lawsuit is improper and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/federal-judge-questions-texas-abortion-law-in-case-filed-by-justice-department-11633112921"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said it wasn't possible }}}\uc2  for the judge to issue a workable injunction because there was no person the court could validly prohibit from enforcing the law.\par \par \uc2 Will the DOJ case settle the Texas law once and for all?\par \par \uc2 Maybe, maybe not. Judge Pitman's decision to grant a preliminary injunction was only the latest step in what could be a long legal process. And even if the federal government prevails, there are open questions about the scope of any remedy the Justice Department could obtain.\par \par \uc2 The clinics' case also is still pending, even though they were unable to block the law before it went into effect. Separately, there is litigation in Texas state court that could affect the future of the six-week ban.\par \par \uc2 When will we know about the legal future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the U.S.?\par \par \uc2 That might not become clear as the result of any of this Texas litigation. Despite the recent attention on the Lone Star State, the biggest pending case {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/mississippi-asks-supreme-court-to-end-roe-v-wade-abortion-rights-11626985810"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 comes from Mississippi }}}\uc2 , which has persuaded the Supreme Court to fully consider the legality of its planned 15-week ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The high court in that case will be directly considering whether it should limit or abandon its past precedents protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. The justices will hear oral arguments in that case in the coming months, and a ruling is expected by next July.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14Jess Bravin contributed to this article.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-in-court-what-you-need-to-know-11631730720"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Law in Court: What You Need to Know }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020211007eha70060p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
