\par \par \uc2 The Texas State Capitol building in Austin, Texas. State lawmakers earlier this year passed a law banning abortions after six weeks of pregnancy. PHOTO: Ilana Panich-Linsman for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 A federal judge late Wednesday blocked enforcement of a Texas ban on abortions after six weeks of pregnancy, a legal blow against a novel law that has severely limited the procedure statewide.\par \par \uc2 U.S. District Judge Robert Pitman in Austin issued a preliminary injunction blocking the law, saying the state had pursued "an unprecedented and aggressive scheme to deprive its citizens of a significant and well-established constitutional right."\par \par \uc2 His order came in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-administration-expected-to-file-suit-soon-challenging-texas-abortion-law-11631205764"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a case brought by the Justice Department }}}\uc2 , which sued Texas last month after \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates were unable to obtain a court order against {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-in-court-what-you-need-to-know-11631730720"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Texas restrictions }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The law, known as the Texas Heartbeat Act, or SB 8, has been in effect for a month. It bars physicians from knowingly performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if there is detectable embryonic cardiac activity.\par \par \uc2 Texas lawmakers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/behind-texas-abortion-law-an-attorneys-unusual-enforcement-idea-11630762683"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 designed the measure }}}\uc2  so that it would be difficult to challenge in court by barring state officials from enforcing the law. Instead, the law delegates enforcement to private parties and authorizes civil damages of $10,000 or more to anyone who successfully sues a defendant accused of performing or aiding in such an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "From the moment S.B. 8 went into effect, women have been unlawfully prevented from exercising control over their lives in ways that are protected by the Constitution," Judge Pitman, an Obama appointee, said in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://s.wsj.net/public/resources/documents/gov.uscourts.txwd.1146510.68.0_4.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 113-page decision }}}\uc2 . "That other courts may find a way to avoid this conclusion is theirs to decide; this court will not sanction one more day of this offensive deprivation of such an important right."\par \par \uc2 The ruling is likely to spark a new chapter of fast-track legal proceedings that could land back at the Supreme Court in the coming weeks. A divided high court on Sept. 1 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-rejects-emergency-request-to-block-texas-six-week-abortion-ban-11630556283"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 declined an emergency request }}}\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers to block the law. The court's majority said there were procedural reasons it couldn't intervene.\par \par \uc2 Judge Pitman chose not to delay the effective date of his injunction to give Texas time to seek an immediate stay from an appeals court, meaning state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers have at least a short time window to resume offering abortions to a broad range of patients if they choose to do so.\par \par \uc2 Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  called the decision "a victory for women in Texas and for the rule of law."\par \par \uc2 "It is the foremost responsibility of the Department of Justice to defend the Constitution," Mr. Garland said. "We will continue to protect constitutional rights against all who would seek to undermine them."\par \par \uc2 The Texas attorney general's office didn't immediately respond to a request for comment but it filed a notice of appeal shortly after the decision was released. The matter will next go to the Fifth U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals, based in New Orleans. In earlier litigation brought by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, that court previously allowed the law to take effect.\par \par \uc2 Amy Hagstrom Miller, founder of Whole Woman's Health, said the organization's four \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics would immediately resume procedures up to 18 weeks of pregnancy. After the law went into effect, the clinics were having to turn away some 80% of patients seeking abortions, Ms. Miller said previously. Wednesday evening, she called the judge's ruling amazing.\par \par \uc2 "It's the justice we've been seeking for weeks," Ms. Miller said.\par \par \uc2 Kimberlyn Schwartz, the spokeswoman for Texas Right to Life, said the organization wasn't surprised by the ruling and called the injunction unprecedented.\par \par \uc2 "We expect a fair shake at the Fifth Court of Appeals," Ms. Schwartz said. "The legacy of Roe v. Wade is you have these judges that will bend over to cater to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  industry."\par \par \uc2 During the month since the law has been in effect, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-prompts-women-to-seek-out-of-state-clinics-11632315616"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 women in Texas have been traveling to other states }}}\uc2  to seek abortions, prompting a spike in patients at clinics in nearby states.\par \par \uc2 The Texas measure is in clear conflict with current Supreme Court precedent protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, but a key question in litigation has been whether a plaintiff could clear early procedural hurdles to get a judge to address the substance of the state restrictions.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department alleged the Texas ban violates the constitutional command that federal law is supreme to state law, as well as the 14th Amendment's guarantee of due process of law, the basis for the Supreme Court's recognition of a constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It also argued the state measure was pre-empted by federal law because it prohibited certain abortions "that federal agencies are charged with facilitating, funding, or reimbursing," including through healthcare programs that cover the procedure in cases of rape or incest. The Texas law includes no exceptions for such cases.\par \par \uc2 The state argued the Justice Department lawsuit was improper and said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/federal-judge-questions-texas-abortion-law-in-case-filed-by-justice-department-11633112921"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 it wasn't possible for the judge to issue a workable injunction }}}\uc2  because there was no person the court could validly prohibit from enforcing the law.\par \par \uc2 Judge Pitman rejected those arguments and found that the Justice Department had a valid basis for suing Texas because the state law prevented federal officials from carrying out their obligations. He also said the federal government could sue to prevent probable violations of citizens' constitutional rights. Texas, he added, couldn't use the law's unusual design to avoid federal judicial review.\par \par \uc2 The judge directed his injunction against the state and any individuals acting on its behalf. It will remain in place while litigation in the case continues unless a higher court steps in to stay it.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Findell and Sadie Gurman contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Brent Kendall at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:brent.kendall@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 brent.kendall@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/federal-judge-blocks-texas-abortion-law-11633568447"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Federal Judge Blocks Texas \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Law }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020211007eha70002t\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
