\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Texas Abortions Pick Up After Federal Judge Allows Them; Clinics say they are moving cautiously because a state law imposing a near ban on abortions allows retroactive lawsuits if the injunction is overturned\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Findell \par \uc2 752 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 October 2021\par 23:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 AUSTIN\u8212\'20\'14Some Texas clinics again began performing abortions later than six weeks Thursday after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/federal-judge-blocks-texas-abortion-law-11633568447"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a federal judge blocked a state law }}}\uc2  imposing a near ban on the procedure, while others are moving more slowly as legal challenges play out.\par \par \uc2 Whole Woman's Health, which operates four Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, said it reached out Wednesday night to women who had been on a waiting list for abortions after being turned away in recent weeks. The Texas Heartbeat Act, also known as SB8, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-takes-effect-pending-supreme-court-action-11630498466"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 went into effect Sept 1 }}}\uc2 . It prohibits abortions after an embryo's cardiac activity can be detected, usually around six weeks into a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 By Thursday morning, women who were more than six weeks pregnant came to Whole Woman's Health clinics for abortions as early as 8 a.m., said Amy Hagstrom Miller, the group's founder.\par \par \uc2 Whole Woman's Health clinics are now busy fielding calls and emails both from patients who were previously turned away and from those who didn't call earlier because their pregnancies were beyond six weeks, Ms. Miller said. Clinics also saw patients for first appointments Thursday and scheduled for abortions on Friday, in keeping with the state's 24-hour waiting period, she said.\par \par \uc2 Many patients are anxious, knowing that the injunction may be in place for only a short window. It will take time to work through backlogged demand, she said.\par \par \uc2 "There's actually hope from patients and staff and there's a little bit of desperation," Ms. Miller said.\par \par \uc2 Despite the injunction, other providers didn't immediately return to performing abortions, fearful of a provision in SB8 that could allow lawsuits to be filed retroactively, clinics said. Many will be comfortable only if the Supreme Court upholds the injunction.\par \par \uc2 Linda Shafer, administrator at Aaron Women's Clinic in Houston, said the clinic's doctor opted to continue to abide by the Texas heartbeat law until an appeals court has ruled. "There's definitely still fear of lawsuits, so we're sticking to the six-week thing," she said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Shafer said sticking to that line is difficult for staff, who have to deal with distraught patients. "There's patients that leave in tears, they can't afford to go anywhere else. It's horrible," she said.\par \par \uc2 In response to a Justice Department legal challenge, U.S. District Judge Robert Pitman in Austin issued a preliminary injunction blocking the law Wednesday, saying the state had pursued "an unprecedented and aggressive scheme to deprive its citizens of a significant and well-established constitutional right."\par \par \uc2 Texas lawmakers designed the measure so that it would be difficult to challenge in court by barring state officials from enforcing the law. Instead, the law delegates enforcement to private parties and authorizes civil damages of $10,000 or more to anyone who successfully sues a defendant accused of performing or aiding in such an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Supporters of the Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law said they would appeal the decision to the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit in hopes of reversing the ruling. They warned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers that they could be sued for abortions that occurred between this week's injunction and any potential reversal of it.\par \par \uc2 "The Texas Heartbeat Act is still in effect," said the bill's author, state \uc2 Sen. Bryan Hughes\uc2 . "Anyone who performs or assists a post-heartbeat \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas remains subject to lawsuits in federal court, and they will also be sued in state court if this injunction is vacated or stayed on appeal."\par \par \uc2 Molly Duane, an attorney at the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  who represents Whole Woman's Health, said the danger of future lawsuits was real because a provision in the original bill says that it isn't a defense for a doctor to rely on the protection of an injunction that is later overturned.\par \par \uc2 "That provision remains a serious concern for physicians and clinics across the state, but what we can tell you today is there are independent providers across the state that are working to open full services and are doing so wary of the fact that the Fifth Circuit may take away this injunction at any moment," Ms. Duane said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Elizabeth Findell at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Elizabeth.Findell@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Elizabeth.Findell@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortions-pick-up-after-federal-judge-allows-them-11633647672"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas Abortions Pick Up After Federal Judge Allows Them }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020211007eha7007bx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
