\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 , 83, has been facing pressure from the left to step down, while Democrats maintain a Senate majority so they can confirm a like-minded successor. PHOTO: Philip Keith for The Wall Street Journal\par \par \uc2 Several coming cases reflect the court's concern for religious expression. In November, the justices will consider a condemned Texas inmate's claim that the state must let his pastor lay hands upon him while he is executed; prison authorities say the request is disruptive.\par \par \uc2 A December argument challenges Maine's public education system, which relies on state tuition vouchers for private schools. Half the state's school districts lack enough students to justify schools of their own, so the state reimburses tuition at secular private schools. Parents who prefer religious schools argue that the tuition-reimbursement program is discriminatory; the state says it should be viewed as equivalent to operating its own schools, which couldn't be sectarian.\par \par \uc2 And last Thursday, the court agreed to consider whether the city of Boston, which occasionally allows outside groups to fly their banners from flagpoles outside City Hall, violated the First Amendment by rejecting a cross-bearing "Christian Flag."\par \par \uc2 Like other workplaces returning to life after coronavirus-driven closings, some things at the high court will be different. Only staff members, attorneys and accredited journalists may enter the courthouse, while the public for the first time can listen live through a link on the court's website. Attorneys are directed to wear N-95 or KN-95 masks within the courtroom "except when presenting argument," but only those with negative Covid-19 tests are allowed in.\par \par \uc2 Justice Kavanaugh, despite his January vaccination, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/justice-brett-kavanaugh-tests-positive-for-covid-19-has-no-symptoms-11633094178"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tested positive Thursday }}}\uc2  and had to skip a Friday ceremony at the courthouse. The court said he would dial in to coming arguments.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What coming Supreme Court case are you most invested in? Why? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 While the court didn't schedule potential blockbusters for its first run of arguments, beginning Monday, it did include cases involving notorious prisoners.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the court will hear the government's bid to block a Guantanamo detainee, Abu Zubaydah, from obtaining depositions from former \uc2 Central Intelligence Agency\uc2  contractors who designed the torture program he allegedly endured at a "black site" in Poland. Mr. Zubaydah wants the testimony for a legal case in Poland, which follows a ruling from the \uc2 European Court of Human Rights\uc2  that Polish authorities breached their treaty obligations. The U.S., asserting that the testimony could disclose state secrets such as the black site's presence in Poland, argues that Mr. Zubaydah's request should be quashed.\par \par \uc2 The following Wednesday, the Justice Department {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-to-consider-boston-marathon-bomber-s-death-sentence-11616420712"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 will seek reinstatement of a death sentence }}}\uc2  for Dzhokhar Tsarnaev, convicted of the 2013 Boston Marathon bombing that killed three and wounded hundreds. A federal appeals court threw out the death sentence after finding the trial judge made several errors, among them insufficiently vetting jurors regarding pretrial publicity.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jess Bravin at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jess.bravin+1@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jess.bravin+1@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Brent Kendall at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:brent.kendall@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 brent.kendall@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-opens-new-term-with-masks-and-in-person-arguments-11633262400"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court's New Term Tackles Guns, \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22 , Religious Expression }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020211003eha3000b5\par }{\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
