\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Judge Temporarily Blocks Parts of New Arizona  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law; Sections would have criminalized abortions performed because of genetic abnormalities\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jennifer Calfas \par \uc2 804 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 September 2021\par 17:26 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A federal judge temporarily blocked part of a new Arizona law that would have allowed felony charges to be brought against doctors who performed abortions based only on genetic abnormalities like Down syndrome.\par \par \uc2 In an order late Tuesday, hours before the legislation was slated to take effect, U.S. District Judge Douglas Rayes said that section of the Arizona law was likely unconstitutional and would make it more difficult for women seeking abortions due to genetic abnormalities to know their rights.\par \par \uc2 Judge Rayes said it would "place a substantial obstacle" in the way of patients and could hinder their relationship with their doctor. He said few \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in the state perform the procedure later in pregnancy, around the time when a genetic diagnosis could be determined.\par \par \uc2 The judge also blocked another section of the law that would have allowed anyone who raised or provided funding for such abortions to be charged as well.\par \par \uc2 The new Arizona law took effect Wednesday. Signed by Republican \uc2 Gov. Doug Ducey\uc2  in April, it includes other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulations like barring doctors or providers from sending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -inducing medication to patients by mail.\par \par \uc2 Two Arizona-based physicians and state-based organizations, represented by the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2  and the ACLU of Arizona, sued the state in August, arguing the law violated a person's constitutional rights to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They said the decision to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  can be complex.\par \par \uc2 "This ruling is a victory for Arizonans," said Ruth Harlow, senior staff attorney at the ACLU Reproductive Freedom Project, which represented plaintiffs in the case. "Politicians shouldn't have the power to approve or reject our personal medical decisions and charge physicians with felonies for serving their patients."\par \par \uc2 Supporters of the law say deciding to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  based solely on a fetus's genetic abnormalities is discriminatory.\par \par \uc2 "We remain confident the law will be upheld and ruled enforceable in its entirety," said \uc2 Cathi Herrod\uc2 , president of the Center for Arizona Policy, which supported the legislation. The office of Arizona Attorney General \uc2 Mark Brnovich\uc2 , a defendant in the case, didn't immediately respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 When Mr. Ducey signed the legislation in April, the governor said it furthered and enhanced protections for fetuses. "There's immeasurable value in every single life\u8212\'20\'14regardless of genetic makeup," he said.\par \par \uc2 Judge Rayes's order Tuesday didn't block part of the law that states fetuses, at any stage of development, would have the same civil rights as any person in Arizona. The judge, who was nominated to the bench by President Obama, said that section would have to be challenged in state courts depending on how it is enforced.\par \par \uc2 Several states in recent years have passed laws restricting or barring abortions based solely on genetic abnormalities like Down syndrome. In June, North Carolina Gov. Roy Cooper, a Democrat, vetoed similar legislation passed by the state legislature.\par \par \uc2 In April, a federal appeals court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/federal-appeals-court-upholds-ohios-down-syndrome-abortion-law-11618348943"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 upheld an Ohio law }}}\uc2  that prevents physicians from providing abortions to patients who said they were seeking the procedure due to a fetus's potential genetic disorder. It was the first time a federal appellate court said this kind of law didn't violate a constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Several months later in June, a different {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/missouris-down-syndrome-abortion-law-blocked-11623274315"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 federal appeals court blocked Missouri }}}\uc2  from enforcing a 2019 law that made it illegal for a physician to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after learning a patient sought the procedure due to fears the fetus had Down syndrome. The decision cited Supreme Court precedent that bars states from restricting abortions before a fetus has reached the stage of viability, which means it can survive outside of the womb.\par \par \uc2 A highly anticipated case before the Supreme Court later this year could lead to the most important ruling on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Roe v. Wade in 1973. The court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/in-mississippi-abortion-case-supreme-court-sets-arguments-for-december-11632176939"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 will begin hearing arguments Dec. 1 }}}\uc2  over a Mississippi law banning most abortions after 15 weeks, which is earlier than the standard previously set by the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 In a 5-4 vote in early September, the Supreme Court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-rejects-emergency-request-to-block-texas-six-week-abortion-ban-11630556283"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 allowed a Texas law }}}\uc2  barring abortions after about six weeks of pregnancy to take effect, prohibiting most procedures in the state. The most restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law in effect in the U.S., the Texas law left {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/as-new-texas-abortion-ban-law-takes-effect-providers-rush-to-adjust-11630521388"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  providers scrambling }}}\uc2  to adjust to the new rules and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-prompts-women-to-seek-out-of-state-clinics-11632315616"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 people seeking abortions to search for care outside of the state }}}\uc2 . The Justice Department {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-administration-expected-to-file-suit-soon-challenging-texas-abortion-law-11631205764?mod=article_inline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sued Texas over the law }}}\uc2  earlier this month. A federal judge {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/justice-department-asks-federal-judge-to-quickly-block-texas-abortion-law-11631684768"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 set an Oct. 1 hearing }}}\uc2  for the case.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jennifer Calfas at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jennifer.calfas@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jennifer.calfas@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/judge-temporarily-blocks-parts-of-new-arizona-abortion-law-11632936417"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Judge Temporarily Blocks Parts of New Arizona \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Law }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020210929eh9t003e9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
