\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Texas  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law Prompts Women to Seek Out-of-State Clinics; Louisiana, Oklahoma facilities report influx of patients from Texas looking for abortions and other services\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Findell \par \uc2 916 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 September 2021\par 13:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 Staff members at Hope Medical Group for Women are booked more than three weeks out and have begun adding some evening hours to get patients in for appointments as quickly as possible. An earlier version of this article incorrectly referred to the center as Kathaleen Pittman. Separately, Texas Alliance for Life is supportive of the Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law. An earlier version of this article incorrectly said that the group advocated for the law. (Corrected on Sept. 22)\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN, Texas\u8212\'20\'14Women's health clinics in Louisiana, Oklahoma and beyond are reporting an influx of out-of-state patients from Texas looking for abortions and other services, weeks after the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  allowed Texas's restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law to take effect.\par \par \uc2 Some clinics in neighboring states said they are overwhelmed with out-of-state patients. They are adding hours to try to handle the backlog, administrators said. In Texas, meanwhile, clinics are turning most of their patients away and losing their staff.\par \par \uc2 The migration out of state follows the enactment of the Texas Heartbeat Act, which became effective Sept. 1 after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-rejects-emergency-request-to-block-texas-six-week-abortion-ban-11630556283"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Supreme Court turned away a legal challenge }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The law bans abortions after "cardiac activity" can be detected, usually around six weeks of gestation, and it deputizes private citizens to sue anyone they believe may have aided such a procedure and collect $10,000. On Monday, a Texas doctor {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-doctor-who-performed-abortion-is-sued-in-test-of-new-law-11632176035"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 who publicly said he performed an \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  was sued in state court by two different plaintiffs, handing Texas the first tests of the law.\par \par \uc2 In Shreveport, La., about 200 miles east of Dallas, staff members at Hope Medical Group for Women are booked more than three weeks out and have begun adding some evening hours to get patients in for appointments as quickly as possible, said administrator Kathaleen Pittman.\par \par \uc2 "Because of the delay, they're going to be farther along," Ms. Pittman said of the effect of the new Texas law. Most patients she sees report their reason for terminating a pregnancy is financial, she said, and many are struggling with the cost of travel to Louisiana and taking time off work for the two required appointments.\par \par \uc2 In Tulsa, Okla., at least 70% of patients at Tulsa Women's Clinic are now from Texas, said executive administrator Andrea Gallegos. Some are coming from as far as the Rio Grande Valley, a 12-hour drive away. The clinic has tripled its number of daily appointments to about 35 and added some Saturday hours, Ms. Gallegos said.\par \par \uc2 The average Texas woman of childbearing age is 17 miles from an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in-state, but 247 miles from one out-of-state, according to an analysis from the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a research group supportive of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Joe Pojman, executive director of Texas Alliance for Life, a group that is supportive of the Texas law, said the state has increased funding by $20 million to assist women who carry pregnancies to term. "Texas has vast resources to help a woman so that no woman seeks an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas or out of state because she has no alternatives," he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Pojman said at least 20 of the state's 23 licensed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers are operating, but his organization doesn't know how many abortions are being performed.\par \par \uc2 Clinics in Texas report that many staff members have quit since the law took effect, saying they are worried about the possibility of being sued, even if they complied fully with the new law. "My staff are scared to come to work. I usually have eight. I have two or three today," said Linda Shafer, administrator of Aaron Women's Clinic in Houston.\par \par \uc2 Amy Hagstrom Miller, founder of Whole Woman's Health, which operates four clinics in North, Central and South Texas, said the clinics are now denying abortions to an estimated 80% of patients they have seen since the new law went into effect, turning them away after an ultrasound. The most the clinics can do at that point is share with patients a few websites listing other options, Ms. Miller said. "They're leaving the clinic without a plan," she said of patients.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 Do you think the Texas law banning most abortions is constitutional? Why or why not? Join the conversation below.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and care advocates said that more women are turning to self-administered methods, like using \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills ordered online. Ms. Miller said most of Texas's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics are small, independent clinics that may be forced to close permanently if the law remains in place.\par \par \uc2 One of the areas most affected is the Rio Grande Valley, the Texas region farthest from any other state and hemmed in by Mexico to the south and immigration checkpoints to the north. That prevents anyone in the country illegally from traveling for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said \uc2 Lucy Felix\uc2 , a Brownsville-based outreach coordinator for the National Latina Institute for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "For our undocumented immigrant communities, forced pregnancy is now the law of the state," she said.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the suits filed Monday, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-administration-expected-to-file-suit-soon-challenging-texas-abortion-law-11631205764"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the U.S. Justice Department has sued Texas }}}\uc2  seeking to block the law, with a hearing set for Oct. 1.\par \par \uc2 Write to Elizabeth Findell at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Elizabeth.Findell@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Elizabeth.Findell@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-prompts-women-to-seek-out-of-state-clinics-11632315616"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Law Prompts Women to Seek Out-of-State Clinics }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020210922eh9m001mf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
