\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Texas Doctor Who Performed  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Is Sued in Test of New Law; Arkansas man says he sued in hopes of collecting $10,000 or challenging the law; other suit is filed by Illinois man described as 'pro choice plaintiff'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Findell and Jacob Gershman \par \uc2 743 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 September 2021\par 00:14 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 AUSTIN, Texas\u8212\'20\'14A Texas doctor who publicly said he performed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was sued Monday in state court by two different plaintiffs, handing Texas the first tests of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-in-court-what-you-need-to-know-11631730720?mod=article_inline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 its new \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  law }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Texas Heartbeat Act bans abortions after "cardiac activity" can be detected, usually around six weeks of gestation, and it deputizes private citizens to sue anyone they believe may have aided such a procedure and collect $10,000. It went into effect Sept. 1.\par \par \uc2 Alan Braid, a San Antonio physician, said in a Washington Post opinion essay Saturday that he had performed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in defiance of the law, widely known as SB 8, earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 A former Arkansas lawyer, Oscar Stilley, who said he is on home confinement serving time after a tax-fraud conviction, filed a civil complaint against the doctor Monday in Bexar County District Court. He said he decided to sue the doctor after he read about the case early Monday morning and wanted to test the Texas law, which doesn't require plaintiffs to be state residents.\par \par \uc2 "The doctor is going to get sued," Mr. Stilley said. "Someone is going to get $10,000 off him. If that's the law, I may as well get the money. If it's not the law, let's go to court and get it sorted out."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Stilley said he is neither antiabortion nor in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, and not affiliated with any \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related political groups. He said he considers the Texas statute to be an "end run" around established law and wants to see a clear ruling on whether it is legitimate.\par \par \uc2 In a separate lawsuit, Felipe N. Gomez, an Illinois resident who is described in his filing as a "pro choice plaintiff," filed a complaint Monday morning in Bexar County. While the complaint is against Dr. Braid, it says Mr. Gomez believes the Texas law to be illegal and asks a court to strike it down. He said that he wasn't interested in collecting any money.\par \par \uc2 "I'm against having someone tell me I have to get a shot or wear a mask and the same people who agree with me on that\u8212\'20\'14the GOP\u8212\'20\'14tell people what they can do with their bodies on the other hand," Mr. Gomez said. "It's inconsistent."\par \par \uc2 Dr. Braid said in his essay that he understood he could face legal consequences for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  he performed. "I wanted to make sure that Texas didn't get away with its bid to prevent this blatantly unconstitutional law from being tested," he wrote.\par \par \uc2 The doctor declined to comment further through a spokesperson with the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights group representing him in his capacity as a plaintiff in a pending federal lawsuit against SB 8.\par \par \uc2 Legal scholars say Dr. Braid and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates representing him could potentially try to turn a lawsuit against the doctor to their advantage.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Braid could try to sue an SB 8 claimant, contending that such people are acting as government agents under color of state law and subject to federal civil-rights laws that could protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, according to Michael Masinter, a professor of law emeritus at Nova Southeastern University in Florida and an expert on civil procedure and civil-rights litigation. The professor said he was skeptical that such a lawsuit could succeed.\par \par \uc2 John Seago, legislative director for Texas Right to Life, hasn't sued Dr. Braid or any other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider. He said the group is wary of bringing a lawsuit against Dr. Braid without more certitude that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  the doctor performed was unlawful under SB 8.\par \par \uc2 "We want to make sure it's someone who violated the law and is worth the time to go into a lawsuit," Mr. Seago said.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Braid didn't explicitly say himself that he tested for a fetal heartbeat as required by law or that he carried out the procedure after detecting cardiac activity, as the law prohibits.\par \par \uc2 Write to Elizabeth Findell at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Elizabeth.Findell@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Elizabeth.Findell@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Jacob Gershman at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jacob.gershman@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jacob.gershman@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-doctor-who-performed-abortion-is-sued-in-test-of-new-law-11632176035"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas Doctor Who Performed \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Is Sued in Test of New Law }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020210920eh9k005sd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
