\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Texas  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law Faces Pushback From Some Companies; \uc2 Lyft\uc2 , Box, \uc2 Stitch Fix\uc2  among dozens of companies to sign statement; some large Texas employers declined to sign\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chip Cutter \par \uc2 1,014 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 September 2021\par 12:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dozens of businesses are going public with their opposition to a new {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-in-court-what-you-need-to-know-11631730720"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas law that bars \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  after about six weeks of pregnancy, a move that follows weeks of debate inside companies about how to respond.\par \par \uc2 Employers including ride-sharing service \uc2 Lyft Inc\uc2 ., cloud-storage company \uc2 Box Inc\uc2 ., online fashion retailer \uc2 Stitch Fix Inc\uc2 . and investment group Trillium Asset Management LLC signed a statement set to be released Tuesday that says "restricting access to comprehensive reproductive care, including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , threatens the health, independence, and economic stability of our workers and customers."\par \par \uc2 Some companies declined to participate. They included \uc2 Starbucks Corp\uc2 . and \uc2 Microsoft Corp\uc2 ., according to people familiar with the matter. A Microsoft spokeswoman declined to comment. A Starbucks spokesman didn't immediately comment.\par \par \uc2 The statement doesn't call for any specific action. Its organizers say it is intended, in part, to show other states considering new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws that they can cause economic harm, such as by hindering employers' ability to recruit workers from out of state. Many of the signatories aren't based in Texas, though a number of them have operations or employees in the state.\par \par \uc2 For some companies, the statement represents their first public comments on the Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law. Many employers have been largely silent since it went into effect earlier this month. Some worried about taking a stand on the issue, fearing potential blowback from customers or employees, executives said. Others, like the Greater Houston Partnership, a business group, waded into polarizing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/relationship-between-houston-business-leaders-and-politicians-fractures-over-texas-voting-access-11620346397"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 debates on voting access }}}\uc2  earlier this year that divided their members, and haven't taken a position on the Texas Heartbeat Act, also known as SB 8.\par \par \uc2 The law, passed by the Republican-controlled Texas legislature and signed by Gov. Greg Abbott in May, says a physician can't knowingly perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if there is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-what-to-know-as-supreme-court-allows-it-to-take-effect-11630606238"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 "detectable fetal heartbeat." }}}\uc2  It also creates a new enforcement structure that allows private citizens to bring a civil lawsuit against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and to collect at least $10,000 in damages plus legal costs per \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  challenged successfully.\par \par \uc2 Gov. Abbott has said in recent weeks that businesses aren't turned off by the state's policies.\par \par \uc2 Salesforce.com Inc\uc2 ., which has an office in Dallas, offered to relocate employees in Texas who are concerned about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/justice-department-asks-federal-judge-to-quickly-block-texas-abortion-law-11631684768"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 access to reproductive healthcare }}}\uc2  in the state. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/benioff/status/1436508394194718720"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 In a tweet, }}}\uc2  CEO \uc2 Marc Benioff\uc2  wrote, "Ohana if you want to move we'll help you exit TX. Your choice," using the Hawaiian word for family. CNBC earlier reported on the policy. A Salesforce spokeswoman declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Shar Dubey\uc2 , CEO of Dallas-based online-dating company \uc2 Match Group Inc\uc2 ., sent a note to employees in which she expressed her opposition to the law. Ms. Dubey also created a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/after-texas-abortion-law-match-ceo-bumble-create-funds-to-support-those-affected-11630617641"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fund for those affected }}}\uc2  by the law, as did Austin-based online dating company \uc2 Bumble Inc\uc2 . Bumble signed the letter. Match didn't.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday's corporate statement frames the debate in largely economic terms. It says that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restriction "impairs our ability to build diverse and inclusive workforce pipelines, recruit top talent across states, and protect the well-being of all the people who keep our businesses thriving day in and out."\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 What impact, if any, will the corporate statement have on the Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 A coalition called Don't Ban Equality organized the statement, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://dontbanequality.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 was signed by more than 50 companies }}}\uc2 . Other signatories include \uc2 Yelp Inc\uc2 ., the customer-service platform \uc2 Zendesk Inc\uc2 . and enterprise software company \uc2 Atlassian Corp\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Laws like these are just bad for the business climate of the state," said Jen Stark, senior director of corporate strategy for the Tara Health Foundation, which helped organize the statement.\par \par \uc2 In recent years, companies have faced {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/with-georgia-voting-law-the-business-of-business-becomes-politics-11618027250"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pressure to respond to social issues }}}\uc2  related to the environment, racial justice and LGBT rights. Progressive activists have frequently used companies' past statements on such issues to push them to further engage on the topics, while CEOs have also faced calls from their own employees to make their views known. Some employees and customers also don't want companies taking positions on these issues.\par \par \uc2 Dell Technologies Inc. CEO \uc2 Michael Dell\uc2 , in an email viewed by The Wall Street Journal, told Texas-based employees earlier this month that the company was reviewing recent legislation in the state. "There is a lot happening in Texas right now. We're all feeling it," Mr. Dell wrote, adding, "There's much we still don't know about how all of these laws will ultimately play out."\par \par \uc2 Yelp\uc2 , which employs more than 100 people in Texas but doesn't have an office in the state, plans to announce that the company's foundation will double match employee contributions in October to groups like the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Planned Parenthood that are fighting the Texas law and similar legislation elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 Some employers that signed on to Tuesday's letter said they generally tried to avoid introducing politics into the workplace but felt drawn to support the statement. At Spot Insurance Inc., an Austin insurance technology startup, Maria Goy, a co-founder and chief operating officer, sent a text to the company's CEO, saying she felt the company should sign the letter. He agreed.\par \par \uc2 Other CEOs said they signed the statement because they worried Texas's law would make it harder to attract people to their organizations. Trevor Best, the Houston-based CEO of Syzygy Plasmonics Inc., which focuses on developing technologies for the energy industry, said his 40-person company routinely recruits engineers and Ph.D. candidates from other states.\par \par \uc2 "I'm trying to get them to move to Texas, and they see this, and they have to think about their families and their daughters, and is this where they want to grow their family?" Mr. Best said. "It's challenging."\par \par \uc2 Write to Chip Cutter at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:chip.cutter@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 chip.cutter@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-faces-pushback-from-some-companies-11632225602"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Law Faces Pushback From Some Companies }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020210921eh9l0012z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
