\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Why Texas  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Opponents Aren't Suing Dr. Alan Braid Over New Law; Physician who said he is first to violate new restrictions doesn't present appealing case for conservative groups, legal scholars say\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jacob Gershman \par \uc2 851 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 September 2021\par 22:14 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 As {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-what-to-know-as-supreme-court-allows-it-to-take-effect-11630606238"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas' \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban }}}\uc2  was set to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-takes-effect-pending-supreme-court-action-11630498466"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 take effect Sept. 1 }}}\uc2 , the state's largest antiabortion group implored residents to bring lawsuits against violators of the law. Since then, Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents have charted a more cautious course.\par \par \uc2 Antiabortion activists haven't sued the physician who last weekend {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-doctor-says-he-defied-state-law-by-performing-abortion-11632090167"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said publicly that he was defying }}}\uc2  the Republican-led Texas Heartbeat Act, also known as SB 8, saying they have little incentive to make a test case out of his actions. The doctor, Alan Braid, practices in San Antonio and said he performed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  this month that exceeded the new restrictions.\par \par \uc2 "We're fully committed to bring lawsuits if needed. We just don't see the need yet," said John Seago, legislative director for Texas Right to Life, which in the days after the bill became law had promised in fundraising letters to "sue abortionists who violate the law."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Texas say they are complying with the law, making Dr. Braid an apparently isolated exception. In the eyes of Texas Right to Life and its allies, the law effectively bans most abortions, and they have little to gain by bringing a lawsuit against Dr. Braid, said Douglas Laycock, a University of Virginia constitutional law professor.\par \par \uc2 "The whole point of the statute is to make it impossible to litigate the constitutionality of the ban on anything after six weeks," said Prof. Laycock. "And so far that's working."\par \par \uc2 Texas Right to Life's reluctance to file suit stems from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/behind-texas-abortion-law-an-attorneys-unusual-enforcement-idea-11630762683"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the unusual nature of SB 8 }}}\uc2 . The law bars physicians and others from knowingly performing or aiding an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if there is "a detectable fetal heartbeat," including embryonic cardiac activity that appears about six weeks into a pregnancy\u8212\'20\'14a period before many women know they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2 Unlike other \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans enacted and struck down elsewhere, SB 8 puts ordinary Texans, instead of government officials, in charge of enforcement, empowering anyone in the country to file lawsuits against alleged violators in Texas. That makes it procedurally cumbersome for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates to get a state or federal court to invalidate the law.\par \par \uc2 In a Washington Post {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/opinions/2021/09/18/texas-abortion-provider-alan-braid/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 opinion essay }}}\uc2  published Saturday, Dr. Braid wrote that he provided an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  this month to a woman in her first trimester that went "beyond the state's new limit" and that he understood there could be legal consequences. He declined to comment through a spokesperson with the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights group representing Dr. Braid pro bono as one of the plaintiffs in a pending federal lawsuit against the Texas law.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights proponents said Dr. Braid is taking a stand against what they view as an intolerable status quo, effectively daring \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents to sue him. "Essentially, Dr. Braid is calling their bluff," said David Donatti, an attorney with the ACLU of Texas.\par \par \uc2 A pair of disbarred attorneys outside the state {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-doctor-who-performed-abortion-is-sued-in-test-of-new-law-11632176035"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have brought claims against Dr. Braid }}}\uc2  in a Texas court, though neither is an antiabortion activist and one said he believes the law is illegal and should be struck down.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Braid might prevail in a lawsuit against him, but it is very possible that the effect of such a victory would be limited, said Stephen Vladeck, a constitutional scholar at University of Texas School of Law. A judgment for Dr. Braid would be unlikely to have sweeping statewide implications for others seeking to sue \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, he said.\par \par \uc2 Texas' Republican-controlled statehouse passed the law at a time when the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is under new scrutiny. In a case reviewing the 1973 Roe v. Wade precedent, the Supreme Court is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/in-mississippi-abortion-case-supreme-court-sets-arguments-for-december-11632176939"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hearing arguments Dec. 1 on Mississippi's bid }}}\uc2  to reinstate its ban on most abortions after 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Even if a court temporarily halts enforcement of SB 8 on constitutional grounds, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers could still be exposed to heavy damages and legal fees should the Supreme Court later rollback Roe v. Wade, according to legal scholars. SB 8 gives claimants four years to file a lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department has sued Texas, asking a federal court to declare SB 8 unconstitutional and prohibit anyone from suing under SB 8. A federal court has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/justice-department-asks-federal-judge-to-quickly-block-texas-abortion-law-11631684768"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 scheduled a hearing in the case for Oct. 1 }}}\uc2 . Another pending federal lawsuit led by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights organizations\u8212\'20\'14now before the Fifth U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals\u8212\'20\'14is also seeking a broad injunction.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Seago, of Texas Right to Life, said his organization is hesitant to take Dr. Braid to court without knowing more about what he did, saying it isn't clear that he even violated the statute.\par \par \uc2 "The first lawsuits that are filed should be when there is credible evidence that a violation has occurred," he said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jacob Gershman at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jacob.gershman@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jacob.gershman@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/why-texas-abortion-opponents-arent-suing-dr-alan-braid-over-new-law-11632262448"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Why Texas \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Opponents Aren't Suing Dr. Alan Braid Over New Law }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020210921eh9l0063h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
