\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Texas Doctor Says He Defied State Law by Performing  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 ; Antiabortion group is looking into the case of Alan Braid, who said he terminated a pregnancy after six weeks\b0\par\par\uc2 By Christine Mai-Duc \par \uc2 616 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 September 2021\par 22:22 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 An antiabortion group in Texas says it is looking into a physician's claim that he defied {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-in-court-what-you-need-to-know-11631730720"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a state law }}}\uc2  that recently took effect by performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a woman past the sixth week of her pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 In an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/opinions/2021/09/18/texas-abortion-provider-alan-braid/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 opinion essay published in the Washington Post }}}\uc2 , Alan Braid disclosed that he performed the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in September.\par \par \uc2 John Seago, legislative director for Texas Right to Life, said the organization is looking into Dr. Braid's claims. "It definitely seems like a legal stunt and we are looking into whether it is more than that," said Mr. Seago. He noted that the law contains a four-year statute of limitations for citizens to sue for violations.\par \par \uc2 In his essay published Saturday, Dr. Braid wrote that he understood he could face legal consequences. "I wanted to make sure that Texas didn't get away with its bid to prevent this blatantly unconstitutional law from being tested," he wrote.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Braid's act could become the first test case of S.B. 8, also known as the Texas Heartbeat Law, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-governor-signs-fetal-heartbeat-abortion-ban-11621452346?page=1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 signed by Republican Gov. Greg Abbott }}}\uc2  in May.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  earlier this month declined to block enforcement of the law, rejecting an emergency request by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics on a 5-4 vote. The Biden administration the next week {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-administration-expected-to-file-suit-soon-challenging-texas-abortion-law-11631205764"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sued to block the law }}}\uc2 , saying it was unconstitutional. A hearing in the case is set for Oct. 1.\par \par \uc2 The law bars doctors from performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  once there is a "detectable fetal heartbeat," which typically happens around six weeks of pregnancy, before some women realize they are pregnant. It provides an exception for medical emergencies that could affect the health of the mother, but not for pregnancies resulting from rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 Under the law's provisions, private citizens can sue \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, doctors, and anyone who helps a woman obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and collect a minimum $10,000 in damages plus reimbursed legal costs if they are successful.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Braid's Houston clinic is already among the plaintiffs in a lawsuit challenging S.B. 8 in federal court. He declined to comment through a spokeswoman for the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is representing him in the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 "Dr. Braid has courageously stood up against this blatantly unconstitutional law," \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2 , the group's president, said in a statement. "We stand ready to defend him against the vigilante lawsuits that S.B. 8 threatens to unleash against those providing or supporting access to constitutionally protected \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care."\par \par \uc2 Dr. Braid, who has been practicing in Texas for more than 40 years and has run clinics in Houston, San Antonio, and Oklahoma, has a history of fighting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 When a 2013 law mandated that doctors perform abortions at hospitals or surgical centers and required providers to have admitting privileges locally, he built a $3 million facility in San Antonio so he could continue providing services there. The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  later struck down most of that law's provisions.\par \par \uc2 He was previously a named plaintiff in cases targeting a 2011 Texas law that requires women seeking abortions to first receive a sonogram, the 2017 Texas law mandating that fetal remains be buried or cremated, and a 2019 Oklahoma law that required doctors to tell patients that the effects of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drugs could be reversed, which medical groups said was false.\par \par \uc2 Write to Christine Mai-Duc at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:christine.maiduc@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 christine.maiduc@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-doctor-says-he-defied-state-law-by-performing-abortion-11632090167"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas Doctor Says He Defied State Law by Performing \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020210919eh9j0035x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
