\par\par \uc2 Commentary (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The Texas  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law Is Unconventional Because It Had to Be; After Roe v. Wade, many people think  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  is off limits to democracy. It isn't.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bryan Hughes \par \uc2 836 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 September 2021\par 20:24 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 There has been so much ill-informed commentary on Texas Senate Bill 8, the Heartbeat Act, that I feel compelled to explain its provisions and defend its logic. I am the author of the bill, which Gov. Greg Abbott signed in May.\par \par \uc2 The law does not ban abortions after six weeks. It requires that a physician performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  first check for a fetal heartbeat. If there is a heartbeat, the physician may not abort the child. When a physician performs an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without checking for a heartbeat, or finds a heartbeat and performs the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  anyway, he has performed an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Unlike other such laws passed in other parts of the country, the Heartbeat Act does not empower any governmental authority to mete out punishment for the crime. Instead it decrees that the doctor may be sued for breaking the law.\par \par \uc2 The mother cannot be sued, and we have bolstered programs to support expectant mothers. Last year the state's Alternatives to \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  program provided support to more than 100,000 pregnant women and adoptive parents through counseling, classes, car seats, diapers and other necessities. This year we added more than $20 million to the program, bringing total funding to over $100 million.\par \par \uc2 The law departs from conventional enforcement channels, obviously. Most 19th- and 20th-century \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws prohibited or regulated the practice in the traditional way. But that is much harder to do thanks to the Supreme Court's Roe v. Wade decision of 1973\u8212\'20\'14a ruling acknowledged even by liberal scholars to be a travesty of constitutional interpretation.\par \par \uc2 One of the numerous unfortunate consequences of Roe is that many people mistakenly believe any regulation on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  must be illegitimate. This is not so. The Supreme Court does not have the power to declare subjects off limits to democratically elected legislatures.\par \par \uc2 Yet prosecutors from across the country\u8212\'20\'14including district attorneys from Dallas, Bexar, Nueces and Fort Bend counties in Texas\u8212\'20\'14have announced their refusal to enforce laws regulating \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even before they become law and before they're litigated. Even if Roe is overturned, they say, they won't enforce democratically passed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws. If officials sworn to enforce state laws pre-emptively decide they won't do it, even when the laws are passed and ratified and have not been challenged in the courts, state legislators are obliged to get creative.\par \par \uc2 Many crimes have a civil analog. Someone who commits a criminal assault, for instance, may be sued in civil court for assault and battery (recall the civil O.J. Simpson trial). Someone who steals property from another may be pursued for the civil tort of conversion. In almost every case, the person wronged, and therefore the person who brings the claim, is the plaintiff.\par \par \uc2 In the case of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the wronged party has been extinguished. If we can't depend on criminal enforcement, even if Roe is overturned, and the party who directly suffered harm cannot bring a claim, what's left? Someone else must enforce the law.\par \par \uc2 In contexts other than \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , citizens often sue to enforce laws that are otherwise difficult for the government to enforce through traditional channels. "Qui tam" actions, in which an individual sues on behalf of himself and the people, were enacted in the U.S. as early as the first Congress. Texas law, for example, allows individuals to sue on behalf of the state to recover money taken by Medicaid fraud.\par \par \uc2 A common objection against the Heartbeat Act warns that left-leaning states may use similar laws to punish behavior progressives don't like. The Journal's editorial board {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-blunder-supreme-court-samuel-alito-john-roberts-whole-womans-health-11630619631"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 asks}}}\uc2 : "Could California allow private citizens to sue individuals for hate speech? Or New York deputize private lawsuits against gun owners?" Those are fair questions, but Texans are not responsible for what progressive politicians in California and New York do. In any case, the right to keep and bear arms and the right to free speech are literally present in the U.S. Constitution, whereas the "right" to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a fantasy spun by the ludicrous logic of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 I believe life begins at conception, and I believe most Texans are in line with that understanding of human personhood. If they are not, we have free and fair elections in which they can make their differences understood. Elected officials in other states may take a different view and they are not beholden to Texas' voters.\par \par \uc2 The Heartbeat Act was necessary because Roe v. Wade attempted to take the question of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of the hands of American democracy. Like it or not, states will keep crafting unconventional means of regulating \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until the Supreme Court puts the question back where it belongs.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Hughes represents District 1 in the Texas Senate.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-unconventional-lawsuit-pro-life-roe-v-wade-heartbeat-six-weeks-11631471508"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Texas \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Law Is Unconventional Because It Had to Be}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020210912eh9c002bd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
