\par\par \uc2 Review & Outlook (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 's Texas Two-Step; The AG files a lawsuit as dubious as the  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  law he wants to stop.\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 396 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2021\par 22:53 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Justice Department sued in federal court Thursday to block the new Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, and its main result will be to prove that both parties are willing to abuse the law to serve their political ends on this fraught issue. It's another low moment for Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , as he seems dedicated again to satisfying the political demands of the White House.\par \par \uc2 As we {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-blunder-supreme-court-samuel-alito-john-roberts-whole-womans-health-11630619631"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote last week}}}\uc2 , the Texas statute violates Roe v. Wade and does so in sneaky fashion by rewarding private actors who sue anyone who assists in an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks of pregnancy. The goal was to evade judicial review in federal court by not having anyone in government as the enforcer. The law is nonetheless almost certain to be struck down as soon as someone attempts to enforce it, which so far no one has.\par \par \uc2 That doesn't justify Mr. Garland's decision to twist federal law and the Constitution to sue Texas. "The United States has the authority and responsibility to ensure that Texas cannot evade its obligations under the Constitution and deprive individuals of their constitutional rights by adopting a statutory scheme designed specifically to evade traditional mechanisms of federal judicial review," says the Justice Department's suit.\par \par \uc2 Sorry, the feds can't sue to block a state law if there is no case or controversy. Justice has no standing to sue. The Justice lawsuit asserts a federal interest because the Texas statute "conflicts with federal law by purporting to prohibit federal agencies from carrying out their responsibilities under federal law related to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services."\par \par \uc2 But the federal government is not an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider. The sovereign interests of the federal government aren't implicated under the Constitution's Supremacy Clause.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Garland suggests that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers are chilled by the law's lawsuit threat. They have no reason to be. They should consult an attorney, who would tell them that the minute someone tries to enforce the law, they can move to dismiss the law as unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 That's the way to defeat the Texas statute\u8212\'20\'14the legal way\u8212\'20\'14not with a patently political lawsuit that should embarrass the former federal judge who now runs Justice.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/merrick-garland-texas-two-step-department-of-justice-abortion-law-lawsuit-11631224546"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Merrick Garland's Texas Two-Step}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020210909eh990086h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
