\par\par \uc2 Latin America News\par \uc2 World\par \b \uc2 Mexico's Supreme Court Decriminalizes  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  in Historic Shift; In a unanimous ruling, the justices pave way for changes in country with world's second-largest Roman Catholic population\b0\par\par\uc2 By Santiago P\u233\'00\'E9rez and David Luhnow \par \uc2 842 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 September 2021\par 00:32 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY\u8212\'20\'14Mexico's Supreme Court unanimously ruled that making \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a crime was unconstitutional, paving the way for far greater access to legal abortions in the country with the world's second-largest Roman Catholic population.\par \par \uc2 In a 10-0 vote, Mexico's justices struck down several provisions of law in the northern states of Sinaloa and Coahuila. The law in Coahuila, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-what-to-know-as-supreme-court-allows-it-to-take-effect-11630606238"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shares a border with Texas  }}}\uc2 , called for one to three years in prison for anyone who terminates a pregnancy or assists in the procedure. The law in Sinaloa defined life as beginning at conception.\par \par \uc2 While the decision only addressed the laws in those two states, the justices said it would establish a precedent that applies to all states, paving the way for changes in the law across the country of 127 million.\par \par \uc2 "From now on, it will not be possible, without violating the criteria of the Court and the Constitution, to prosecute any woman who has an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the circumstances that this Court has considered valid," said Mexico chief justice Arturo Zald\u237\'00\'EDvar.\par \par \uc2 The ruling could be a watershed moment for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access across Latin America. In December, Argentina became the region's first large country {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/abortion-once-unthinkable-in-argentina-becomes-legal-11609314445"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to make \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  legal  }}}\uc2 , joining Uruguay, Cuba and Guyana. A symbol of the movement in Argentina\u8212\'20\'14green scarves and handkerchiefs worn by women\u8212\'20\'14has spread to countries including Mexico and Brazil.\par \par \uc2 As in the U.S., the issue is deeply divisive. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is largely restricted in much of Latin America, including Brazil, the country with the largest Roman Catholic population. A decision on the issue is expected from the nation's Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 "Mexico's decision represents a turning point that Latin America and the Caribbean are making in recognizing women's rights to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a matter of fundamental rights and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Debora Diniz, an anthropologist and founder of a Brazilian women's rights organization that brought the case in Brazil.\par \par \uc2 A month after the Argentina move, Honduras's congress enshrined its antiabortion law in the Constitution and required that any future change would need three-fourths approval from congress. Most surveys in Brazil show a majority are still against changing the laws to allow legal abortions.\par \par \uc2 Before the ruling, Mexico's Episcopal Conference, the main body of the Catholic Church in Mexico, said Sunday that the law should protect human beings from the moment of conception. "Human beings must be recognized with dignity during all stages of life," it said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 In some countries including El Salvador, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal even in cases of rape or incest and often prosecuted. In June, El Salvador released a 30-year-old woman who had been in prison for nine years on charges that she tried to abort her unborn baby. At age 20, when she was eight months' pregnant, she slipped and fell at home, according to her family's testimony. They took the unconscious girl to the hospital, where the fetus died. The woman was charged with and convicted of having attempted an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Despite the restrictions, there were an estimated 6.5 million abortions each year in Latin America from 2010 to 2014, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2  , a U.S.-based policy group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and tracks national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  statistics. It estimated that some 760,000 women are treated each year from complications related to unsafe abortions and that some 900 women die each year.\par \par \uc2 Although legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been making slow gains in Mexico in recent years, most of Mexico's 32 states have laws that provide for penalties for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  except in cases of rape or when the mother's life is endangered by the pregnancy. Only four states\u8212\'20\'14Mexico City, Hidalgo, Veracruz and Oaxaca\u8212\'20\'14allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in most circumstances.\par \par \uc2 The ruling opens the door to the release of people incarcerated for having had or performed abortions across the country. Despite some strict antiabortion laws, relatively few people have been prosecuted or imprisoned.\par \par \uc2 In January 2020, there were 105 people, five women and 100 men, in state prisons in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases, and 12 men in federal prisons, according to Vida Nueva Digital, an online news service, citing government documents.\par \par \uc2 Mexican President \uc2 Andr\u233\'00\'E9s Manuel L\u243\'00\'F3pez Obrador\uc2  has avoided taking sides in disputes over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which he sees as a politically explosive issue.\par \par \uc2 "These are very controversial, polemical matters, and we don't want to stir up any confrontation," he said Tuesday before the ruling. "The best thing in this case is that, if it's before the Supreme Court, that it's resolved there."\par \par \uc2 Anthony Harrup in Mexico City contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Santiago P\u233\'00\'E9rez at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:santiago.perez@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 santiago.perez@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and David Luhnow at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:david.luhnow@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 david.luhnow@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and David Luhnow at\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/mexicos-supreme-court-decriminalizes-abortion-in-historic-shift-11631049288"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mexico's Supreme Court Decriminalizes \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  in Historic Shift  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020210907eh970048t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
