\par\par \uc2 Politics and Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Justice Department Looks for Ways to Challenge Texas  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law; Attorney General Garland says DOJ will enforce a federal law prohibiting people from threatening or preventing women seeking abortions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sadie Gurman and Eliza Collins \par \uc2 814 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 September 2021\par 22:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Justice Department won't tolerate violence or threats against people seeking abortions in Texas, U.S. Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  said, as the Biden administration considers ways to challenge a new state law banning most abortions.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Garland said Monday that the department was "urgently" exploring its options to challenge {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-what-to-know-as-supreme-court-allows-it-to-take-effect-11630606238"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the new law }}}\uc2 , which bars most abortions after about six weeks of pregnancy. The Supreme Court late Wednesday {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-ruling-raises-doubts-about-chief-justices-hold-on-the-roberts-court-11630624331"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 allowed it to take effect }}}\uc2 , but didn't rule on the measure's constitutionality.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department will "continue to protect those seeking to obtain or provide \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  services" under a separate federal law that makes it a crime to injure or intimidate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -clinic patients and employees, Mr. Garland said. It also prohibits damaging property at such facilities and other \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive-health\b0 \highlight\uc2  centers.\par \par \uc2 Officials have reached out to federal prosecutors and \uc2 Federal Bureau of Investigation\uc2  field offices across Texas to discuss how to enforce the federal law, known as the 1994 Freedom of Access to Clinic Entrances Act, he said.\par \par \uc2 "The department will provide support from federal law enforcement when an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic or \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  center is under attack," Mr. Garland said.\par \par \uc2 A representative for Republican \uc2 Texas Gov. Greg Abbott\uc2  didn't immediately respond to an email seeking comment.\par \par \uc2 The Texas law, which took effect Wednesday, dictates that a physician can't knowingly perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if there is "a detectable fetal heartbeat," which includes embryonic cardiac activity that appears {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-rejects-emergency-request-to-block-texas-six-week-abortion-ban-11630556283"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 about six weeks into a pregnancy }}}\uc2 . The Texas Heartbeat Act, known as SB 8, provides an exception for medical emergencies but not for pregnancies resulting from rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers assigned enforcement to private parties, giving them an incentive by authorizing damages of $10,000 or more if they successfully sued a defendant they accused of performing or aiding an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That novel provision was designed to create procedural obstacles to challenges filed by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates.\par \par \uc2 That provision could also make it more difficult for the federal government to challenge the law, said University of Texas law professor Steve Vladeck. The Justice Department could contemplate ways to sue Texas, he said. The department could also seek ways to restrict federal funding or try to determine whether there are federal medical facilities in Texas that can provide abortions after the six-week period, he added.\par \par \uc2 "We're in pretty uncharted territory here," Mr. Vladeck said. "This is a sticky wicket procedurally."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Garland's statement signals that the Justice Department intends to increase prosecutions of people for violence or vandalism at \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics after years of spotty enforcement and amid concerns about an uptick in such crimes because of Texas's new law, said Mary Ziegler, a law professor at Florida State University.\par \par \uc2 Antiabortion activists criticized the statement, while those supportive of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights said it was a good first step\u8212\'20\'14but not enough.\par \par \uc2 "The Justice Department should be focused on protecting our basic rights, including our right to life, not on acting as the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  industry's personal attorneys before a relevant lawsuit is even in process," said Lila Rose, founder and president of Live Action, a group that opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Jacqueline Ayers, vice president of government relations and public policy at the Planned Parenthood Federation of America, said the group welcomed the statement, "but we hope and expect that the administration also intends to use every other tool at its disposal to protect patients who have no access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas right now."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Garland's warning came as Democrats in Congress have also vowed to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in response to the Supreme Court's order. House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  (D., Calif.) said the chamber would vote later this month on legislation that would ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions before fetal viability.\par \par \uc2 However, the legislation is unlikely to pass the evenly divided Senate where it would need 60 votes. Senate Majority Leader Chuck Schumer (D., N.Y.) hasn't announced a pending vote.\par \par \uc2 The expected outcome in the Senate renewed calls from progressives to abolish the 60-vote threshold on most legislation known as the legislative filibuster, and some pollsters say it will be a key issue in coming elections. Antiabortion female voters have long been active in elections, but the perceived threat to Roe v. Wade\u8212\'20\'14the 1973 ruling that women have the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14could activate a new bloc of female voters who favor \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights as well, said Christine Matthews, a pollster who has worked for Republican candidates.\par \par \uc2 Write to Sadie Gurman at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:sadie.gurman@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sadie.gurman@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Eliza Collins at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:eliza.collins+1@wsj.com."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 eliza.collins+1@wsj.com. }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/justice-department-looks-for-ways-to-challenge-texas-abortion-law-11630959275"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Justice Department Looks for Ways to Challenge Texas \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Law  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020210906eh96003bh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
