\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Behind Texas  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law, an Attorney's Unusual Enforcement Idea; Jonathan F. Mitchell taught law and clerked for Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2  before devising a provision that has confounded  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates and animated their opponents\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jacob Gershman \par \uc2 1,102 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 September 2021\par 13:38 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Behind a Texas law that has confounded legal scholars and given \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents hope is a publicity-shy, 45-year-old West Coast litigator known for his command of abstruse legal theory.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-what-to-know-as-supreme-court-allows-it-to-take-effect-11630606238"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Texas Heartbeat Act  }}}\uc2  has survived a brush with the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  and made Texas the most restrictive in the nation for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, thanks largely to its unusual enforcement scheme. The law puts ordinary Texans\u8212\'20\'14not any government official\u8212\'20\'14in charge of enforcing a prohibition on performing or aiding abortions after six weeks of pregnancy, making it more difficult to challenge in court.\par \par \uc2 The principal architect of the private-enforcement provision is Jonathan F. Mitchell, a constitutional litigator, former Justice \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2  clerk and law professor who in his spare time works pro bono advising the Republican legislators in Texas, according to people familiar with the legislation's development. He is active with the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2  , the conservative legal group.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mitchell helped draft the bill at the suggestion of state Sen. \uc2 Bryan Hughes\uc2  , an East Texas Republican, who said he sponsored it as a way for Texas to enact a so-called heartbeat bill that many other states passed into law.\par \par \uc2 But the senator said he also wanted to avoid the fate of those other bills, which were all struck down by judges who said the laws placed an undue burden on women seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before the fetus was viable. District attorneys in Texas' more liberal cities were already saying they wouldn't enforce \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in the event of a \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  ruling overturning Roe v. Wade. So Mr. Hughes wanted a bill that could remain effective even without prosecutions.\par \par \uc2 "We knew we had to have another way," said Mr. Hughes, chairman of the chamber's influential Senate State Affairs policy-making committee. "We were going to find a way to pass a heartbeat bill that was going to be upheld."\par \par \uc2 Some legal scholars, including conservatives, are dubious that the Texas law can survive a more sustained legal review and expect courts\u8212\'20\'14either at the federal or state level\u8212\'20\'14to halt it with some kind of general injunction. But they say Mr. Mitchell still has defied the odds by seeing the law go into effect.\par \par \uc2 "He would have been a pretty successful legal academic," said Ilya Somin, a \uc2 George Mason University\uc2  law professor and former colleague of Mr. Mitchell's. "He's a creative legal thinker."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mitchell has taught law at several law schools, including \uc2 George Mason University\uc2  and Stanford Law School, before entering state government in 2010 as the Texas' solicitor general under Gov. Rick Perry. He left the job when Mr. Perry's tenure ended. In 2017, President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said he would tap Mr. Mitchell to lead the Administrative Conference of the United States, an obscure federal agency that advises on administrative law and regulatory procedures. But his confirmation never came up for a vote.\par \par \uc2 In 2018, Mr. Mitchell drafted {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.virginialawreview.org/articles/writ-erasure-fallacy/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 "The Writ-of-Erasure Fallacy,"  }}}\uc2  a Virginia Law Review article that articulated the legal theories that would eventually find their way into the Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law. The article was a deep dive into the subject of judicial review and raised the idea that when a court rules a statute unconstitutional, the law isn't erased from the books and could be modified to allow for "private enforcement."\par \par \uc2 He described how laws could be constructed to "enable private litigants to enforce a statute even after a federal district court has enjoined the executive from enforcing it," without going in-depth about the applicability to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws.\par \par \uc2 After opening a solo law practice, Mr. Mitchell extended the idea to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2019 when he advised an antiabortion East Texas pastor in drafting an ordinance adopted by a number of smaller Texas cities, including the city of Lubbock, that allowed Texas citizens to obtain an injunction against anyone performing or helping to carry out an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Efforts by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates to stop enforcement of the Lubbock ordinance failed in federal court, where a judge ruled that the plaintiff, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  , couldn't sue the government over the law.\par \par \uc2 The Texas Heartbeat Act, or SB 8, as the Texas law is known, is a legal labyrinth of statutory construction that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-abortion-ruling-brings-new-uncertainty-to-decades-old-fight-11630621820"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has confounded some of the legal profession's most seasoned minds  }}}\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights activists have denounced the law as diabolical, while some legal scholars have marveled at its creative clauses.\par \par \uc2 In a typical challenge to an antiabortion law, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates can sue government officials tasked with enforcing the statute and wage their fights in courts and regions of their choosing. SB 8, which many Republican lawyers in the statehouse helped shape into its final form, turns the table on the geographic advantage. Claimants can sue on their home turf, even if the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -provider defendant is located elsewhere, and avoid courts in more Democratic areas. With no government official to sue, plaintiffs lack standing to move pre-emptively against the laws.\par \par \uc2 The law brims with financial enticements for claimants and their lawyers. The law sets a floor for damages at $10,000 per unlawful \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but sets no limit on how much money claimants can recover. If they prevail, they can also demand the losing party pay their legal bills. If they lose in court and their case is dismissed, they owe the defendant nothing.\par \par \uc2 The law permits multiple lawsuits to be filed by different individuals over a single \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Once a claimant collects damages, though, the others suing may not collect more money from the same defendant for the same violation.\par \par \uc2 The Republican-led state Senate and House passed the measure in May with votes almost entirely split along party lines. Between the two chambers, only two Democrats voted for the bill. No Republicans voted against the ban. Texas Gov. Greg Abbott , a Republican, signed the bill on May 19, and it went into effect Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 In an unsigned 5-4 order, the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  declined to block the law from taking effect. The conservative majority wrote that there were "serious questions regarding the constitutionality of the Texas law" but said the court might lack the jurisdiction to act because of procedural technicalities. The three liberal justices and Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  filed dissents.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jacob Gershman at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jacob.gershman@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jacob.gershman@wsj.com  }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/behind-texas-abortion-law-an-attorneys-unusual-enforcement-idea-11630762683"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Behind Texas \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Law, an Attorney's Unusual Enforcement Idea  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020210904eh940015p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
