\par\par \uc2 Business\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Lyft\uc2 , Uber to Cover Legal Costs of Drivers Sued Under Texas  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law; Law creates new structure that allows citizens to bring a civil lawsuit against  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  providers and those who assist along the way\b0\par\par\uc2 By Preetika Rana \par \uc2 646 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 September 2021\par 01:23 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Lyft Inc\uc2 . and \uc2 Uber Technologies Inc\uc2 . said they would cover the legal costs of drivers in Texas who might be sued for ferrying women to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics.\par \par \uc2 Lyft\uc2 's Chief Executive Officer \uc2 Logan Green\uc2  tweeted the decision on Friday, saying the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-what-to-know-as-supreme-court-allows-it-to-take-effect-11630606238"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas law that bars \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  after about six weeks }}}\uc2  of pregnancy "threatens to punish drivers for getting people where they need to go\u8212\'20\'14especially women exercising their right to choose."\par \par \uc2 Uber CEO \uc2 Dara Khosrowshahi\uc2  chimed in an hour later, tweeting: "Team Uber is in too and will cover legal fees in the same way. Thanks for the push," he wrote, referring to \uc2 Lyft\uc2 's Mr. Green.\par \par \uc2 The law, which took effect this week after the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-rejects-emergency-request-to-block-texas-six-week-abortion-ban-11630556283"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court declined an emergency request }}}\uc2  from state healthcare providers to block it, creates a new enforcement structure that allows private citizens to bring a civil lawsuit against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and those who assist along the way. Those citizens could potentially collect at least $10,000 in damages plus legal costs per \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  challenged successfully.\par \par \uc2 Companies have largely stayed on the sidelines in the subsequent controversy over the law since it has taken effect. But some have gotten involved or been drawn into the fray.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, \uc2 Match Group Inc\uc2 . CEO \uc2 Shar Dubey\uc2  and \uc2 Bumble Inc\uc2 . said they would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/after-texas-abortion-law-match-ceo-bumble-create-funds-to-support-those-affected-11630617641"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 set up a fund to assist Texas-based employees }}}\uc2  who will now need to seek care out of the state. The companies run two of the most popular dating apps in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 GoDaddy Inc\uc2 . said it would no longer host the website, prowhistleblower.com, citing violations of its terms of service including its rule against collecting private or personally identifiable information without that person's consent.\par \par \uc2 The website was affiliated with Texas Right to Life and featured a button that the Texas antiabortion advocacy group said was where people could submit anonymous tips to identify the location of illegal abortions. Users on social media called for the website to be taken down.\par \par \uc2 GoDaddy on Thursday gave the group 24 hours to move to a different service provider, a company representative said in a statement. The internet-domain registration and website-hosting company declined to comment further.\par \par \uc2 Texas Right to Life said in a post on its \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account Friday that it aimed to have the site restored with a different provider within 24 to 48 hours.\par \par \uc2 "Too bad for the mob: We will not be silenced," the group said in a tweet. "Anti-Lifers hate us because we're winning." The group couldn't immediately be reached for further comment.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's decision puts ride-hailing companies, who don't directly employ their drivers, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-abortion-ruling-brings-new-uncertainty-to-decades-old-fight-11630621820"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in a precarious spot }}}\uc2 . Drivers could fear being named as defendants in a legal suit and avoid certain destinations, or decline certain trips thinking they aren't worth the risk.\par \par \uc2 "Imagine being a pregnant woman trying to get to a healthcare appointment and not knowing if your driver will cancel on you for fear of breaking a law," \uc2 Lyft\uc2  said in a blog post published Friday.\par \par \uc2 Lyft\uc2  decided to defend drivers so they aren't discouraged from accepting such rides, spokeswoman Julie Wood said. The company hasn't set a limit on how much it will spend in drivers' defense, she added.\par \par \uc2 Lyft\uc2 's Mr. Green said the company is separately donating $1 million to Planned Parenthood so "that transportation is never a barrier to healthcare access." The CEO called on other companies to donate.\par \par \uc2 Bowdeya Tweh contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Preetika Rana at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:preetika.rana@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 preetika.rana@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/lyft-to-cover-legal-costs-of-drivers-sued-under-texas-abortion-law-11630700449"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Lyft, Uber to Cover Legal Costs of Drivers Sued Under Texas \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Law }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020210903eh93005h9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
