\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Texas  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban: What to Know About the New Law; Texas Heartbeat Act, or SB 8, would ban most abortions in the state after about six weeks of pregnancy, with no exceptions for rape or incest\b0\par\par\uc2 By Byron Tau \par \uc2 945 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 September 2021\par 14:56 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A new Texas law that bars \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after about six weeks of pregnancy took effect this week, after the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-rejects-emergency-request-to-block-texas-six-week-abortion-ban-11630556283?mod=searchresults_pos2&page=1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court declined an emergency request  }}}\uc2  from state healthcare providers to block it. Here's a look at what the law does, and what's next for the legal controversy surrounding the new Texas rules and similar proposals being considered by state legislatures around the country.\par \par \uc2 What does the law do?\par \par \uc2 The Texas Heartbeat Act, also known as SB 8, passed into law earlier this year by the Republican-controlled Texas legislature and signed by Gov. Greg Abbott in May. It says a physician can't knowingly perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if there is "a detectable fetal heartbeat."\par \par \uc2 In practice, this restricts most abortions after about six weeks of pregnancy and makes Texas the most restrictive state in the nation for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. The act provides an exception for medical emergencies that could affect the health of the mother but none for pregnancies resulting from rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 The Texas law {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/mississippi-asks-supreme-court-to-end-roe-v-wade-abortion-rights-11626985810"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 differs from other states' recent attempts at restrictions  }}}\uc2  to severely limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, because it creates a new enforcement structure that allows private citizens to bring a civil lawsuit against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and to collect at least $10,000 in damages plus legal costs per \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  challenged successfully.\par \par \uc2 In addition, the law also allows potential lawsuits against not only clinics and doctors, but also anyone who aids or abets an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including insurance companies and transportation providers.\par \par \uc2 What did the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  say about the Texas law?\par \par \uc2 After a federal appeals court declined to intervene, a coalition of state health providers filed an emergency application on Aug. 30 with the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  to block the Texas law, two days before its effective date. The court remained silent as the law went into effect as scheduled on Sept. 1 and then issued a 5-4 decision just before midnight declining to intervene. In an unsigned order, a conservative majority acknowledged there were "serious questions regarding the constitutionality of the Texas law" but said the court might lack the jurisdiction to act because of procedural technicalities. The three liberal justices and Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  filed dissents.\par \par \uc2 How does the Texas law conflict with current \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  precedent on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 In 1973, the court ruled in Roe v. Wade that women had the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8211\'20\'13effectively outlawing numerous restrictions around the country. The court said women possessed a constitutional right to privacy that protected their ability to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but that right could be limited in some cases. The court said abortions in the first trimester of pregnancy couldn't be regulated, and abortions later in the pregnancy could be more regulated, or restricted outright, though with exceptions if the health of the mother was at stake.\par \par \uc2 The court over the years has reined in the broader \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  right established by Roe. In a landmark 1992 case, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  v. Casey, a controlling opinion by three Republican-appointed justices sought to find middle ground, affirming women's right to end pregnancies before fetal viability but providing lawmakers greater power to regulate the procedure after the fetus was deemed capable of survival outside the womb. Subsequent cases have upheld bans on some late-term abortions, while also striking down some state laws that have tried to restrict the procedure.\par \par \uc2 What does this mean for Roe v. Wade?\par \par \uc2 The order didn't directly address longstanding \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  precedents on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. The justices were considering only what rules should be in place in Texas for now, while litigation challenging the ban continues.\par \par \uc2 The ruling doesn't mean the court will ultimately uphold the Texas law. But it does signal that future challenges to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights could get a sympathetic hearing from a new court with additional conservative justices who may want to retreat from the court's previous decisions.\par \par \uc2 What's next for the Texas law?\par \par \uc2 The law will remain in effect\u8212\'20\'14for now. However, both sides in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate are likely to continue to clash over the state measure. There may be other ways to challenge the Texas ban, especially if a doctor or healthcare provider is willing to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in defiance of the law, which could trigger a fresh case.\par \par \uc2 How today's \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  sees \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights remains unclear, in part because nominees have refused to discuss positions on potential hot-button issues during their confirmation hearings, citing the need to remain impartial and open-minded if actual litigation came before them. In some cases, however, their prior records suggest skepticism about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 There are six Republican-appointed justices on the court, and choosing nominees skeptical of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights has been a longstanding Republican goal. During his 2016 campaign, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said he would appoint "pro-life justices" who "automatically" would vote to overrule Roe. His three appointees, Justices \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  , \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  , joined Wednesday's majority with Justices \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Many conservatives are banking on Mr. Trump's prediction. In a pending case to be argued in the coming months, Mississippi, whose law barring most abortions after 15 weeks has been blocked by lower courts, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/mississippi-asks-supreme-court-to-end-roe-v-wade-abortion-rights-11626985810"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 asks the justices to overrule Roe  }}}\uc2  and return the legal clock to 1973, when states had full power to regulate or outlaw abortions.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-what-to-know-as-supreme-court-allows-it-to-take-effect-11630606238"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Ban: What to Know About the New Law  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020210902eh92004ed\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
