\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ruling Brings New Uncertainty to Decades-Old Fight; Some  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  opponents talk of replicating Texas's law, while  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters pledge to continue fight in court\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jacob Gershman \par \uc2 1,264 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2021\par 23:12 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 Some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws enacted by conservative legislatures have not been allowed by courts to be enforced. An earlier version of this article incorrectly said the courts have allowed the laws to be enforced. (Corrected on Sept. 2)\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  's refusal to halt a new restrictive Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law has plunged the fight over the issue into a new phase of turmoil, with both sides of the issue scrambling to make sense of a legal terrain at its most uncertain since the court recognized a constitutional right to the procedure nearly 50 years ago.\par \par \uc2 In a 5-4 late-night ruling Wednesday, the conservative bloc of justices held sway over dissenting views from Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  and the court's liberal minority and allowed the nation's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-takes-effect-pending-supreme-court-action-11630498466?mod=article_inline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 toughest restrictions on abortions  }}}\uc2  to stay in effect at least for the short term.\par \par \uc2 The ruling was decided on largely technical grounds, with the majority emphasizing it wasn't ruling on the law's constitutionality. To the surprise of many, however, it left in place a law that effectively shut down most abortions in the second-most populous state.\par \par \uc2 To \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters and foes alike, the ruling was seen as a potential turning point in a bitterly contested debate that has long rested on the presumption that as long as Roe. v. Wade stands as precedent, sweeping restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  can't withstand judicial review.\par \par \uc2 While some groups supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights vowed to keep their focus on court challenges, some of their opponents were looking ahead to new possible legislation.\par \par \uc2 "I'd like to see the Texas bill pass in Florida," said Florida State Rep. Anthony Sabatini, an antiabortion Republican whose district includes Lake County, outside Orlando. He called the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  ruling "one of the most important legal decisions since Casey," referring to the 1992 case that reaffirmed the core of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 "We're very excited," he said.\par \par \uc2 At the center of the battle is a state law that on its surface seemed to mirror other laws enacted by conservative legislatures that courts have not allowed to be enforced.\par \par \uc2 The statute makes it unlawful for a physician to terminate a fetus with a detectable heartbeat, which generally occurs six weeks into a pregnancy, making exceptions for medical emergencies but not cases involving rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 Over the past decade, many similar state laws were swiftly struck down by federal courts, on grounds that they conflict with the central thrust of Roe v. Wade, that the Constitution protects the right to early-term abortions.\par \par \uc2 The Texas law, though, was constructed with legal trap doors and the intention to make it as difficult as possible to challenge in court. The law puts the enforcement powers in the hands of private citizens, rather than state or local enforcement agencies. And it gives Texans a large financial incentive to blow the whistle on previously legal abortions, allowing the collection of hefty damages and attorneys' fees from any person who allegedly performs or aids in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after detection of a heartbeat\u8212\'20\'14with no caps on recovery.\par \par \uc2 For now, the lack of a central enforcement authority has thrown into confusion questions over who would be sued to stop the law.\par \par \uc2 The law "clearly violates the federal Constitution as it's currently interpreted," said Douglas Laycock, a \uc2 University of Virginia\uc2  constitutional-law professor. "The problem with the case is there no one to sue," said the professor, who described the Texas statute as "exceedingly clever."\par \par \uc2 The justices writing on either side acknowledged the unusual nature of the statute, known as SB 8. The majority said the challenge raised "complex and novel\u8230\'20\'26procedural questions." Writing in dissent, Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  wrote that by banning state officials from "enforcing the act directly and relying instead on citizen bounty hunters, the Legislature sought to make it more complicated for federal courts to enjoin the act on a statewide basis."\par \par \uc2 For \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters, the confusion for now invites more fighting in court.\par \par \uc2 "We are pursuing every possible legal option to keep fighting this ban until \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is restored in Texas," said \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2  , president and chief executive of the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights legal group that has led the court challenge against SB 8.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Northup also said she was heartened by House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  's announcement Thursday that the House would vote later this month on a bill to expand protections for healthcare professionals to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 For now, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Texas said they are complying with the law, turning away most patients seeking abortions. The possibility that the law could stay in effect for weeks or months has prompted some Democratic legal figures to reassess that strategy.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, President Biden called the law's enforcement structure a "bizarre scheme" that could lead to a constitutional quagmire. "Complete strangers will now be empowered to inject themselves in the most private and personal health decisions faced by women," he wrote.\par \par \uc2 Massachusetts Attorney General \uc2 Maura Healey\uc2  said that the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  has made the choice between compliance and defiance much tougher to answer and that clinics should consider breaking the law. "That's a legitimate question given the instantaneous overnight chilling effect this unconstitutional law has had on access to care," she said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters expect to see more conservative states follow Texas's lead.\par \par \uc2 "History has taught us that what works in one state will then be replicated in another state," said Kathaleen Pittman, the clinic administrator at Hope Medical Group for Women in Shreveport, La. "This is not going to be an isolated situation," she said.\par \par \uc2 Some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  foes said the impact of the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  ruling has been overstated. Steven Aden , general counsel for \uc2 Americans United for Life\uc2  , said he expects the litigation to wind up in Texas state courts, where he thinks \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates might have longer-term success in defeating the statute.\par \par \uc2 "If everyone would just stand down and wait a while for the inevitable state lawsuits to be ruled on, I think we would see the law becomes a dead letter at least until the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  overturns Roe," he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Aden said the more consequential case in the horizon is the coming \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  review of a Mississippi law prohibiting most abortions at 15 weeks, a case that he said puts the preservation of Roe v. Wade more squarely in the hands of justices.\par \par \uc2 Still, some conservatives including Mr. Sabatini in Florida were looking at enacting their own versions of SB 8.\par \par \uc2 "I wouldn't be surprised if we see something very similar introduced in South Carolina," said South Carolina Sen. Lawrence K. Grooms, a Republican from outside Charleston. He was the principal sponsor of a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban enacted in February that lacked the private enforcement provisions of the Texas bill.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge blocked enforcement of the South Carolina law in a ruling now under review by the Fourth Circuit. Mr. Grooms said if the injunction against the heartbeat law isn't overturned, or if the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  doesn't overturn Roe v. Wade in the interim, legislators would likely try to advance a bill modeled after SB 8.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Calfas contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jacob Gershman at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jacob.gershman@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jacob.gershman@wsj.com  }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-abortion-ruling-brings-new-uncertainty-to-decades-old-fight-11630621820"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Ruling Brings New Uncertainty to Decades-Old Fight  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020210902eh9200795\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
