\par\par \uc2 Politics and Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  Declines to Block Restrictive Texas  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law; New measure, known as the fetal-heartbeat law, bars the procedure after about six weeks of pregnancy\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brent Kendall and Jess Bravin \par \uc2 1,837 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2021\par 16:52 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14A divided \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  late on Wednesday allowed the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-takes-effect-pending-supreme-court-action-11630498466"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nation's toughest restrictions on abortions  }}}\uc2  to take effect, declining to block a new Texas law that bars the procedure after about six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The court's nighttime order, which rejected an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-asked-to-intervene-on-texas-six-week-abortion-ban-11630355606"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 emergency request by clinics and \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 -rights advocates  }}}\uc2 , marks a turning point in the legal battle over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and comes the day the Texas ban went into force. While not a final ruling on the measure's constitutionality, the court's action validates, at least temporarily, a novel attempt by Texas lawmakers to insulate antiabortion legislation from court challenge.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://s.wsj.net/public/resources/documents/21a24_8759.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unsigned order came on a 5-4 vote  }}}\uc2 , with most of the conservative justices in the majority.\par \par \uc2 President Biden criticized the decision in a statement Thursday and said that he has launched a "whole-of-government effort" to respond to it.\par \par \uc2 "For the majority to do this without a hearing, without the benefit of an opinion from a court below, and without due consideration of the issues, insults the rule of law and the rights of all Americans to seek redress from our courts," Mr. Biden said.\par \par \uc2 Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  and the court's three liberal justices dissented. As so often before, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  exposed deep fissures among the justices. The court was uncharacteristically silent as the Texas law took effect Wednesday, leaving the public to guess about the direction of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights until 24 hours later, when the court issued a single majority opinion and four separate dissents.\par \par \uc2 The state law, signed by Republican Gov. Greg Abbott in May with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/as-new-texas-abortion-ban-law-takes-effect-providers-rush-to-adjust-11630521388"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an effective date of Sept. 1  }}}\uc2 , dictates that a physician can't knowingly perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if there is "a detectable fetal heartbeat," which includes embryonic cardiac activity that appears about six weeks into a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 An \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban so early in a pregnancy\u8212\'20\'14at a stage before some women realize they are pregnant\u8212\'20\'14conflicts with current \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  precedent. The high court has previously forbidden states from banning the procedure before a fetus is able to live outside the womb. The Texas Heartbeat Act, known as SB 8, provides an exception for medical emergencies but none for pregnancies resulting from rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 The high-court majority said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers "raised serious questions regarding the constitutionality of the Texas law at issue." But the court went on to say that continuing litigation over the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions raised "complex and novel" questions about legal procedure that undercut the providers' request to halt the ban right now.\par \par \uc2 "In light of such issues, we cannot say the applicants have met their burden to prevail in an injunction or stay application," the court wrote. Longtime Justices \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  , along with Trump appointees \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  , \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  , formed the majority.\par \par \uc2 The majority said its order allowing the ban "is not based on any conclusion about the constitutionality of Texas's law, and in no way limits other procedurally proper challenges to the Texas law, including in Texas state courts."\par \par \uc2 The four dissenters each issued a separate opinion, joining one another on many aspects of their objections.\par \par \uc2 The most scathing came from Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  , who accused Texas lawmakers of cynically flouting legal precedents\u8212\'20\'14and the court's majority of disingenuously endorsing the lawmakers' strategy.\par \par \uc2 "Taken together, the Act is a breathtaking act of defiance\u8212\'20\'14of the Constitution, of this Court's precedents, and of the rights of women seeking abortions throughout Texas," she wrote, joined by Justices \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  and \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Chief Justice Roberts, in his dissent, used milder language for similar sentiments, questioning the state's "not only unusual, but unprecedented" way of crafting its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, which for tactical reasons delegated enforcement to private parties instead of state officials.\par \par \uc2 Joined by Justices Breyer and Kagan, the chief justice said the law should be blocked while "courts may consider whether a state can avoid responsibility for its laws in such a manner."\par \par \uc2 The emergency appeal, which arrived at the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  on Monday, was centered on the preliminary question of what rules should apply in Texas while \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers continue to challenge the ban in lower courts. That issue came to the justices after a federal appeals court halted trial-court proceedings in the case for now and declined to block the ban from taking effect.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Texas were squeezing in patients and performing procedures Tuesday night until the clock ticked past midnight and the restrictions took effect, said \uc2 Amy Hagstrom Miller\uc2  , the founder and chief executive of Whole Woman's Health, which operates clinics in Texas and has previously challenged Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions before the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 All four Whole Woman's Health clinics in Texas were open Wednesday and operating in accordance with the new law, Ms. Miller said. The clinics are able to offer ultrasounds and make plans to provide the procedure for anyone without detectable embryonic cardiac activity.\par \par \uc2 Houston Women's Reproductive Services is no longer providing abortions past the six-week mark, in accordance to the law, said Kathy Kleinfeld, the clinic's administrator.\par \par \uc2 "There's confusion, frustration, desperation," she said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents cheered when the law went into effect, describing the moment as historic even as they waited for word from the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 "Right now in the great state of Texas, every single child with a detectable heartbeat is legally protected from being killed by the violence of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." said \uc2 Lila Rose\uc2  , founder and president of Live Action, a group that opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. "This is a historic step forward for basic human rights, but more progress must be made."\par \par \uc2 Typically, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates have challenged new restrictions before they go into effect by suing the government officials who would be in charge of enforcement. But Texas lawmakers devised a measure that shifts enforcement from the state to private parties.\par \par \uc2 Under the law, private parties can file civil lawsuits against any person who allegedly performs or aids a banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or who intends to do so. A successful suit entitles the plaintiff to collect at least $10,000 in damages per \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  challenged.\par \par \uc2 While critics of the law have described the sum as a bounty to encourage private groups to sue \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, state officials emphasized this feature in papers filed Tuesday with the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  , arguing that it was among many reasons \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers weren't entitled to an emergency order blocking the law.\par \par \uc2 "This court cannot expunge the law itself. Rather, it can enjoin only enforcement of the law. But the governmental defendants explicitly don't enforce the law," Texas Attorney General \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2  and other state officials told the high court.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics challenging the law put it differently. The measure replaces established legal procedures "with distorted versions designed to maximize the abusive and harassing nature of the lawsuits and to make them impossible to fairly defend against," they said in their \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  filing.\par \par \uc2 John Seago of Texas Right to Life said his group wasn't planning to bring enforcement lawsuits right away and was working under the assumption that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers are complying with the law.\par \par \uc2 Roughly a dozen states have passed "heartbeat bills," but none of the other measures is in effect, and they differ from Texas's private-enforcement approach. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters say the fetal-heartbeat terminology used to describe the six-week bans is misleading because an embryo isn't deemed a fetus until the 11th week of pregnancy.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics or doctors can still challenge the constitutionality of the law if they are sued, but \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters say that approach could put the provider in financial jeopardy. Having the law in effect would "immediately and irreparably decimate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in Texas," the providers said in their brief.\par \par \uc2 Until recently, a slim majority of the court had affirmed the core principles of Roe v. Wade and subsequent precedents that narrowed but didn't eliminate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. By a 5-4 vote in June 2020, for instance, the court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-strikes-down-louisiana-abortion-law-11593440418"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 struck down a Louisiana law  }}}\uc2  requiring physicians who perform abortions to hold hospital-admitting privileges, which the majority found a burden that would reduce access to the procedure without providing medical benefits. Chief Justice Roberts, citing the weight of precedent, joined the court's then-four-member liberal minority to form the majority.\par \par \uc2 But the 2018 retirement of Justice \uc2 Anthony Kennedy\uc2  and the death last year of Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  paved the way for President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and a Republican-controlled Senate to appoint Justices Kavanaugh and Barrett, conservatives whose records indicate deep skepticism of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Mr. Trump said during the 2016 presidential campaign that the 1973 Roe opinion establishing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights would "automatically" be overruled should he have two or three seats to fill on the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  . Mr. Trump also appointed Justice Gorsuch to fill the seat vacated by conservative Justice \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2  's February 2016 death.\par \par \uc2 Antiabortion lawmakers in GOP-controlled states have rushed to test that promise, passing measures that flout court precedent prohibiting undue burdens on women seeking to end their pregnancies or preserving their right to make such decisions before fetal viability. Last year, the conservative Fifth U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals, in New Orleans, struck down a Mississippi law prohibiting most abortions at 15 weeks, well before viability, finding no way to square it with current precedent.\par \par \uc2 Despite that precedent, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-to-consider-abortion-restrictions-from-mississippi-11621259099"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court agreed to hear Mississippi's appeal  }}}\uc2  and its argument that the Roe decision and its progeny "are egregiously wrong." The case is expected to be argued in the coming months, with a decision before July.\par \par \uc2 Though the Texas law is in effect, separate state-court litigation could reduce some of its immediate impact.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Travis County District Court Judge Amy Clark Meachum, a Democrat elected to the bench, temporarily barred a statewide antiabortion group from bringing enforcement lawsuits against several plaintiffs who are challenging the new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in her court.\par \par \uc2 The plaintiffs include a Plano, Texas, lawyer who has donated to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights causes and a Dallas lawyer who represents women impregnated by sexual assault. In court filings, they said they feared that giving money to groups that support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access or providing legal counsel to assault victims considering abortions could subject them to suits under the law.\par \par \uc2 Texas Right to Life, a defendant in the Travis County lawsuits, said it never threatened to sue those specific plaintiffs.\par \par \uc2 Judge Meachum has scheduled a Sept. 13 court hearing on whether to bar such lawsuits for a longer period.\par \par \uc2 Jacob Gershman, Jennifer Calfas and Sarah Toy contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Brent Kendall at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:brent.kendall@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 brent.kendall@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Jess Bravin at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jess.bravin+1@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jess.bravin+1@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Jess Bravin at\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-rejects-emergency-request-to-block-texas-six-week-abortion-ban-11630556283"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court Declines to Block Restrictive Texas \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Law  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020210902eh92000jh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
