\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Texas Ruling Raises Doubts About Chief Justice's Hold on the Roberts Court; Looming Mississippi case will put Roe v. Wade directly before Supreme Court with five conservatives steering to right of \uc2 John Roberts\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jess Bravin and Brent Kendall \par \uc2 1,100 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2021\par 23:43 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14The Supreme Court's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-rejects-emergency-request-to-block-texas-six-week-abortion-ban-11630556283?mod=hp_lead_pos1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 5-4 midnight order letting Texas ban most abortions }}}\uc2  raised new uncertainty about \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but one point was clear: It is another sign that Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  is struggling to steer the Roberts Court at a time when an even bigger \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case is looming.\par \par \uc2 A conservative chief justice who prizes consensus and calibration in jurisprudence once again found himself out of step with five colleagues further to his right. Instead, Chief Justice Roberts landed in an increasingly familiar place on the court he leads: dissenting alongside three liberals who, often for different reasons, share his disagreement with the newly expanded conservative majority.\par \par \uc2 According to a compilation by University of Texas professor Steve Vladeck, Wednesday's case was the fourth time this term that Chief Justice Roberts joined liberals to dissent from emergency orders issued by the court.\par \par \uc2 In his dissent, joined by two liberals, the chief justice warned of "the consequences of approving the state action, both in this particular case and as a model for action in other areas." The unsigned majority opinion expressed no such concerns, saying that procedural factors made it impossible for the court to block the law from going into effect.\par \par \uc2 "If I were the chief justice, I'd be worried not only about how people perceive my power on the court today, but also my ability to forge a consensus over the long term with the other justices on a broad range of issues," said William & Mary law professor Neal Devins.\par \par \uc2 The Texas ruling didn't directly address \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, and the conservative majority made clear that was intentional in its one-paragraph opinion. "This order is not based on any conclusion about the constitutionality of Texas's law," the court said.\par \par \uc2 Instead, it said that procedural complexities prevented them from intervening, given that Texas state officials can't themselves enforce the law, and because it isn't clear if, or when, a private party might attempt to invoke the law to sue an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider. The majority also noted that there are other avenues for challenging {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-what-to-know-as-supreme-court-allows-it-to-take-effect-11630606238"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Texas ban }}}\uc2 , especially in state court.\par \par \uc2 Even before the Texas case rose quickly and unexpectedly to the Supreme Court this week, the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights was in the spotlight, with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/mississippi-asks-supreme-court-to-end-roe-v-wade-abortion-rights-11626985810"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a case from Mississippi }}}\uc2  slated for argument in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 At issue there is a state law banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks of pregnancy, far earlier than current precedent, which is before fetal viability, or about 24 weeks. Based on those precedents, a federal appeals court in New Orleans blocked the Mississippi law.\par \par \uc2 Prior to recent appointments by Republican President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the court would have been unlikely to consider Mississippi's appeal, which directly challenges Roe v. Wade, the 1973 ruling recognizing the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and subsequent decisions that limited but didn't eliminate that right.\par \par \uc2 The Texas law bars abortions if there is "a detectable fetal heartbeat," which includes embryonic cardiac activity that appears about six weeks into a pregnancy. Lawmakers assigned enforcement to private parties, giving them an incentive by authorizing damages of $10,000 or more if they successfully sued a defendant they accused of performing or aiding an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That legal novelty was designed to create procedural obstacles to challenges filed by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates.\par \par \uc2 Cases continue both in the lower federal courts and in Texas state court. Meanwhile, the pending Mississippi case, Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, could make the Texas matter moot. Should the court strike down Mississippi's 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restriction, Texas's six-week deadline almost assuredly would fall as well, regardless of its enforcement mechanism.\par \par \uc2 Alternatively, under a state law enacted in January, Texas automatically would outlaw all abortions, other than to save the woman's life or prevent severe injury, should the Supreme Court accept Mississippi's invitation to overrule Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Until recently, Chief Justice Roberts, an appointee of GOP President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , regularly voted against extending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights when new questions appeared before the court. But he has been uncomfortable discarding even precedents he believes are mistaken based only on the court's personnel changes, a process more typical for political bodies such as Congress than judicial institutions.\par \par \uc2 With myriad precedents from the court's more liberal past facing conservative challenges, Chief Justice Roberts's belief in institutional stability increasingly has placed him in alignment with the court's diminished liberal wing.\par \par \uc2 It is a striking and swift turnaround from the last two years, which saw Chief Justice Roberts at the peak of his power.\par \par \uc2 In 2016, he faced consignment to a conservative minority when President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  nominated the liberal \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  to succeed the late Justice \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2 . Senate Republicans blocked then-Judge Garland's appointment, however, preserving the vacancy for Mr. Trump to fill with then-Judge \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 . When Mr. Trump tapped then-Judge \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  to succeed the more moderate Justice \uc2 Anthony Kennedy\uc2  in 2018, the chief justice emerged as the "median justice" who in ideologically-divided cases cast the deciding vote between two four-member blocs to his left and right.\par \par \uc2 For two years, he steered the court cautiously to the right, generally siding with business, law enforcement and religious organizations but on occasion voting with liberals to limit extreme jumps in the law or curb Trump administration initiatives that flouted rule-making procedures.\par \par \uc2 During the coronavirus pandemic, he had set out the controlling principle for public-health regulations: deference to politically accountable authorities such as governors over the response to Covid-19, rather than judicial second-guessing when litigants sued to lessen or tighten countermeasures.\par \par \uc2 But when Justice Amy Coney Barrett succeeded the late Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  in October 2020, Chief Justice Roberts lost the driver's seat. Over the chief's objection, the court began exempting religious institutions from capacity limits and other public health orders that applied to the general public.\par \par \uc2 Now, this week's Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case has provided the most visible example of this court's rightward shift with Justice Barrett, while highlighting the limits of Chief Justice Roberts's influence.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jess Bravin at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jess.bravin+1@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jess.bravin+1@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Brent Kendall at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:brent.kendall@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 brent.kendall@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-ruling-raises-doubts-about-chief-justices-hold-on-the-roberts-court-11630624331"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas Ruling Raises Doubts About Chief Justice's Hold on the Roberts Court  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020210902eh92007vd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
