\par\par \uc2 Review & Outlook (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Texas's  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law Blunder; The Supreme Court was right not to interfere for now, but the statute won't survive scrutiny on the merits.\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Editorial Board \par \uc2 815 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2021\par 22:50 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 America is back fighting its endless legal war over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A new front opened late Wednesday when five Justices issued an unsigned opinion declining to block a Texas law banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks. Cue the hysterics about the end of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. But this law is a misfire even if you oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and neither side should be confident the law will be upheld.\par \par \uc2 For starters, the Texas statute clearly violates the Court's Roe v. Wade (1973) and Casey (1992) precedents by making \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  illegal during the first trimester without exceptions for rape or incest\u8212\'20\'14and it does so in a slippery way to duck federal judicial review.\par \par \uc2 Most laws delegate enforcement to public officials. This one delegates exclusive enforcement to private citizens, who are authorized to sue anyone who "aids or abets" an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks. Citizens who prevail in their civil lawsuits are entitled to at least $10,000 per \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  along with legal costs.\par \par \uc2 The law sets an awful precedent that conservatives should hate. Could California allow private citizens to sue individuals for hate speech? Or New York deputize private lawsuits against gun owners?\par \par \uc2 Texas argues that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers don't have standing to challenge the law because the state isn't enforcing it and neither at this point is any private citizen. Thus there is no case or controversy, which is what courts are supposed to settle. This is technically correct and it is why the five Justices declined to enjoin the law.\par \par \uc2 "Federal courts enjoy the power to enjoin individuals tasked with enforcing laws, not the laws themselves," says their {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/20pdf/21a24_8759.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unsigned opinion}}}\uc2 , citing the Court's recent decision in California v. Texas (2021). In that case a 7-2 majority dismissed Texas's ObamaCare challenge after finding the Court lacked jurisdiction to hear the case since the feds weren't enforcing the individual mandate.\par \par \uc2 Justices \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  and \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  wrote in a fierce dissent that the Court\u8212\'20\'14read Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14had applied standing principles selectively. They're right. And some conservative Justices may now enjoy hoisting the Chief on his own standing petard. But their unsigned opinion suggests they also have doubts about the Texas law.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers have "raised serious questions regarding the constitutionality of the Texas law at issue. But their application also presents complex and novel antecedent procedural questions on which they have not carried their burden," the five Justices write. "We stress that we do not purport to resolve definitively any jurisdictional or substantive claim in the applicants' lawsuit" and the Court's order "is not based on any conclusion about the constitutionality of Texas's law." Texas state courts may also have a say, the Justices add.\par \par \uc2 The Chief writes in a dissent, joined by Justices Elena Kagan and \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 , that he would grant the providers "preliminary relief" to "preserve the status quo ante" so "the courts may consider whether a state can avoid responsibility for its laws in such a manner." But the dissenting Justices acknowledge that Texas may be correct that "existing doctrines preclude judicial intervention."\par \par \uc2 What they want is to issue what is essentially an advisory opinion in the form of an injunction. This is not the role of the courts. In any case, a provider who gets sued under the Texas law will undoubtedly seek to dismiss the lawsuit under the Court's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  precedents. Then the law will be properly enjoined.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, Texas Republicans have handed Democrats a political grenade to hurt the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cause. Pro-life groups have spent nearly 50 years arguing that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a political question to be settled in the states by public debate. Yet now in Texas they want to use the courts via civil litigation to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Democrats are already having a field day with the Texas law. "This law is so extreme it does not even allow for exceptions in the case of rape or incest," President Biden said in a statement. Look for Democrats to raise the political pressure even higher on the Supreme Court this coming term when it hears a Mississippi case that bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks. The Justices could uphold the Mississippi law by narrowing Casey's "undue burden" standard. But the left will flog the Texas law and proclaim that upholding the Mississippi law is a fast track to overturning Roe.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes we wonder if Texas Attorney General \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2  is a progressive plant. His ill-conceived legal attack against ObamaCare backfired on Republicans in last year's election and lost at the Supreme Court. Now he and his Texas mates are leading with their chins on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . How about thinking first?\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-blunder-supreme-court-samuel-alito-john-roberts-whole-womans-health-11630619631"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas's \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Law Blunder}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020210902eh92007n1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
