\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 As New Texas  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban Takes Effect, Providers Rush to Adjust; Clinics were squeezing in patients until minutes before midnight ahead of a ban on abortions after six weeks of pregnancy\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jennifer Calfas and Sarah Toy \par \uc2 1,351 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 September 2021\par 22:38 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers across Texas are scrambling to adapt to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-takes-effect-pending-supreme-court-action-11630498466"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a new law that took effect Wednesday }}}\uc2 , banning the procedure after about six weeks of pregnancy, the most restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Without interference from the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  before midnight Tuesday, abortions past the six-week mark are now barred in the nation's second-most populous state, and private citizens can file civil lawsuits to enforce the new law. It is possible the high court might still intervene. But {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-governor-signs-fetal-heartbeat-abortion-ban-11621452346"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the state law }}}\uc2 , enacted in May and effective Sept. 1, has left clinic directors, doctors and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates adapting to new measures and offering guidance for patients who suddenly are no longer eligible to receive an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Many \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Texas were squeezing in patients and performing procedures until minutes before midnight, when restrictions took effect, according to clinic leaders. Clinics were inundated with calls as patients sought guidance. Many providers adjusted their usual offerings to align with the new law by Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Patients filled waiting rooms Tuesday night at the four Whole Woman's Health clinics in Texas, said Amy Hagstrom Miller, the founder and chief executive of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider that has previously {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-voids-texas-abortion-regulations-1467036610"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 challenged Texas \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  restrictions }}}\uc2  before the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 At the Whole Woman's Health clinic in Fort Worth, 27 patients were still in the waiting room by 10 p.m. Tuesday night, as antiabortion protests continued outside the clinic's doors. Doctors were performing abortions at the clinic until 11:56 p.m., she said.\par \par \uc2 All four Whole Woman's Health clinics in Texas were open Wednesday and operating in accordance with the new law, Ms. Miller said. The clinics are able to offer ultrasounds and make plans to provide the procedure for anyone without detectable embryonic activity, which typically occurs about six weeks into pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Miller said clinic staff would provide guidance for any patients who are no longer able to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Texas and may opt to travel to a clinic out of the state, for example.\par \par \uc2 "The compliance has been onerous and has been lengthy," she said. "Our teams are on site today."\par \par \uc2 Double the number of patients arrived at Houston Women's Reproductive Services this week as women rushed to be seen before the law took effect, said Kathy Kleinfeld, the clinic's administrator. The clinic's phone lines were especially busy Wednesday, when the clinic stopped offering abortions past the six-week mark in accordance with the new law, she said.\par \par \uc2 One patient who was eight weeks pregnant arrived at the clinic Tuesday for an ultrasound and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  consultation, said Ms. Kleinfeld. In Texas, there is a mandatory 24-hour waiting period for all abortions, said Ms. Kleinfeld, so the patient couldn't get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that day.\par \par \uc2 Texas Politics\par \par \uc2 More WSJ coverage selected by the editors\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-abortion-law-takes-effect-pending-supreme-court-action-11630498466"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Law Takes Effect Pending Supreme Court Action }}}\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/texas-gop-lawmakers-send-voting-bill-to-governors-desk-11630449811"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas GOP Lawmakers Send Voting Bill to Governor's Desk }}}\par \par \uc2 "She was pleading with us," said Ms. Kleinfeld. "We just had to explain that this is a temporary law and hopefully it'll be struck down soon, but for now it is the law and we have to comply with it."\par \par \uc2 She said she referred the woman to a directory that listed out-of-state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services and told her she could reach back out to the clinic if things changed.\par \par \uc2 "There's confusion, frustration, desperation," she said.\par \par \uc2 John Seago, legislative director at Texas Right to Life, the state's largest antiabortion organization and which helped craft the legislation, said the group felt optimistic but was awaiting further action from the Supreme Court before celebrating.\par \par \uc2 "We'll feel a lot better once it's official," Mr. Seago said. "But it is promising that the court did not feel the need to step in late last night or this morning before the bill took effect."\par \par \uc2 Since it was enacted in May, other states have reached out to officials at Texas Right to Life asking for recommendations on how to successfully bring forward similarly restrictive state laws, Mr. Seago said.\par \par \uc2 About a dozen other states have passed so-called heartbeat bills over the years but courts have blocked such legislation from taking effect. The other bills included criminal or administrative penalties. The Texas law shifts enforcement from state officials to private parties, making it one of the reasons state officials who advocated for the law argued \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers weren't entitled to an emergency order blocking it.\par \par \uc2 "Texas is succeeding where a dozen other states have not in getting this bill to actually go into effect," Mr. Seago said.\par \par \uc2 Antiabortion groups have applauded the law. "Right now in the great state of Texas, every single child with a detectable heartbeat is legally protected from being killed by the violence of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." said Lila Rose, founder and president of Life Action, an antiabortion rights group. "This is a historic step forward for basic human rights, but more progress must be made."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters say the fetal-heartbeat terminology used to describe the six-week bans is misleading because an embryo isn't deemed a fetus until the 11th week of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Between 85% and 90% of abortions in Texas occur after six weeks of pregnancy, say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers challenging the new Texas law, which they call a near-ban on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban contradicts precedent set by the Supreme Court that determines the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before a fetus reaches viability, or when a fetus could survive outside of a womb.\par \par \uc2 "We're still hoping to hear from the court today and we will keep fighting," Marc Hearron, senior counsel at the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  and lead attorney on the challenge to the Texas law {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-asked-to-intervene-on-texas-six-week-abortion-ban-11630355606?mod=article_inline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 at the Supreme Court }}}\uc2 , said Wednesday in a press call.\par \par \uc2 The new law increases the distance a patient may have to travel to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if they are no longer eligible to in Texas, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a research group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Now, patients may have to travel an average of 248 miles to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic, compared with an average of 12 miles before the law took effect, the group said.\par \par \uc2 Clinics in nearby states were preparing for an increase in patients from Texas. Trust Women clinics in Oklahoma City and Wichita, Kan., already were experiencing increases in appointments before the law took effect.\par \par \uc2 Phones were ringing nonstop Wednesday at Hope Medical Group for Women in Shreveport, La., an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic about 25 miles from the border of eastern Texas, with many potential patients from Texas hoping to schedule appointments. First appointments for patients are already booked almost a month out, said Kathaleen Pittman, the clinic's administrator, who is also working to find other clinics that could take patients sooner.\par \par \uc2 "We're trying not to postpone anyone who's already scheduled, but if for any reason someone calls and they can't make it in, we immediately go to our appointments list and try to bring in other patients sooner."\par \par \uc2 In Houston, at the Planned Parenthood Center for Choice, staff physician Bhavik Kumar provided care to an influx of patients through the evening Tuesday before the law took effect\u8212\'20\'14the hardest he has worked in his life, he said. Many patients Wednesday still had hope that something could change in Texas, he said.\par \par \uc2 "It's so difficult to find the words because this is not something that you train for as a physician," Dr. Kumar said. "You don't train to know how to provide care, to know how to make somebody feel better, and then deny them of that. It feels really unethical."\par \par \uc2 Write to Jennifer Calfas at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:Jennifer.Calfas@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jennifer.Calfas@wsj.com }}}\uc2  and Sarah Toy at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:sarah.toy@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sarah.toy@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/as-new-texas-abortion-ban-law-takes-effect-providers-rush-to-adjust-11630521388"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 As New Texas \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Ban Takes Effect, Providers Rush to Adjust  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020210901eh9100439\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
