\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Catholic Bishops Debate Communion for Biden, Politicians Who Support  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights; Question has split U.S. Catholic hierarchy and raised tensions between its leadership and \uc2 Pope Francis\b0\par\par\uc2 By Francis X. Rocca \par \uc2 976 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 June 2021\par 23:58 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 U.S. Catholic bishops on Thursday debated whether to lay out standards for denying Communion to politicians including President Biden who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, a question that has split the U.S. Catholic hierarchy and raised tensions with \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The bishops were considering a proposed doctrinal document on the Eucharist that would include a section on the conditions under which Catholic politicians may receive Communion. The bishops voted on the proposal Thursday, with results to be announced Friday.\par \par \uc2 The issue has become a proxy for disagreements among bishops about which agenda to give priority to in their dealings with U.S. political leaders: traditional moral and cultural values or social and economic justice. The debate also reflects bishops' {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/pope-struggles-to-contain-conservative-liberal-tensions-in-catholic-church-11616245220"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 different views over the leadership }}}\uc2  of \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 , who has played down \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a political issue by comparison with his predecessors.\par \par \uc2 "Almost daily I speak with people, Catholics, laity, who are confused by the fact that we have a president who professes devout Catholicism and yet advances the most radical pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  agenda in our history," Bishop Donald Hying of Madison, Wisconsin, said at the spring meeting of the United States Conference of Catholic Bishops. "They're looking for some direction, some teaching from us."\par \par \uc2 But Cardinal Joseph Tobin of Newark, N.J., and one of \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 's closest allies among the U.S. bishops, warned, "Any effort by this conference to move in support of the categorical exclusion of Catholic political leaders from the Eucharist based on their public-policy positions would thrust the bishops of our nation into the very heart of the toxic partisan strife which has distorted our own political culture and crippled meaningful dialogue."\par \par \uc2 The two were among more than three dozen bishops who spoke on the matter on the second day of the USCCB meeting. Only a few of the bishops explicitly mentioned Mr. Biden or House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , both Catholics who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 That section of the proposed document addressing Catholic politicians was originally proposed in December by a committee formed to deal with the Biden administration. Some bishops have wanted that section to state that politicians such as Mr. Biden who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are ineligible to receive the sacrament. The Vatican has discouraged such a move, warning that it could be divisive, and the pope himself has signaled his preference for a more conciliatory approach to the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the head of the bishops' doctrinal committee announced that it had modified its proposal for the document on Communion in deference to Vatican concerns.\par \par \uc2 "We are no longer proposing a national policy" on politicians receiving Communion, said Bishop Kevin Rhoades of Fort Wayne-South Bend, Ind. He said the final doctrinal document might still include practical guidance for bishops dealing with Catholic politicians who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The White House didn't comment on the Thursday debate. White House spokesman Andrew Bates has said in the past that Mr. Biden is a "strong person of faith."\par \par \uc2 Disagreement over how to deal with Mr. Biden has divided the Catholic hierarchy from the start of the administration. On inauguration day in January, Archbishop Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Gomez of Los Angeles, who is president of the USCCB, wrote that "our new President has pledged to pursue certain policies that would advance moral evils," including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and contraception. Cardinal Blase Cupich of Chicago tweeted an extraordinary public rebuke of Archbishop G\u243\'00\'F3mez.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, a public statement by \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  on the same day didn't mention or allude to the new president's deviations from Catholic teaching. In November, the pope departed from custom to phone Mr. Biden to congratulate him on his election win.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden is a frequent Mass-goer who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-trump-catholic-voters-presidential-election-abortion-supreme-court-11600961731"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 regularly receives Communion }}}\uc2 . Cardinal Wilton Gregory, who as archbishop of Washington is the president's pastor, has said that he wouldn't deny Mr. Biden the sacrament.\par \par \uc2 Pope Francis{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/pope-says-fighting-abortion-and-poverty-are-equally-sacred-causes-1523268012"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has often denounced \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 . However, he made clear early in his pontificate that he would play down the issue in favor of questions of social and economic justice, including inequality, the rights of migrants and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/pope-francis-weighs-adding-ecological-sin-to-church-teachings-11573839966"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 protection of the environment }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Inevitably this debate is also a referendum on \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  because those bishops who feel we need to take an exceptionally strong line on Biden because of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they don't feel supported by this pope," said John Allen, president of Crux Catholic Media and author of numerous books on the church.\par \par \uc2 SHARE YOUR THOUGHTS\par \par \uc2 Should Catholic politicians who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights be able to receive Communion? Join the conversation below.\par \par \uc2 The Vatican's approach to pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights Catholic politicians has shifted markedly since 2004, when then-Sen. John Kerry, a Catholic who supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, was the Democratic nominee for president. Then-Cardinal \uc2 Joseph Ratzinger\uc2 , later Pope \uc2 Benedict XVI\uc2 , instructed U.S. bishops that a Catholic politician who actively supported legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be denied Communion.\par \par \uc2 Cardinal Ratzinger also wrote that "not all moral issues have the same moral weight as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and euthanasia\u8230\'20\'26There may be a legitimate diversity of opinion even among Catholics about waging war and applying the death penalty, but not however with regard to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and euthanasia."\par \par \uc2 By contrast, his successor as Vatican doctrinal chief, Cardinal Luis Ladaria, warned the U.S. bishops last month that "it would be misleading if such a statement [on Communion] were to give the impression that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and euthanasia alone constitute the only grave matters of Catholic moral and social teaching that demand the fullest level of accountability on the part of Catholics."\par \par \uc2 Write to Francis X. Rocca at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:francis.rocca@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 francis.rocca@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/catholic-bishops-debate-communion-for-biden-11623937299"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Catholic Bishops Debate Communion for Biden, Politicians Who Support \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Rights }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020210617eh6h001jq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
