\par\par \uc2 Politics and Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 What's in Biden's $6 Trillion Budget Plan; The president wants increases in spending on education, healthcare and renewable energy\b0\par\par\uc2 By WSJ Staff \par \uc2 2,427 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 May 2021\par 19:52 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Biden administration Friday {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-budget-plan-fiscal-2022-11622169489"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 released its budget }}}\uc2  for fiscal year 2022 calling for increases in spending on education, healthcare and renewable energy.\par \par \uc2 The president is proposing a $6 trillion budget for the year beginning Oct. 1, including $1.52 trillion in discretionary spending for the military and domestic programs. Excluding emergency measures last year tied to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/covid-19-vaccinations-in-the-south-are-among-lowest-in-u-s-11622203201"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the coronavirus pandemic }}}\uc2 , the request would boost base discretionary spending by 8.4%, or $118 billion, from the $1.4 trillion authorized in fiscal 2021.\par \par \uc2 The document is a statement of the administration's priorities, with much haggling and input from Congress to come. The budget lays out how the president's plans for spending over the next decade on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-set-to-unveil-2-trillion-infrastructure-plan-11617181208"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 infrastructure }}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/whats-in-bidens-american-families-plan-proposal-11619621174"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 social programs, such as paid family leave and universal preschool }}}\uc2 , will affect federal debt and deficits.\par \par \uc2 Here is what is in the Biden administration's budget:\par \par \uc2 Taxes\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden would sharply raise taxes on corporations and high-income households to pay for his plans. The corporate tax rate would rise to 28% from 21%, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-budget-said-to-assume-capital-gains-tax-rate-increase-started-in-late-april-11622127432"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the top capital-gains rate would rise to 43.4% from 23.8% }}}\uc2  and unrealized capital gains would be taxed at death with a $1 million per-person exemption. The administration is also proposing an expansion of the \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2  that would double its staffing over a decade and is projected to yield $700 billion in net savings from tougher tax enforcement.\par \par \uc2 The plan includes some tax cuts, such as an extension of the expanded child tax credit through 2025 and permanent extensions of expanded tax credits for health insurance, low-income workers and parents who pay for child care. It is silent on individual tax breaks that are scheduled to lapse at the end of 2025, including the larger standard deduction. And it is silent on the $10,000 cap on the state and local tax deduction, a limit opposed by many Democrats.\par \par \uc2 The budget outlines the tax-increase proposals the administration has already described, and it excludes other items\u8212\'20\'14such as changes to estate taxes, payroll taxes and a deduction for closely held businesses\u8212\'20\'14that were part of the 2020 campaign but not part of the first wave of administration plans.\par \par \uc2 For the first time, the budget attaches official revenue estimates to some of the individual pieces. Limits on deductions for companies based in countries without minimum taxes would raise $390 billion over a decade, for example. The capital-gains changes would yield $322 billion.\par \par \uc2 Health\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden's budget requests almost $132 billion for the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2 , a $25 billion increase from fiscal 2021 that reflects priorities such as more spending on pandemic preparedness and programs to reduce racial disparities in healthcare.\par \par \uc2 The increase in the discretionary budget includes spending to enhance the U.S. Strategic National Stockpile to prepare for future pandemics, biomedical research and funding on programs to reduce maternal mortality.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden is calling for empowering Medicare to negotiate for lower drug prices. In the budget request, he calls for adding dental, vision and hearing coverage to the health program for seniors and lowering the age of eligibility for Medicare from 65 to 60. He also supports a public option, which would be a government-run health insurance option that competes with private insurers, and a permanent expansion of subsidies to help reduce premiums for Affordable Care Act health plans.\par \par \uc2 The budget request would give $8.7 billion to the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 , the biggest bump in nearly 20 years, and provide more than $200 million largely aimed at reducing maternal mortality and morbidity.\par \par \uc2 The president wants $10.7 billion, an increase of $3.9 billion over 2021 enacted levels, for combating the opioid crisis. The budget also requests $1.6 billion, more than double the 2021 enacted level, to expand access to mental-health care, and a boost of $2.2 billion to the \uc2 Indian Health Service\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden is asking for $6.5 billion to start a new agency called the Advanced Research Projects Agency for Health. The sum represents a big portion of the approximately $9 billion requested budget increase, to $51 billion, for the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  would get a requested budget amount of $6.5 billion in fiscal 2022, up from the year-ago budget of $6.05 billion. Much of the \uc2 FDA\uc2 's funding comes not from tax revenue but from user fees paid by industries such as those that produce pharmaceuticals, medical devices and tobacco.\par \par \uc2 Abortion\par \par \uc2 The budget proposal doesn't include a ban on using federal dollars for abortions in most cases, known as the Hyde Amendment.\par \par \uc2 The 1976 Hyde Amendment bars federal funding for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with exceptions for victims of rape or incest or if the mother's life is in danger. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents say it ensures federal tax dollars don't go to paying for the procedure, while supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights say it disproportionately affects low-income women and women of color who are Medicaid recipients.\par \par \uc2 Removing the language was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/bidens-budget-to-spotlight-battle-over-abortion-restrictions-11622118882"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sought by Democrats and \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 -rights advocates }}}\uc2 , but it will face headwinds in Congress, where most Republicans are opposed. Mr. Biden supported the Hyde Amendment for years before {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-reverses-stance-on-hyde-amendment-citing-roe-v-wade-11559871101"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reversing his position }}}\uc2  during his presidential campaign.\par \par \uc2 Energy/Environment\par \par \uc2 The proposed budget calls for more than $36 billion in investments to combat climate change. It deepens his pitch for how the U.S. can turn {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-to-urge-climate-action-at-world-leaders-summit-11619085614"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 global emissions-reductions efforts }}}\uc2  into an economic opportunity that creates jobs for researchers, inventors and blue-collar workers in charge of building the infrastructure needed for a cleaner energy sector.\par \par \uc2 The budget proposal links clean-energy spending to job growth, estimating $2 billion for welders, electricians and other workers on clean-energy projects. Another $580 million would create 250,000 jobs to clean up abandoned mines and drilling wells, the proposal said.\par \par \uc2 A chunk of $171 billion in federal research and development money would focus on technologies that could steer U.S. power needs away from fossil fuels and help the country's energy, transportation and industrial sectors reach net zero carbon emissions by 2050. More than $10 billion\u8212\'20\'14a nearly 30 percent increase over last year's budget\u8212\'20\'14would fund clean-energy innovation across nondefense agencies. Part of that would upgrade the Energy Department's national labs to build out climate and clean-energy research programs.\par \par \uc2 The White House proposed to restore capacity at the \uc2 U.S. Environmental Protection Agency\uc2 , the primary agency for climate-change regulations that has strained under budget pressures. The agency is slated to get $11.2 billion, a 21% increase from last year.\par \par \uc2 The budget also boosts spending for traditional programs, including water infrastructure improvements and cleanup money for contaminated neighborhoods, in line with President Biden's environmental justice push to aid historically disadvantaged communities.\par \par \uc2 Defense\par \par \uc2 The administration outlined a fiscal year 2022 budget of $715 billion for the Pentagon. Officials said the budget reflected Mr. Biden's shifting priorities toward confronting China and Russia and reducing emphasis on what Defense Secretary Lloyd Austin calls the "old wars" in places such as Afghanistan and in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 Officials said the proposed budget, a 1.6% increase over last year, will fund the administration's focus on near-peer competitors, as well as modernization, investments in service members and addressing climate change. The Army took a cut of about $5 billion, while the Air Force and the Navy, which includes the Marine Corps, saw slight increases to help those services prepare to confront China.\par \par \uc2 The Army's cuts stem in part from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/u-s-aircraft-carrier-leaving-asia-to-help-with-afghanistan-troop-withdrawal-11622034089"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the drawdown of all American troops }}}\uc2 from Afghanistan, which is expected to be complete by early summer.\par \par \uc2 Budget officials noted that for the first time in 20 years, the budget pays for war-operations costs, which since 2001 have been paid for through a separate account, in the base budget. They described the change as a bureaucratic signal that the U.S. role in conflicts in Iraq, Afghanistan and elsewhere are over.\par \par \uc2 Instead, the budget earmarks $5.1 billion for the Pacific Deterrence Initiative, or PDI, which dedicates a funding stream for operations and other requirements in Pacific Asia, much of which will be used to fund platforms and operations in the region against China.\par \par \uc2 Immigration\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden set a goal of resettling up to 125,000 refugees in 2022, up from President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's cap of 15,000 for the current fiscal year. The $4.3 billion request for refugee services includes a goal of providing better services for unaccompanied minors and expediting the process of uniting them with relatives and removing them from U.S. custody.\par \par \uc2 The request includes money for border security modernization to screen for human, drug and weapons trafficking. It calls for no new funds for a border wall and requests the cancellation of any unused funds for the wall, but requests $861 million to increase security and economic support for Central American nations. It also calls for $891 million to hire more immigration judges and support staff to work through court backlogs.\par \par \uc2 Transportation\par \par \uc2 Many of the transit provisions in the budget are proposals from Mr. Biden's American Jobs Plan, including funds for upgrading 20,000 miles of highways and streets and a focus on repairing the 10 most economically significant bridges, many of which require reconstruction. It also will repair the worst 10,000 smaller bridges, providing critical linkages to communities.\par \par \uc2 It also provides funds for replacing buses and railcars, electrifying the federal fleet of vehicles\u8212\'20\'14including Postal Service vehicles\u8212\'20\'14and would extend and enhance tax credits for electric vehicles. Mr. Biden's budget also calls for a 35% increase in \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  funding and a new competitive grant program for local passenger rail.\par \par \uc2 Education\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden's budget spells out his plans to have the government make four additional years of schooling\u8212\'20\'14including preschool and two years of community college\u8212\'20\'14free to families, with taxpayers covering the cost. It would also substantially boost federal funding for primary and secondary schools in high-poverty areas\u8212\'20\'14so-called Title 1 schools.\par \par \uc2 The budget would give grants to states to provide universal preschool to all 3- and 4-year-olds, as well as funding to ensure workers at the schools earn $15 an hour. Those two initiatives would cost $3.5 billion in the fiscal year through September 2022. Providing free community college would cost about $19 billion in the fiscal year. Those funds would go to states that agreed to put up matching funds\u8212\'20\'14$1 for every $3 the federal government contributed\u8212\'20\'14to cover tuition at community college.\par \par \uc2 The budget also calls for expanding a program that provides cash grants to students from poor and modest-income families to cover college tuition\u8212\'20\'14including at four-year schools\u8212\'20\'14and living expenses while in school. Under the proposal, eligible students would receive Pell grants of up to $8,370 a year\u8212\'20\'14some would receive less, depending on their incomes. That would be $1,875 above the current maximum award. The grants, unlike student loans, don't have to be repaid. The budget would also boost funding to colleges that serve high shares of Black, Hispanic, and Native American students.\par \par \uc2 Agriculture\par \par \uc2 The Agriculture Department would see a 16% funding increase, with significant additional funding for priorities like rural broadband, improving rural water infrastructure and improving forest management to prevent forest fires.\par \par \uc2 The budget would also allocate more money toward developing agricultural technologies to help farmers boost their output while also pursuing conservation initiatives and sequestering carbon on their land. The budget includes $300 million in new conservation funds, including support for voluntary private lands conservation.\par \par \uc2 A major part of the Agriculture Department's budget goes to nutrition programs: Food stamps would receive $6.7 billion under Mr. Biden's request, a $1 billion increase from this year's enacted level.\par \par \uc2 Justice Department\par \par \uc2 Following last year's killing of George Floyd at the hands of Minneapolis police, the Justice Department under the Biden administration has made a priority of improving relationships between police and the communities they serve. The budget request asks for a 5.6% increase for the agency, including new funding to promote alternatives to prison and to support community programs to try to prevent violence before it happens.\par \par \uc2 It includes around $400 million in new funds to improve relationships between law enforcement and the communities they work in; nearly $700 million for efforts to reform the criminal-justice system; and nearly $500 million in new funding for the agency's office of violence against women. Much of that proposed funding would be used to provide grants to outside groups; the request translates to a 44% increase in the amount of funding for such grants. The budget request comes as the Justice Department and local law-enforcement agencies are anticipating increased violence this summer.\par \par \uc2 The budget also asks for another $100 million to fight domestic terrorism after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/riot-at-the-u-s-capitol-11610068066"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Jan. 6 riot at the U.S. Capitol }}}\uc2 , with most of the resources requested to be split between the \uc2 Federal Bureau of Investigation\uc2  and the U.S. attorney's offices around the country. It also asked for an additional $4 million to research the root causes of domestic terrorism.\par \par \uc2 Cybersecurity\par \par \uc2 The budget requests a boost in cybersecurity funding the administration says is necessary to counter challenges posed by Russia and China.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency\uc2  would get an additional $110 million, bringing its funding to $2.1 billion. The bill provides $20 million for a new Cyber Response and Recovery fund.\par \par \uc2 The blueprint also proposes $500 million for the Technology Modernization Fund "to address urgent IT modernization challenges, bolster cybersecurity defenses, and improve the delivery of Covid-19 pandemic relief," and $750 million to agencies "affected by recent, significant cyber incidents," such as the \uc2 SolarWinds\uc2  hack, to address gaps in security capabilities.\par \par \uc2 Officials say cyberattacks pose a growing threat to national security and public safety. Mr. Biden signed an executive order in March that sought to reorient the federal government's approach to cybersecurity around prevention rather than crisis response.\par \par \uc2 Space\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Aeronautics and Space Administration\uc2  would get a 6.3% funding bump to support human and robotic exploration of the moon and Mars. Mr. Biden also calls for additional funding for aeronautics research and climate science, including beefing up climate satellite monitoring programs.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/whats-in-bidens-6-trillion-budget-plan-11622223706"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 What's in Biden's $6 Trillion Budget Plan }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020210528eh5s004s9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
