\par\par \uc2 Commentary (U.S.)\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Democracy Is the Issue in the Mississippi  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Case; The high court could put questions about gestational limits back into voters' hands\u8212\'20\'14where they belong.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Clarke D. Forsythe \par \uc2 932 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 May 2021\par 16:48 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court announced Monday that it will hear Mississippi's defense of its limit on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks of pregnancy. However the high court rules, it's unlikely to satisfy activists on either side. But a large majority of Americans would likely support a decision upholding the law.\par \par \uc2 Mississippi's law prohibits \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  when "the probable gestational age of the unborn human being" is "greater than" 15 weeks, "except in a medical emergency or in the case of a severe fetal abnormality," defined as a condition that is "incompatible with life outside the womb." As in previous \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases, the law was challenged as unconstitutional in toto by an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic, Jackson Women's Health Organization.\par \par \uc2 The state's limit has broad support nationally. As one researcher {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://digitalcommons.law.uga.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=2056&context=fac_artchop"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 found}}}\uc2 , "Polls stretching back for decades show that two-thirds or more of the public believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should generally be illegal in the second trimester." The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/1576/abortion.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most recent}}}\uc2  Gallup poll on the matter in 2018 found that 65% thought \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be illegal in the second trimester (after 12 weeks). A January 2020 Marist Poll found 7 in 10 Americans support limiting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the first trimester.\par \par \uc2 There is a notion\u8212\'20\'14repeated throughout the media and implied sometimes even by Supreme Court justices\u8212\'20\'14that Americans are too "polarized" to decide the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue through the democratic process and need the court to do it for them. The Mississippi case and polling on gestational limits obviously demonstrates that there is copious middle ground. The position of activists at both ends of the spectrum shouldn't obscure the broad public agreement on moderate limits, and the potential for legislators to write reasonable laws.\par \par \uc2 Mississippi hasn't asked the Supreme Court to overturn Planned Parenthood v. Casey or Roe v. Wade. Instead, the justices will consider "whether all pre-viability prohibitions on elective abortions are unconstitutional," a modest question. This is one the high court has never addressed directly, though it has bypassed it more than once. The Supreme Court created its "viability rule" in Roe\u8212\'20\'14though it was dictum, a point not necessary to its decision. And it merely repeated the rule in Casey, also dictum, based on the casual assertion that states have two interests in limiting abortions\u8212\'20\'14in prenatal lives and maternal health\u8212\'20\'14which are "not strong enough" before fetal viability to justify a broad limitation.\par \par \uc2 Mississippi is challenging those factual assumptions by pointing to evolving understanding of fetal development through ultrasonography, enhanced state legal protection in tort and criminal law for prenatal injury and contemporary data on the medical risks to women from late-term abortions. A well-regarded 2004 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://journals.lww.com/greenjournal/Fulltext/2004/04000/Risk_Factors_for_Legal_Induced_Abortion_Related.20.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 study}}}\uc2 \u8212\'20\'14which Mississippi cites in its legislative findings\u8212\'20\'14found that the risk of maternal mortality increases considerably for late-term abortions. Specifically, "compared with women whose abortions were performed at or before 8 weeks of gestation, women whose abortions were performed in the second trimester were significantly more likely to die of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related causes."\par \par \uc2 Many Americans might be surprised to know that since Roe in 1973, the Supreme Court hasn't addressed an actual \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  prohibition that applied before fetal viability. (The Congressional ban on partial-birth \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which the Court upheld in 2007, prohibited one very narrowly defined \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedure.) Many scholars on both sides of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue agree that the high court has never given an adequate rationale for its viability rule.\par \par \uc2 Mississippi's law also spotlights the embarrassing disparity between U.S. and international law on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A 2011 United Nations {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.un.org/development/desa/pd/sites/www.un.org.development.desa.pd/files/files/documents/2020/Jan/un_2011_worldabortionpolicieswallchart.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 survey}}}\uc2  and other studies show that America\u8212\'20\'14because of Roe\u8212\'20\'14is one of only seven nations, of some 198 across the globe, that allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for any reason after 20 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Mississippi's law reflects the diversity of approaches that the states have taken on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent years. Delaware, Hawaii, Illinois, Maine, Massachusetts, New Mexico, New York, Rhode Island and Vermont have passed laws to codify Roe. These would keep \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal for nearly any reason, at any time of pregnancy, as the Supreme Court through Roe has dictated for 48 years. Six other states had previously passed similar laws.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, some 13 states in recent years, including Mississippi, have passed strong limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Missouri, North Dakota, South Dakota, Tennessee and Utah have also passed prohibitions of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  conditioned on the overruling of Roe. None is enforceable now due to injunctions by federal or state courts. Virtually all exclude the women seeking abortions from any legal penalties\u8212\'20\'14Mississippi's are limited to the "physician"\u8212\'20\'14as state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy generally did for nearly a century before Roe.\par \par \uc2 Like it or not, this diversity is democracy at work. These varied laws embody public opinion much better than the sweeping edict in Roe that put the court at the center of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics. Legislators are accountable to the people for their votes, unlike judges. There is democratic legitimacy in laws passed by elected representatives, reflecting the consent of the governed.\par \par \uc2 If the court loosens its grip on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics, the states have shown that they are ready and able to address the issue in ways that reflect Americans' varying viewpoints, grounded in the science of fetal development and maternal health.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Forsythe is senior counsel at \uc2 Americans United for Life\uc2  and author of "Abuse of Discretion: The Inside Story of Roe v. Wade."\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/democracy-is-the-issue-in-the-mississippi-abortion-case-11621442926"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democracy Is the Issue in the Mississippi \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Case}}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020210519eh5j0038p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
