\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  to Review Mississippi Law Limiting  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights; High court will take up state appeal seeking to ban the procedure after 15 weeks of pregnancy, giving justices opportunity to revisit Roe v. Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brent Kendall and Jess Bravin \par \uc2 1,670 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2021\par 22:37 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14The \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  said it would consider the legality of a Mississippi \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law that sought to ban the procedure after 15 weeks of pregnancy, a case that gives the justices {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/the-mississippi-abortion-case-at-the-supreme-court-what-you-should-know-11621337351"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an opportunity to revisit precedents  }}}\uc2  protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The court's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/search.aspx?filename=/docket/docketfiles/html/public/19-1392.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one-sentence order  }}}\uc2  on Monday, coming after eight months of deliberation, crystallized hopes and fears of partisans who have battled for decades over the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue and the direction of the high court. Republicans have long sought to build a \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  with enough conservative justices to narrow, if not abandon, precedents dating back to the landmark 1973 decision in Roe v. Wade that established a woman's constitutional right to choose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before fetal viability. Democrats have made the preservation of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights a central plank of their opposition to dozens of Republican nominees to the federal judiciary, warning that Roe and its progeny were under threat.\par \par \uc2 What You Should Know\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/the-mississippi-abortion-case-at-the-supreme-court-what-you-should-know-11621337351"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 A Q&A about the Mississippi \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  case at the Supreme Court and its implications.  }}}\par \par \uc2 "The fact that the justices decided to take the case indicates a willingness of at least five of them to revisit existing precedent," said \uc2 University of Chicago\uc2  law professor Geoffrey Stone, who has written extensively about the history of the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rulings. "The only reason to hear the case would be to do that."\par \par \uc2 The court will consider the case during its next term, which begins in October.\par \par \uc2 If Mississippi wins, the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  would be allowing states more room to regulate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than at any time since Roe. The court wouldn't have to explicitly overturn Roe and other precedents, but any ruling for the state would put significant limits on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, Mr. Stone said.\par \par \uc2 Mississippi's only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic, the Jackson Women's Health Organization, challenged the state's 2018 law as unconstitutional. Two lower courts agreed, saying an unbroken line of \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  rulings dating back to Roe established that states can regulate\u8212\'20\'14but not outright ban\u8212\'20\'14the procedure in the pre-viability months of pregnancy. The state appealed to the high court in a bid to save the law, which isn't in effect because of the lower-court rulings.\par \par \uc2 Mississippi's law would allow abortions after 15 weeks only in cases of medical emergency or severe fetal abnormality. The state argues that fetal viability\u8212\'20\'14defined by the Roe court as the capability of meaningful life outside the mother's womb\u8212\'20\'14shouldn't be the standard for allowing states to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying lawmakers had legitimate interests in protecting fetal life from the outset. By 15 weeks, the human fetus has made important physiological developments and abortions are riskier to the mother, the state says.\par \par \uc2 In a tweet, Republican Mississippi Attorney General Lynn Fitch, who filed the appeal, said the state law aimed "to promote women's health and preserve the dignity and sanctity of life. I remain committed to advocating for women and defending Mississippi's legal right to protect the unborn," she said.\par \par \uc2 The high court's decision to consider the Mississippi ban "is an ominous sign and an alarming reminder that the threat to the legal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is imminent and real," said Christian LoBue, a representative of Naral Pro-Choice America, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights group. "If Roe v. Wade were to fall as a result of this case, states across the country are poised to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The impact would be devastating, especially on those who already face the greatest barriers to care."\par \par \uc2 Mississippi is one of several Republican-led states that have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/graphics/state-of-abortion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed more aggressive restrictions  }}}\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent years, including so-called fetal-heartbeat laws that would ban the procedure as early as six weeks into pregnancy. Those states have been more willing to test the legal waters since the 2018 retirement of Justice \uc2 Anthony Kennedy\uc2  , a maverick conservative who for years was the court's pivotal vote protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The court shifted further to the right last year after the death of Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  , a leading member of the court's liberal wing who viewed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights as essential to women's equality. She was succeeded by Justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  , a conservative who personally opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and whose legal writings {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/what-trump-pick-amy-coney-barrett-could-mean-for-future-of-the-supreme-court-11601155192"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 suggest she is skeptical of the current scope of \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights  }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 University of Notre Dame\uc2  law professor Carter Snead, a member of University Faculty for Life, said he hoped the court would "finally end its failed and constitutionally unjustified experiment as the nation's ad hoc \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulatory body of last resort."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Snead, a former faculty colleague of Justice Barrett who publicly advocated for her confirmation, said the court should renounce Roe and other precedents that have "undermined the rule of law, broken our electoral politics and resulted in a staggering number of lives lost."\par \par \uc2 The Jackson clinic had urged the \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  not to hear the case and instead leave the lower-court rulings in place. The state's ban extends well before viability and contravened the fundamental tenet of the high court's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights precedents, the clinic argues.\par \par \uc2 "As the only \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic left in Mississippi, we see patients who have spent weeks saving up the money to travel here and pay for child care, for a place to stay and everything else involved," said Diane Derzis, the facility's owner. "If this ban were to take effect, we would be forced to turn many of those patients away, and they would lose their right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in this state."\par \par \uc2 Nearly 90% of abortions take place in the first 12 weeks of pregnancy, according to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2  , a research group supportive of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The court has largely upheld \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights since the Roe decision but typically by the narrowest of margins. That record puts the focus on the three justices appointed since 2017 by President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , who made conservative judicial nominations a priority during his administration.\par \par \uc2 When asked during a 2016 presidential debate whether he wanted to see Roe v. Wade reversed, Mr. Trump said: "If we put another two or perhaps three justices on, that will happen, and that'll happen automatically, in my opinion, because I am putting pro-life justices on the court."\par \par \uc2 President Biden made support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights part of his campaign platform last year. On Monday, White House press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  declined to comment on the Mississippi case, but said the president remains "committed to codifying Roe" in federal law rather than relying on shifting \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  majorities.\par \par \uc2 Only three justices on the current court\u8212\'20\'14\uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  , \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  and \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2  , all appointed by Democratic presidents\u8212\'20\'14have embraced a broad view of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Conservative Justices \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  have consistently voted to uphold \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions. Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  voted last year to strike down a Louisiana \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulation, but couched his views as adherence to precedent rather than agreement with the prior decision's fundamental reasoning.\par \par \uc2 In that case, from last June, a 5-4 court struck down a law requiring that any doctor who provides abortions obtain admitting privileges to a local hospital. Chief Justice Roberts joined four liberal justices, including the late Justice Ginsburg, to hold the law unconstitutional, citing a 2016 decision in which the high court struck down a similar Texas law that the court found imposed an undue burden on women while providing no health benefits.\par \par \uc2 Justices \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  and \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  , Mr. Trump's first two appointees, dissented in the Louisiana case along with Justices Thomas and Alito. Justice Ginsburg, died three months later and was succeeded by Justice Barrett, who was confirmed in October.\par \par \uc2 The Mississippi case touches a sharp point of the nation's political and cultural divide and comes as the makeup of the court itself has become a deeply partisan issue.\par \par \uc2 Some Democrats, furious at what they consider hardball moves by Republicans to secure a conservative majority by blocking President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  from filling a vacancy in 2016 and then confirming a Trump nominee days before last year's presidential election, have floated various proposals {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/group-of-democratic-lawmakers-to-present-plan-to-expand-supreme-court-11618447336"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to temper the rightward shift  }}}\uc2 , including by adding seats that President Biden could fill.\par \par \uc2 The president, who hasn't endorsed any particular plan, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-to-sign-executive-order-creating-commission-on-supreme-court-changes-11617983199"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 appointed a blue-ribbon commission  }}}\uc2  to study potential changes to the court. The panel is set to hold its first meeting on Wednesday and deliver a report in six months.\par \par \uc2 Although 1973's Roe v. Wade remains the best-known \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case, the court's current legal framework for evaluating restrictions on the procedure stems from a 1992 decision, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  v. Casey. The joint opinion by Republican-appointed Justices Kennedy, \uc2 Sandra Day O'Connor\uc2  and David Souter sought a compromise that narrowed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights without foreclosing the procedure altogether.\par \par \uc2 The Casey decision emphasized the state's interest in protecting "the potential life within the woman," holding that government could impose restrictions that might discourage abortions, so long as they didn't amount to an "undue burden" on women who decide to end their pregnancies.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Rights\par \par \uc2 More WSJ coverage on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, selected by the editors\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/the-mississippi-abortion-case-at-the-supreme-court-what-you-should-know-11621337351"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Mississippi \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Case at the Supreme Court: What You Should Know  }}}\uc2  (May 18)\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-to-decide-if-kentucky-attorney-general-can-maintain-abortion-law-defense-11617035582"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court to Decide if Kentucky Attorney General Can Maintain \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22 -Law Defense  }}}\uc2  (March 29)\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/arkansas-governor-signs-bill-that-bans-most-abortions-11615330493"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Arkansas Governor Signs Bill That Bans Most Abortions  }}}\uc2  (March 9)\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/south-carolina-passes-fetal-heartbeat-abortion-legislation-11613599308"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 South Carolina Passes 'Fetal Heartbeat' \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Legislation  }}}\uc2  (Feb. 17)\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-strikes-down-louisiana-abortion-law-11593440418"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Louisiana \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Law Struck Down By Supreme Court  }}}\uc2  (June 29, 2020)\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/graphics/state-of-abortion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The State of \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22 : How New Laws Could Affect Access  }}}\uc2  (Aug. 6, 2019)\par \par \uc2 Write to Brent Kendall at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:brent.kendall@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 brent.kendall@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Jess Bravin at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jess.bravin+1@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jess.bravin+1@wsj.com  }}}\uc2  and Jess Bravin at\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-to-consider-abortion-restrictions-from-mississippi-11621259099"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court to Review Mississippi Law Limiting \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Rights  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020210517eh5h0025t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
