\par\par \uc2 Health Policy\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Biden Administration Proposes Reversing Ban on Federal Funds for Clinics That Refer Abortions; Trump-era rule led Planned Parenthood to withdraw from the program known as Title X\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michelle Hackman \par \uc2 601 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 April 2021\par 17:55 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14The Biden administration took initial steps to undo a Trump administration policy that stripped federal family-planning dollars from clinics that refer patients for abortions, a move that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/planned-parenthood-to-withdraw-from-title-x-funding-program-over-abortion-restrictions-11566242931"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 drove Planned Parenthood to withdraw from the program }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  on Wednesday proposed a rule that, when finalized, would reverse the policy on the federal program, known as Title X. The program offers about $286 million each year to clinics that provide reproductive healthcare and other women's health screenings, such as Pap smears, to about four million uninsured Americans a year.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration rule, enacted in 2019, called for clinics that received federal funding to physically separate their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services in a separate building from all other services, and not to discuss abortions as an option with patients.\par \par \uc2 The change was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/trump-administration-moves-on-abortion-cheer-antiabortion-movement-1505505209"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one of a series of steps }}}\uc2  the Trump administration took to cut funds for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, a top concern for religious conservatives, who were a key part of former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's base and who shaped many aspects of his healthcare policy.\par \par \uc2 Federal dollars have long been prohibited by law from directly funding abortions, though advocates who oppose the procedure say funding to providers like Planned Parenthood, even for other services, essentially subsidizes them and makes it easier for Planned Parenthood and other clinics to provide abortions.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters criticized the Trump administration policy, calling it a "gag rule" because it prevented doctors and other healthcare professionals from presenting patients with all available family-planning options.\par \par \uc2 These critics said that by pushing Planned Parenthood clinics out of the federal network where uninsured women could receive family-planning services, the Trump administration also cut off access for women in many underserved communities where Planned Parenthood is the only provider offering birth control and other women's healthcare.\par \par \uc2 At the time of the change, Planned Parenthood clinics served about 40% of the program's overall patients and received about $60 million in annual funding.\par \par \uc2 "Women should be able to make informed decisions about their healthcare, including their reproductive healthcare decisions, without government interference," said Jamille Fields Allsbrook, director of women's health and rights at the \uc2 Center for American Progress\uc2 , a liberal Washington think tank.\par \par \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  List, a national antiabortion group, criticized the plan to roll back the rule, saying the Trump administration's decision was consistent with the statutory language of Title X and supported by Americans who oppose using taxpayer dollars to fund \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not 'family planning' and Biden-Harris Democrats pursue this extreme, unpopular agenda at their political peril," said SBA List President Marjorie Dannenfelser.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration also worked to reshape the family-planning program to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/u-s-cuts-funding-for-title-x-family-planning-program-1519433036"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 include faith-based clinics }}}\uc2  that discourage the use of contraception and advocate abstinence or "natural" birth-control methods. In 2019, for example, a Christian organization known as Obria that doesn't offer any forms of contraception for the first time received a $1.7 million grant from Title X.\par \par \uc2 Women's health advocates had hoped the Biden administration would move more quickly to undo the Trump administration's rules, since a formal rule change could take months or longer. But the slower approach could fortify the administration against lawsuits saying it violated federal policy-making rules, which brought down many quickly enacted Trump administration policies.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/biden-administration-proposes-reversing-ban-on-federal-funds-for-clinics-that-refer-abortions-11618422917"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Biden Administration Proposes Reversing Ban on Federal Funds for Clinics That Refer Abortions  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020210414eh4e0063h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
