\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Federal Appeals Court Upholds Ohio's Down Syndrome  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Law; Ohio law prohibits doctors from performing an  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  once a patient reveals she is motivated by fears the fetus has Down syndrome\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jacob Gershman \par \uc2 915 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 April 2021\par 21:27 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A splintered federal appeals court on Tuesday {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.opn.ca6.uscourts.gov/opinions.pdf/21a0083p-06.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 upheld an Ohio law }}}\uc2  that makes it illegal for a physician to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  upon learning that the patient wants the procedure because she fears the fetus has Down syndrome.\par \par \uc2 In a 9-7 vote, the Sixth U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals in Cincinnati allowed Ohio to enforce the law, reversing an earlier ruling by a smaller panel of the court.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://search-prod.lis.state.oh.us/solarapi/v1/general_assembly_132/bills/hb214/EN/05?format=pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The statute }}}\uc2  doesn't prevent women from terminating a fetus diagnosed with Down syndrome. But it prevents a physician from performing the procedure if the pregnant woman tells the doctor she is seeking the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because she has reason to believe the fetus has the genetic disorder.\par \par \uc2 The dispute over the law expands a relatively new front in the legal battle over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Supreme Court has never reviewed the constitutionality of restraints on abortions performed because of predicted characteristics of the fetus, such as disability, race or gender. Tuesday's ruling marks the first time a federal appellate court has held that a statute like Ohio's doesn't violate the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Republican legislators voted for the law, known as H.B. 214, in 2017. Before it could take effect, a federal judge barred the state from enforcing it while the court heard a lawsuit challenging it.\par \par \uc2 A three-judge panel of the Sixth Circuit upheld that injunction in 2019.\par \par \uc2 The court, whose composition swayed rightward under President Trump, then {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/appeals-court-reviews-ohio-law-restricting-down-syndrome-abortions-11583938782"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 agreed to rehear the case }}}\uc2  before the full bench of active judges.\par \par \uc2 In the opinion, Circuit Judge Alice Batchelder, a George H.W. Bush appointee, wrote that Ohio had legitimate reasons for enacting the law that outweighed the "minor burdens" on a woman's protected right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 She wrote that Ohio had an interest in both protecting the Down syndrome community from the stigma associated with Down-syndrome-selective abortions and "protecting pregnant women and their families from coercion by doctors who advocate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Down-syndrome-afflicted fetuses."\par \par \uc2 A woman whose doctor is disqualified from performing the procedure would still be free to secure an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from another doctor who is unaware of her motivations, she wrote.\par \par \uc2 Several dissents accompanied the opinion, including from the court's chief judge, R. Guy Cole, Jr., who was appointed by President Clinton.\par \par \uc2 "In its haste to reconcile the law with a woman's right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the majority turns H.B. 214 into a don't ask, don't tell law," he wrote. "So long as doctors don't ask and women don't tell, the majority reassures us that women remain free to exercise their constitutional rights."\par \par \uc2 Antiabortion conservatives, whose legal movement has made little headway in the courts in recent years, said the decision was a breakthrough.\par \par \uc2 James Bopp, general counsel for the antiabortion \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2  organization, said the ruling "debunks the idea that Roe v. Wade is absolute at least prior to viability and that there are no other potential interests that the state can assert to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Roe found that before viability, a state's interest in protecting unborn life isn't enough of a compelling interest to justify banning the procedure. Ohio argued that the law is advancing a different compelling interest: protecting the Down-syndrome population from discrimination and elimination.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights proponents have assailed the statute and others likes it as an unconstitutional limit on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that would chill open communication between doctors and their pregnant patients.\par \par \uc2 "Today's ruling is an affront on the important, trusting relationship between healthcare providers and the individuals and families we serve," said Chrisse France, executive director of Preterm, a Cleveland \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic and plaintiff in the lawsuit. "The Sixth Circuit allowed politicians to exploit the real needs and concerns of people with Down syndrome to push their antiabortion agenda."\par \par \uc2 Around a dozen states have active laws on the books restricting abortions based on one or more fetal characteristics: the sex or race of the fetus or because of a genetic anomaly, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/state-policy/explore/abortion-bans-cases-sex-or-race-selection-or-genetic-anomaly"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to the Guttmacher Institute }}}\uc2 , a research group that promotes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Some of those statutes haven't faced judicial scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 Most of the laws restricting abortions based on genetic anomaly aren't currently enforced, according to the institute.\par \par \uc2 In 2018, the Seventh U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals in Chicago struck down a provision of a 2016 Indiana statute\u8212\'20\'14signed into law by then Gov. Mike Pence\u8212\'20\'14that restricted abortions solely motivated by the fetus's race, sex or disability. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/supreme-court-wont-reinstate-indiana-ban-on-abortion-for-sex-selection-11559053382"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Supreme Court in 2019 let stand }}}\uc2  a lower-court ruling blocking the Indiana law without making any pronouncements about its constitutionality.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and other conservatives have raised the specter of a new eugenics movement, likening selective \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the culling of undesirable populations advanced by social reformers in the early 1900s.\par \par \uc2 In a dissenting opinion Tuesday, Circuit Judge Bernice B. Donald, a President Obama appointee, bristled at the comparisons to past eugenics.\par \par \uc2 "Invocations of the history of eugenics are irrelevant and unduly simplify and distort the history of the eugenics movement and its relation to the \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement," she wrote.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jacob Gershman at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jacob.gershman@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jacob.gershman@wsj.com }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/federal-appeals-court-upholds-ohios-down-syndrome-abortion-law-11618348943"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Federal Appeals Court Upholds Ohio's Down Syndrome \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Law  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020210413eh4d006hd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
