\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Tennessee Asks Supreme Court to Reinstate 48-Hour Waiting Period for Abortions; State asks justices to enforce restriction while it appeals lower court order that found it unconstitutional\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jess Bravin \par \uc2 536 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 April 2021\par 00:48 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON\u8212\'20\'14The state of Tennessee asked the Supreme Court Monday to reinstate a 48-hour waiting period imposed on women seeking abortions, after lower courts found it unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 Tennessee asked the high court to reimpose the restriction while it appeals a 136-page opinion from a federal judge in Nashville, who found that the restriction imposed an undue burden on women's right to end pregnancy, the standard the Supreme Court applies under a 1992 precedent known as Planned Parenthood v. Casey.\par \par \uc2 "Although fourteen other States have similar waiting-period laws that generally require two trips to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider, Tennessee is the only State in the Nation that cannot enforce its law because of a federal judicial decree," Tennessee said in its filing.\par \par \uc2 Last October, U.S. District Judge Bernard Friedman found the waiting period "burdens the majority of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients with significant, and often insurmountable, logistical and financial hurdles" because it requires two visits to the clinic. Most women in the state who seek abortions are poor, and often must travel significant distances to reach one of the four Tennessee clinics, he found. Taking time off from work, arranging child care and other necessary steps can make obtaining the procedure prohibitive, Judge Friedman found.\par \par \uc2 Tennessee's filing said that the waiting period had been in effect for some five years before Judge Friedman's ruling, during which time the state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate "declined only slightly" and remained above 10,000 procedures annually.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the state argued that the district court order interfered with Tennessee's efforts to reduce the number of abortions. "As long as the injunction remains in place, some unborn children will be aborted who might otherwise be spared that fate. Some women will choose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without making an informed and deliberate decision, and some will later come to regret that irreversible decision," the state's brief argued.\par \par \uc2 In February, a divided panel of the Sixth U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals in Cincinnati declined to block the district court order. Writing for the majority, Judge Karen Nelson Moore said Tennessee's measure "appears to be yet another unnecessary, unjustified, and unduly burdensome state law that stands between women and their right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Dissenting Appeals Court Judge Amul Thapar wrote that the Casey decision itself had upheld a waiting period in Pennsylvania. "Since Casey, no federal appellate court has successfully struck down an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  waiting period. Why? Because the Supreme Court says that waiting periods are constitutional," he wrote.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court is expected to seek a response from the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers that challenged the Tennessee restriction before considering the state's application.\par \par \uc2 The narrow Supreme Court majority that upheld \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in recent years eroded following the retirement of Justice \uc2 Anthony Kennedy\uc2  in 2018 and Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 's death last year. Their successors, Justices \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and Amy Coney Barrett, are expected to be significantly more sympathetic to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/tennessee-asks-supreme-court-to-reinstate-48-hour-waiting-period-for-abortions-11617670083"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tennessee Asks Supreme Court to Reinstate 48-Hour Waiting Period for Abortions  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020210406eh460002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
