\par\par \uc2 U.S. News\par \uc2 US\par \b \uc2 Arkansas Governor Signs Bill That Bans Most Abortions; Move follows laws in other states that are part of an effort to prompt \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  to revisit Roe v. Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kris Maher \par \uc2 457 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 March 2021\par 22:54 GMT\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal Online\par \uc2 WSJO\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Arkansas Gov. Asa Hutchinson signed legislation Tuesday banning nearly all abortions in the state, as he and other supporters said they hope it prompts the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  to revisit and ultimately overturn existing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case law.\par \par \uc2 The Arkansas measure would ban all abortions unless the procedure is necessary to save a woman's life. There are no exemptions in the law for women who become pregnant through rape or incest.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates said they would challenge the law because it violates a woman's constitutional right to terminate a pregnancy established in the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  's Roe v. Wade decision.\par \par \uc2 Gov. Hutchinson, a Republican, acknowledged that passage of the law is aimed at challenging the court's 1973 decision. "It is the intent of the legislation to set the stage for the Supreme Court overturning current case law," he said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 He said he signed the legislation because of his personal convictions, and that he would have preferred it included exemptions for rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 The ACLU of Arkansas said it would challenge the law. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal in all 50 states, including Arkansas, and we'll fight as long as it takes to keep it that way. Governor Hutchinson: we'll see you in court," the organization said on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The Arkansas legislation comes after passage of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/south-carolina-passes-fetal-heartbeat-abortion-legislation-11613599308?page=1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a law in South Carolina  }}}\uc2 last month that would have prohibited abortions after a fetal heartbeat is detected, typically after six to eight weeks. That law was suspended by a federal judge one day after it passed.\par \par \uc2 Courts have blocked measures to restrict abortions in Alabama, Mississippi and Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Last June, the Supreme Court, in a 5-4 decision, struck down a Louisiana law that required \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doctors to have admitting privileges at nearby hospitals.\par \par \uc2 More states are expected to pass \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions since the October confirmation of \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  gave the court a 6-3 conservative majority.\par \par \uc2 Separately on Tuesday, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in Ohio sued the \uc2 Ohio Department of Health\uc2  to block a state law requiring fetal remains to be cremated or buried.\par \par \uc2 The ACLU of Ohio and others who brought the lawsuit in Hamilton County Court of Common Pleas said the law, which is scheduled to take effect April 6, is impossible to follow because the state hasn't yet issued any necessary rules.\par \par \uc2 A spokeswoman for the state department of health couldn't immediately be reached for comment.\par \par \uc2 Write to Kris Maher at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:kris.maher@wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 kris.maher@wsj.com  }}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/arkansas-governor-signs-bill-that-bans-most-abortions-11615330493"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Arkansas Governor Signs Bill That Bans Most Abortions  }}}\par \par \uc2 Document WSJO000020210309eh39006hd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw254\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3810\pichgoal600
